    ns1:description """# Metagenome-Atlas\r
\r
[![Anaconda-Server Badge](https://anaconda.org/bioconda/metagenome-atlas/badges/latest_release_relative_date.svg)](https://anaconda.org/bioconda/metagenome-atlas)\r
[![Bioconda](https://img.shields.io/conda/dn/bioconda/metagenome-atlas.svg?label=Bioconda )](https://anaconda.org/bioconda/metagenome-atlas)\r
[![Documentation Status](https://readthedocs.org/projects/metagenome-atlas/badge/?version=latest)](https://metagenome-atlas.readthedocs.io/en/latest/?badge=latest)\r
![Mastodon Follow](https://img.shields.io/mastodon/follow/109273833677404282?domain=https%3A%2F%2Fmstdn.science&style=social)\r
<!--[![follow on twitter](https://img.shields.io/twitter/follow/SilasKieser.svg?style=social&label=Follow)](https://twitter.com/search?f=tweets&q=%40SilasKieser%20%23metagenomeAtlas&src=typd) -->\r
\r
\r
Metagenome-atlas is a easy-to-use metagenomic pipeline based on snakemake. It handles all steps from QC, Assembly, Binning, to Annotation.\r
\r
![scheme of workflow](resources/images/atlas_list.png?raw=true)\r
\r
You can start using atlas with three commands:\r
```\r
    mamba install -y -c bioconda -c conda-forge metagenome-atlas={latest_version}\r
    atlas init --db-dir databases path/to/fastq/files\r
    atlas run all\r
```\r
where `{latest_version}` should be replaced by [![Version](https://anaconda.org/bioconda/metagenome-atlas/badges/version.svg)](https://anaconda.org/bioconda/metagenome-atlas)\r
\r
\r
# Webpage\r
\r
[metagenome-atlas.github.io](https://metagenome-atlas.github.io/)\r
\r
# Documentation\r
\r
https://metagenome-atlas.readthedocs.io/\r
\r
[Tutorial](https://github.com/metagenome-atlas/Tutorial)\r
\r
# Citation\r
\r
> ATLAS: a Snakemake workflow for assembly, annotation, and genomic binning of metagenome sequence data.  \r
> Kieser, S., Brown, J., Zdobnov, E. M., Trajkovski, M. & McCue, L. A.   \r
> BMC Bioinformatics 21, 257 (2020).  \r
> doi: [10.1186/s12859-020-03585-4](https://doi.org/10.1186/s12859-020-03585-4)\r
\r
\r
# Developpment/Extensions\r
\r
Here are some ideas I work or want to work on when I have time. If you want to contribute or have some ideas let me know via a feature request issue.\r
\r
- Optimized MAG recovery (e.g. [Spacegraphcats](https://github.com/spacegraphcats/spacegraphcats))\r
- Integration of viruses/plasmid that live for now as [extensions](https://github.com/metagenome-atlas/virome_atlas)\r
- Add statistics and visualisations as in [atlas_analyze](https://github.com/metagenome-atlas/atlas_analyze)\r
- Implementation of most rules as snakemake wrapper\r
- Cloud execution\r
- Update to new Snakemake version and use cool reports.\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1183" ;
    ns1:license "BSD-3-Clause" ;
    ns1:name "Metaenome-Atlas" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1183?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1189?version=2> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Birgit Rijvers",
        "Dennis Dollée",
        "Helena Rasche" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2024-11-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """This is an aggregation of the work done in [Seq4AMR](https://workflowhub.eu/projects/110) consisting of the following workflows:\r
\r
- [WF1: AbritAMR / AMRFinderPlus](https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/634)\r
- [WF2: Sciensano](https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/644) (**not currently included**)\r
- [WF3: SRST2](https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/407) \r
- [WF4: StarAMR](https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/470)\r
\r
## Installation\r
\r
- You will need to:\r
    - run the [RGI Database Builder](https://my.galaxy.training/?path=?tool_id=toolshed.g2.bx.psu.edu%2Frepos%2Fcard%2Frgi%2Frgi_database_builder%2F1.2.0) as a Galaxy admin (if this hasn't been done already)\r
    - [Have the en_US.UTF-8 locale installed](https://github.com/galaxyproject/tools-iuc/issues/6467) on the compute nodes executing cast/melt jobs.\r
    - Install the requisite tools with e.g. [`shed-tools`](https://ephemeris.readthedocs.io/en/latest/commands/shed-tools.html) command from the [`ephemeris`](https://ephemeris.readthedocs.io/en/latest/) suite: `shed-tools install -g https://galaxy.example.com -a API_KEY -t tools.yaml` (tools.yaml is provided in this repository.)\r
- Then you can import this workflow\r
    - Navigate to `/workflows/import` of your Galaxy server\r
    - Select "GA4GH servers"\r
    - Enter `name:"AMR-Pathfinder"`\r
- And run it\r
    - You must provide a Sequencing collection (list:paired of fastq files)\r
    - And a Genomes collection (list of fasta files) \r
    - Both of these should use **identical** collection element identifiers\r
\r
## Outputs\r
\r
This will produce two important tables: "Binary Comparison" and a "% Identity Scored Outputs". \r
\r
### Binary comparison\r
\r
This file reports the discovery or absence of specific AMR genes across all tested AMR Analysis tools. You will mostly see 1s (presence) or 0s (absence) but you may occasionally see higher numbers when an AMR tool reports multiple hits for a specific gene.\r
\r
### % Identity Scored Outputs\r
\r
This is similar to binary comparison, but using the % identity reported by each AMR tool. For cases where multiple hits were detected, we take the highest.\r
\r
## Known Issues\r
\r
The names for identified AMR genes is highly inconsistent across AMR analysis tools. We urge the AMR community to rectify this by standardising gene names used in their tooling.""" ;
