&nbsp;\r
\r
## 2. Creating and Preparing Indexes ??\r
\r
I'm sorry, but the [main workflow](https://github.com/yonesora56/plant2human/blob/main/Workflow/plant2human_v3_stringent.cwl) does not currently include the creation of an index process (both for protein structure (foldseek index) and protein sequence (BLAST index)).\r
Please perform the following processes in advance.\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
## ⚠️ Important: Database Version Compatibility ⚠️\r
\r
This workflow uses data from the **AlphaFold Protein Structure Database (AFDB) version 6**. Due to recent database updates (v4 → v6, October 2025), users should be aware of potential version mismatches between different data components.\r
\r
## Understanding the Version Issue\r
\r
| Component | AFDB Version | Source |\r
|-----------|-----------------|--------|\r
| Query structures (your plant proteins) | **v6** | AlphaFold Database API |\r
| ⚠️ Foldseek pre-built index | **v4** | `foldseek databases` command |\r
| ⭐ Foldseek index (built myself) | **v6** | FTP download from AFDB |\r
| `sequences.fasta` (for BLAST index) | **v6** | FTP download from AFDB |\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
## Two Main Workflow Options\r
\r
We provide two workflow variants to address this version compatibility issue:\r
\r
| Workflow | Index Source | AFDB index Version Match | Database Options | Use Case |\r
|----------|--------------|:-------------:|------------------|----------|\r
| **`plant2human_v3_permissive.cwl`** | `foldseek databases` (pre-built) | ❌ v4 vs v6 | UniProt50, Swiss-Prot, Proteome, etc. | Exploratory analysis (swiss-prot,TrEMBL) |\r
| **`plant2human_v3_stringent.cwl`** | `foldseek createdb` (self-built) | ✅ v6 = v6 | Human proteome only | Rigorous analysis |\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
### Option 1: Permissive Mode\r
\r
**Pros:**\r
- Easy setup with `foldseek databases` command\r
- Access to diverse databases (UniProt50, Swiss-Prot, etc.)\r
\r
**Cons:**\r
- Version mismatch between query (v6) and index (v4)\r
- Some proteins may have updated structures in v6 that differ from v4\r
\r
**When to use:** broad searches (including swiss-prot, TrEMBL)\r
\r
➡️ **Go to:** [2-1a. Creating a Foldseek Index (Option 1: Permissive Mode))](#2-1a-creating-a-foldseek-index-option-1-permissive-mode)\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
### Option 2: Stringent Mode (Recommended)\r
\r
**Pros:**\r
- Full version consistency (v6 query ↔ v6 index)\r
- Smaller index size (Human proteome only: ~24,000 proteins)\r
- Reproducible results with matched database versions\r
\r
**Cons:**\r
- Requires manual download and index creation\r
- Limited to Human proteome only\r
\r
**When to use:** Final analysis for publications, when version consistency is critical\r
\r
➡️ **Go to:** [2-1b. Creating Index (Stringent Mode)](#2-1b-creating-a-foldseek-index-option-2-stringent-mode)\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
## 2-1. Creating a Foldseek Index for structural alignment\r
\r
## 2-1a. Creating a Foldseek Index (Option 1: Permissive Mode)\r
\r
In this workflow, the target of the structural similarity search is specified as the AlphaFold database to perform comparisons across a broader range of species.\r
Index creation using the `foldseek databases` command is through the following command.\r
\r
Please select the database you want to use from `Alphafold/UniProt,` `Alphafold/UniProt50-minimal`, `Alphafold/UniProt50`, `Alphafold/Proteome,` `Alphafold/Swiss-Prot.`\r
\r
```bash\r
# Supported databases in this workflow\r
Alphafold/UniProt\r
Alphafold/UniProt50-minimal\r
Alphafold/UniProt50\r
Alphafold/Proteome\r
Alphafold/Swiss-Prot\r
```\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
You can check the details of this database using the following command.\r
\r
```bash\r
docker run --rm quay.io/biocontainers/foldseek:10.941cd33--h5021889_1 foldseek databases --help\r
```\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
For example, if you want to specify AlphaFold/Swiss-Prot as the index, you can do so with the following CWL file;\r
\r
```bash\r
# execute creation of foldseek index using "foldseek databases"\r
# test date: 2025-12-12\r
cwltool --debug --outdir ./index/ \\\r
./Tools/02_foldseek_database.cwl \\\r
--database Alphafold/Swiss-Prot \\\r
--index_dir_name index_swissprot \\\r
--index_name swissprot \\\r
--threads 16\r
```\r
---\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
## 2-1b. Creating a Foldseek Index (Option 2: Stringent Mode)\r
\r
In this mode, you download structure files directly from AFDB v6 and create your own index.\r
This ensures version consistency between query and target structures.\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
### Step 1: Download Human proteome from AFDB v6\r
\r
```bash\r
# Download date: 2026-03-20\r
# file size is ~5GB\r
\r
cd ./index\r
\r
# curl\r
curl -O https://ftp.ebi.ac.uk/pub/databases/alphafold/v6/UP000005640_9606_HUMAN_v6.tar\r
\r
# or aria2c\r
aria2c -c --max-connection-per-server=4 \\\r
--min-split-size=1M \\\r
-o "UP000005640_9606_HUMAN_v6.tar" \\\r
"https://ftp.ebi.ac.uk/pub/databases/alphafold/v6/UP000005640_9606_HUMAN_v6.tar"\r
\r
cd ../\r
```\r
\r
### Step 2: Create Foldseek index using `foldseek createdb` command\r
\r
```bash\r
# test date: 2026-03-20\r
# foldseek version: https://github.com/steineggerlab/foldseek/releases/tag/10-941cd33\r
cwltool --debug --outdir ./index/ \\\r
./Tools/02_foldseek_createdb.cwl \\\r
--input_structure_files ./index/UP000005640_9606_HUMAN_v6.tar \\\r
--index_dir_name index_human_proteome_v6 \\\r
--index_name human_proteome_v6 \\\r
--threads 16\r
```\r
---\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
## 2-2. Creating a Index for protein "sequence" alignment (Common)\r
\r
An index protein sequence FASTA file must be downloaded to obtain the amino acid sequence using the `blastdbcmd` command from the AlphaFold Protein Structure Database.\r
This workflow uses the version of the protein sequence that was used for structure prediction.\r
\r
>**?? Note:**: This FASTA file is extremely large (**> 109GB !**), so it's probably best to delete FASTA file after creating the index.\r
\r
```bash\r
# Preparation for BLAST index\r
# test date: 2026-03-21\r
cd ./index\r
\r
# curl\r
curl -O https://ftp.ebi.ac.uk/pub/databases/alphafold/sequences.fasta # AFDB version 6\r
\r
# or aria2c (recommend)\r
aria2c --continue=true \\\r
--max-connection-per-server=4 \\\r
--min-split-size=1M \\\r
https://ftp.ebi.ac.uk/pub/databases/alphafold/sequences.fasta # AFDB version 6\r
\r
# rename\r
mv sequences.fasta afdb_all_sequences_v6.fasta\r
cd ../\r
```\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
```bash\r
# execute creation of BLAST index using "makeblastdb"\r
# test date: 2026-03-21\r
cwltool --debug \\\r
--outdir ./index/ \\\r
./Tools/03_makeblastdb.cwl \\\r
--index_dir_name index_uniprot_afdb_all_sequences_v6 \\\r
--input_fasta_file ./index/afdb_all_sequences_v6.fasta\r
```\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
> **?? Note:** It is estimated to take 2~ hours for creating index.\r
> **This index is about  > 150GB!**\r
> **We are currently investigating whether it can be executed by another method...**\r
\r
---\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
## 3. Execution of the `plant2human` workflow (main workflow)\r
\r
> **?? Note:** Network access required in this process!\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
In this process, we perform a structural similarity search using the `foldseek easy-search` command and then perform a pairwise sequence alignment of the amino acid sequences of the hit pairs using the `needle` and `water` commands.\r
Finally, based on this information, we create a scatter plot and output a jupyter notebook as a report.\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
> **?? Note:** For Permissive Mode (using pre-built indexes like Swiss-Prot), see [Workflow/README.md](https://github.com/yonesora56/plant2human/blob/main/Workflow/README.md).\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
## ?? YAML Parameter File Reference (Stringent Mode)\r
\r
The main workflow requires a YAML parameter file to specify input files and parameters.\r
Below is a detailed explanation of each parameter.\r
\r
**Example file (2026-03-22 update!):** [`job/plant2human_v3_stringent_example_os100.yml`](https://github.com/yonesora56/plant2human/blob/main/job/plant2human_v3_stringent_example_os100.yml)\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
### Input File Parameters\r
\r
| Parameter | Type | Description | Example |\r
|-----------|------|-------------|---------|\r
| `INPUT_DIRECTORY` | Directory | Directory containing mmCIF structure files from Step 1 | `../test/.../os_100_genes_mmcif/` |\r
| `FILE_MATCH_PATTERN` | string | File pattern for structure files | `"*.cif"` |\r
| `FOLDSEEK_INDEX` | File | Foldseek index created in Step 2-1b | `../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6` |\r
| `QUERY_IDMAPPING_TSV` | File | ID mapping TSV from Step 1 | `..._idmapping_all.tsv` |\r
| `QUERY_GENE_LIST_TSV` | File | Original gene list TSV | `oryza_sativa_random_100genes_list.tsv` |\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
### Foldseek Parameters (`foldseek easy-search` command)\r
\r
for more details, please execute the below command.\r
\r
```bash\r
docker run --rm quay.io/biocontainers/foldseek:10.941cd33--h5021889_1 \\\r
foldseek easy-search --help\r
```\r
\r
| Parameter | Default | Description |\r
|-----------|---------|-------------|\r
| `COVERAGE_THRESHOLD` | `0.75` | (0~1) Coverage threshold for search results |\r
| `COV_MODE` | `5` | (1,2,3,4,5) Coverage mode for search results. `5` means short sequence needs to be at least x% of the other seq. length |\r
| `EVALUE` | `0.1` | E-value threshold for structural similarity search |\r
| `ALIGNMENT_TYPE` | `2` | 0: 3Di only, 1: **TM-align (default)**, 2: 3Di+AA |\r
| `THREADS` | `16` | Number of CPU threads |\r
| `SPLIT_MEMORY_LIMIT` | `"120G"` | Memory limit for large searches |\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
## YAML Template for Stringent Mode\r
\r
Copy and modify this template for your analysis:\r
\r
```YAML\r
# ============================================================\r
# YAML Parameter File for plant2human_v3_stringent.cwl\r
# Species: [Your Species Name]\r
# ============================================================\r
\r
# ---------- INPUT DIRECTORY ----------\r
INPUT_DIRECTORY:\r
  class: Directory\r
  location: ./path/to/your_mmcif_directory/           # <-- CHANGE THIS!\r
\r
FILE_MATCH_PATTERN: "*.cif"\r
\r
# ---------- FOLDSEEK INDEX (Stringent Mode) ----------\r
FOLDSEEK_INDEX:\r
  class: File\r
  location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6  # <-- Adjust path if needed\r
  secondaryFiles:                                               # <-- If you do not place the index in the “index” directory, you must specify the path to all generated index files! (This is generally not required.)\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6_ca\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6_ca.dbtype\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6_ca.index\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6_h\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6_h.dbtype\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6_h.index\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6_mapping\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6_ss\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6_ss.dbtype\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6_ss.index\r
    # No _taxonomy for self-built index\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6.dbtype\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6.index\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6.lookup\r
    - class: File\r
      location: ../index/index_human_proteome_v6/human_proteome_v6.source\r
    # No .version for self-built index\r
\r
# ---------- FOLDSEEK DEFAULT PARAMETERS ----------\r
COVERAGE_THRESHOLD: 0.75\r
COV_MODE: 5\r
EVALUE: 0.1\r
ALIGNMENT_TYPE: 1  # 1 = TM-align\r
THREADS: 16\r
SPLIT_MEMORY_LIMIT: "120G"\r
\r
# ---------- EXTRACT ID COLUMNS ----------\r
WF_COLUMN_NUMBER_QUERY_SPECIES: 1\r
WF_COLUMN_NUMBER_HIT_SPECIES: 2\r
\r
QUERY_IDMAPPING_TSV:\r
  class: File\r
  format: edam:format_3475\r
  location: ./path/to/your_idmapping_all.tsv          # <-- CHANGE THIS!\r
\r
QUERY_GENE_LIST_TSV:\r
  class: File\r
  format: edam:format_3475\r
  location: ./path/to/your_gene_list.tsv              # <-- CHANGE THIS!\r
```\r
\r
---\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
