<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1230?version=2> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Marco Salvi" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-02-21" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """Forecasting the atmospheric dispersal of volcanic products requires accurate input parameters for transport models, including meteorological data and ash/gas emission terms. Such forecasting builds upon three basic ingredients:\r
1. Meteorological Data. Typically derived from global, regional, or local-scale models, meteorological data drive the transport and deposition of volcanic particles.\r
2. Transport Models. These models simulate atmospheric dispersal, incorporating processes such as wind advection, turbulent diffusion, and particle sedimentation. Besides atmospheric density and viscosity, sedimentation velocities depend also on particle size, density, and shape, which affect the particle drag coefficients and atmospheric residence times. Some transport models (e.g. FALL3D) include volcanic ash aggregation and aerosol chemistry, two aspects that can improve the accuracy of dispersal forecasts.\r
3. Emission Models. Emission models provide the so-called Eruption Source Parameters (ESP), needed to characterize the near vent properties of volcanic plumes (that are a kind of clouds which are connected to their source). The ESP, including eruption start and duration, cloud injection height, vertical mass distribution across the eruption column, Total Grain Size Distribution (TGSD), and Mass Eruption Rate (MER) are essential inputs to transport models. While some parameters (e.g., eruption start and end times, column height) can be directly observed, others like MER must be estimated by means of indirect methods, which introduces significant input uncertainties that propagate through the modelling workflow.\r
\r
The DTC-V2 workflow (WF5201) configures and runs an ensemble of FALL3D model realizations, potentially assimilating in the model different sources of data (groundbased or satellite-based observations). A major advantage of the ensemble model approach is that it allows delivering both deterministic (e.g. ensemble mean) and probabilistic (e.g. fraction of ensemble members exceeding a given condition) forecasts, reflecting the inherent uncertainties in the ESPs. On a coarse-grain, the workflow requires four main steps:\r
1. Get atmospheric data from Numerical Weather Prediction (NWP) models, assumed to be run and delivered by a third party. DTC-V2 can ingest either NWP forecast data (up to a few days ahead) or reanalyses (for past events or “what if” scenarios). Different agencies (e.g. ECMWF, National Weather Services, etc.) serve global/regional data at various spatial and temporal resolutions.\r
2. Get the Eruption Source Parameters (ESP) from different sources; e.g. from volcano monitoring data provided by State Volcano Observatories (SVO). In case ESPs from several sources are available, the workflow assigns a ranked\r
priority as shown in Table 1.\r
3. Setup the FALL3D model and run an ensemble of simulations in the FENIX RI or HPC (leonardo@CINECA). The different ensemble members, each representing a single deterministic scenario, are set by perturbing some critical ESPs (e.g. eruption column height) within their uncertainty range. The model allows introducing both absolute and relative errors for all ESP around the observed value.\r
4. Post-process the results""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1230" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-V2] WF5201: Forecast volcanic ash fallout and dispersal in the atmosphere" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1230?version=2> ;
    ns1:version "2" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1231?version=3> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Marco Salvi" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-06-30" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """# [DTC-T1] WF6101: Tsunami impact forecasting\r
\r
This repository contains a Common Workflow Language (CWL) and Ro-Crate metadata definition for DTC-T1 workflow 6101, which is designed for \r
providing tsunami impact forecasting following a tsunamigenic earthquake event, based on a probabilistic approach. The workflow integrates real-time\r
earthquake data, runs HPC simulations, and generates tsunami hazard maps. \r
\r
The main CWL implementation is found in WF6101.cwl, together with ST610106 and ST610109 describing complex steps. The file mini-ST610106.cwl is refereed to a specific\r
realization of the ST610106, where different alternative options are included.\r
\r
To see a preview of the RO-Crate metadata the `ro-crate-preview.html` file can be opeens in the browser.\r
\r
## Overview\r
\r
The DTC-T1 Digital Twin component operates a single workflow (WF6101) that runs 11 main steps. DTC-T1 is also referred to as PTF,\r
Probabilistic Tsunami Forecasting. \r
\r
The workflow is initialized by a potentially tsunamigenic earthquake event. Based on the real-time event parameters together with long-term information \r
retrieved from a regional-scale tsunami hazard model, an ensemble of earthquake scenarios compatible with the occurring event is built, where each scenario\r
is weighted by the probability of being a good representation of the actual event. The tsunami impact computed by numerical modeling and the scenario \r
probabilities are aggregated into exceedance probabilities for given tsunami intensities at given coastal points to calculate the hazard curves and\r
subsequently the hazard maps. \r
\r
The workflow is designed to operate in (near-)real-time and to be employed at Tsunami Warning Centres for operational tsunami early warning and forecasting,\r
as well as for rapid post-event assessment.\r
\r
## Workflow structure\r
\r
The workflow consists of multiple steps (ST), datasets (DT), and software services (SS). Below is a simplified breakdown:\r
\r
- The Scenario Player (ST610101) acts like a data archive, storing observations (synthetic or measured) (DT6101) recorded by external data providers\r
(i.e. seismic data, labeled as DT6103, sea level data, labeled as DT6104, and GNSS data, labeled as DT6105) and sending them to specific data listeners.\r
\r
- The Listener EQ (ST610102) evaluates the earthquake information that initialise the workflow execution, while Listeners SL (ST610103) and GNSS (ST610104)\r
elaborate observational sea level and ground deformation data for further comparison with simulated data. \r
\r
- The Ensemble Manager (ST610105) combines the information from the Listener EQ with the long-term information from a regional hazard model (DT6102) and \r
defines a list of scenarios (DT6106) compatible with the triggering event, with their associated probabilities (DT6107). \r
\r
- The ensemble of earthquake scenarios is provided as input to the Scenario Modelling (ST610106), which makes use of pre-defined topo-bathymetric grids (DT6109) \r
and computes tsunami intensities (DT6110) and ground deformation (DT6111). In the modeling step different alternative and/or combined models can be used \r
in each single workflow realization. More specifically, different source and tsunami models can be combined through the following software: \r
Tsunami-HySEA (SS6107), SeisSol (SS6106), BingClaw (SS6109), Shaltop (SS6110), Landslide-HySEA (SS6108), Source-to-wave filter (SS6112), \r
and the Inundation AI module (SS6111). User settings will dictate which model combinations are invoked. As an alternative, precomputed scenarios \r
can be simply retrieved (SS6119). \r
\r
- The Misfit Evaluator (ST610107) combines simulation outputs with eventual SL and GNSS data to evaluate the degree of confidence between the simulated \r
scenarios and observations. According to the computed misfit, the initial scenario ensemble and related probabilities can be updated. \r
\r
- The Hazard Aggregator (ST610108) aggregates scenario probabilities and impact metrics to calculate hazard curves at multiple forecast points (DT6112).\r
\r
-  The AL (Alert level) and Visualization step (ST610109) makes the final processing of the results, converting hazard into Alert Levels and producing \r
probabilistic visual maps (DT6113). It is worth noting that only the Tsunami Service Providers (TSP) are allowed in producing and issuing the AL for \r
operational tsunami early warning and forecasting.\r
\r
- In case of landslide-triggered tsunamis, input in the form of shake maps generated for the occurring event through the Earthquake Modeling \r
(ST610111) is forwarded to the Landslide Scenario Manager (ST610110), which in turn feeds landslide scenarios (DT6108) to the Scenario Modelling.\r
\r
\r
## Workflow diagram\r
\r
Here is a visual overview of the described workflow, and the corresponding CWL graph automatically generated from the metadata information stored in the \r
CWL workflow file.\r
\r
![wf_diag](images/PTF_Workflow_Diagram_FROZEN.drawio.png)\r
\r
![cwl_wf](images/cwl_graph_wf.png)\r
\r
## The mini-ST610106 for operationalization\r
\r
We focused on one realization of the Scenario Modeling step (hereinafter called mini-ST610106), where the T-HySEA code (SS6107) is used\r
to model both the earthquake source and the following tsunami, and only tsunami intensities (DT6110) are saved. In this experiment, the singularity \r
image of the T-HySEA code created with the eFlows4HPC image service creation is used. Moreover, PyCOMPSs functionalities have been implemented in \r
order to execute the mini-ST610106 as a COMPSs job on the Booster partition of the Leonardo cluster at the HPC CINECA infrastructure.\r
\r
![cwl_mini](images/ministep_graph.png)\r
\r
To enable execution, an executable CWL file containing only the information required to run the process has been produced. In this case, \r
the processing step is implemented by the bash script `run_gpu_compss.sh` that uses PyCOMPSs to submit a job to the Leonardo cluster queue through \r
the Slurm scheduler. The executable CWL file does not explicitly specify input and output data because the underlying processing step manages \r
these details internally. An input configuration file `input.yaml` provides the necessary global input data.\r
\r
### How to execute the mini-ST610106\r
\r
The mini-workflow depicted is executed using the following command:\r
\r
`cwltool --cachedir cache --preserve-entire-environment mini-ST610106.cwl input.yaml`\r
\r
In this command, the --cachedir flag is employed to save all files generated during execution for debugging purposes, while the \r
--preserve-entire-environment flag ensures that the current environment variables are transferred into the custom execution environment \r
established by cwltool. Once the command is launched, cwltool creates an isolated execution environment where it runs the bash script, which, in turn, \r
submits the PyCOMPSs job to the computational node via Slurm, with the standard output and error captured in the cwl.out and cwl.err files, respectively.\r
\r
The execution exploits the singularity image of the Tsunami-HySEA code, built with the image creation service of eFlows4HPC using\r
parameters that fit the architecture of the Cineca cluster Leonardo (i.e., linux/amd64, x86_64, openmpi@4.1.4, cuda@11.8).""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1231" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophysics" ;
    ns1:license "CC-BY-NC-ND-4.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-T1] WF6101: Tsunami impact forecasting" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1231?version=3> ;
    ns1:version "3" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1234?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.1234.1" ;
    ns1:creator "Tobias Bachmann" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-01-02" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """## Installation\r
\r
Other than cloning this repository, you need to have bash installed (which is most likely the case if you use Linux, *BSD or even MacOS).\r
For the Python code, the arguably easiest and cleanest way is to set up a Python virtual environment and install the dependencies there:\r
\r
``` shell\r
$ python3 -m venv ./hcp-suite-venv # Setup the virtual environment\r
$ source ./hcp-suite-venv/bin/activate # Activate the virtual environment\r
$ pip install pandas pingouin networkx nilearn nibabel ray # Install dependencies within the virtual environment\r
$ pip install ipython jupyterlab # Install interactive Python shells to run hcp-suite code in\r
```\r
\r
## Usage: CPM tutorial\r
\r
The following tutorial uses the gambling task as an example, the variable to be predicted is BMI. Differing commands for resting-state fMRI are provided for the corresponding steps.\r
\r
### Overview\r
\r
Python code in this repository is written to be used in an interactive Python shell. Either Jupyter Lab or Jupyer Notebook is recommended as plots and images are conveniently displayed in-line but any Python shell (e.g. iPython) should work.\r
\r
Generally speaking, the procedure is as follows:\r
\r
1. Downloading data of subjects to be included in the analysis\r
2. Parcellation of CIFTI files with `task.sh prepare` (at the code's current state, the HCP's folder structure is assumed)\r
3a. Extraction of task-based fMRI time series based on EV files provided by the HCP with `get_ev_timeseries()` from `hcpsuite.py`\r
3b. \r
4. Computing of correlation matrices via `compute_correlations()` from `hcpsuite.py`\r
5. Performing of CPM, the functions are provided by `cpm_ray.py`\r
6. Establishing of statistical significance by way of permutation testing (also `cpm_ray.py`)\r
\r
The following code snippets are to be run in an interactive Python shell (e.g. the "Console" of Jupyter Lab):\r
\r
#### Downloading data\r
\r
We will use Amazon Web Services to download HCP data. Set up access to HCP data via Amazon Web Services by following their [documentation](https://wiki.humanconnectome.org/docs/How%20To%20Connect%20to%20Connectome%20Data%20via%20AWS.html). You should be provided with an AWS Access Key ID and Secret Access Key we are going to put into Python variables (in quotation marks) for easy access:\r
\r
``` python\r
aws_access_key_id="Replace with your access key ID"\r
aws_secret_access_key="Replace with your secret access key"\r
\r
# Also, specify a location you want to download files to. We will use this variable repeatedly.\r
data_dir = "./HCP_1200"\r
```\r
\r
We now need a list of subject IDs to be included in the analysis. Save them in a simple text file with one line per subject ID. For the rest of this tutorial, we will use the gambling task as an example (the list of IDs will be called ```gambling_subjects.ids```). As preprocessing for resting-state data differs, we will provide resting-state specific instructions when needed (the list of IDs will be called ```rest_subjects.ids```).\r
\r
``` python\r
import sys\r
\r
sys.path.append("../hcp-suite/lib") # This assumes the hcp-suite directory is in the current working directory's parent directory. You can also use absolute paths.\r
from hcpsuite import *\r
from cpm_ray import *\r
\r
subj_ids = load_list_from_file("./gambling_subjects.ids") # Change the path to gambling_subjects.ids if it resides elsewhere\r
\r
# Check the number of subjects\r
len(subj_ids)\r
```\r
\r
Next, we will use the function download_hcp_files() to download all needed files from the HCP's AWS storage. This function takes the following arguments in this order: List of subject IDs, download location, task name, AWS access key ID, and AWS secret access key. It will return a list of missing subjects we capture in the variable missing_subjs:\r
\r
``` python\r
missing_subjs = download_hcp_files(subj_ids, data_dir, "gambling", aws_access_key_id, aws_secret_access_key)\r
```\r
\r
The previous command will print a list of missing subjects (if any) since not all data from all subjects is available on AWS, unfortunately. You can either obtain data from other sources or remove the missing subjects from our list of subject IDs:\r
\r
\r
``` python\r
for missing_subj in missing_subjs:\r
    subj_ids.remove(missing_subj)\r
```\r
\r
#### Parcellation\r
\r
Parcellation involves combining voxels of the "raw" CIFTI files into parcels as specified by the combined cortical, subcortical, and cerebellar parcellation we dubbed "RenTianGlasser" after the authors of the individual parcellations.\r
\r
##### Task-based fMRI\r
``` python\r
parcellate_ciftis(subj_ids=subj_ids,\r
                  parcellation_fname="./hcp-suite/data/parcellations/RenTianGlasser.dlabel.nii",\r
                  task="gambling",\r
                  data_dir=data_dir) # If you have installed Connectome Workbench in a non-standard way,\r
                                     # so that 'wb_command' is not in your $PATH, add\r
                                     # 'wb_command="/replace/with/path/to/wb_command"'\r
```\r
\r
##### Resting-state fMRI\r
\r
As resting-state fMRI data was collected in two separate sessions called REST1 and REST2, we need to parcellate twice:\r
\r
```\r
# First for REST1\r
\r
parcellate_ciftis(subj_ids=subj_ids, \r
                  parcellation_fname="./hcp-suite/data/parcellations/RenTianGlasser.dlabel.nii", \r
                  task="REST1", \r
                  data_dir=data_dir) # If you have installed Connectome Workbench in a non-standard way, \r
                                     # so that 'wb_command' is not in your $PATH, add \r
                                     # 'wb_command="/replace/with/path/to/wb_command"'\r
\r
# Now for REST2\r
parcellate_ciftis(subj_ids=subj_ids, \r
                  parcellation_fname="./hcp-suite/data/parcellations/RenTianGlasser.dlabel.nii", \r
                  task="REST2", \r
                  data_dir=data_dir) # If you have installed Connectome Workbench in a non-standard way, \r
                                     # so that 'wb_command' is not in your $PATH, add \r
                                     # 'wb_command="/replace/with/path/to/wb_command"'											 \r
```\r
\r
\r
#### Extraction of time series\r
\r
##### Task-based fMRI\r
``` python\r
ev_data_dict = get_ev_timeseries(subj_ids, ["win.txt"], task="gambling",\r
                                 runs=('LR', 'RL'),\r
                                 parcellation="RenTianGlasser",\r
                                 data_dir=data_dir) # If you have installed Connectome Workbench in a non-standard way,\r
                                                    # so that 'wb_command' is not in your $PATH, add\r
                                                    # 'wb_command="/replace/with/path/to/wb_command"'\r
\r
# Now, we save the extraced time series as text files in a directory of our choice\r
# (in this case: ./GAMBLING_win)\r
save_data_dict(ev_data_dict, path="./GAMBLING_win")\r
```\r
\r
##### Resting-state fMRI\r
\r
Extraction of time series for resting-state fMRI is less complicated via the `get_rest_timeseries` function:\r
\r
```\r
ts_dict = get_rest_timeseries(subj_ids, data_dir)\r
\r
# Save time series files in directory "REST"\r
save_data_dict(ts_dict, path="./REST")\r
```\r
\r
\r
#### Computation of correlation matrices\r
\r
We continue in our Python shell to compute correlation matrices:\r
\r
``` python\r
# We load the saved time series from the previous step\r
cd GAMBLING_win # save_data_dict() writes file names into file "ts_files" but without paths,\r
                # thus the easiest way is to change into the directory containing the files\r
time_series, time_series_files = load_time_series("./ts_files") # ... and read them from there\r
\r
correlation_measure, correlation_matrices = compute_correlations(time_series, kind='tangent')\r
# Tangent in our experience provides the best results, but there are alternatives:\r
# https://nilearn.github.io/dev/modules/generated/nilearn.connectome.ConnectivityMeasure.html\r
\r
# We then save the matrices into a single file in the NIfTI format for downstream processing\r
save_matrix(cifti_dim_to_nifti_dim(correlation_matrices), "./GAMBLING_win-tangent.nii.gz")\r
```\r
\r
#### CPM\r
\r
For actual CPM, we need to install and run [Ray](https://ray.io) (run this e.g. in your Python virtual environment as described in [Installation](#installation)):\r
\r
``` shell\r
$ pip install ray\r
$ ray start --head --num-cpus=16 # Run this on your main node.\r
                                 # Processes take up a lot of RAM, be careful not to use too many CPUs\r
```\r
\r
Optionally add more Ray nodes to form a cluster, see the [Ray documentation](https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/cluster/vms/user-guides/launching-clusters/on-premises.html) for details.\r
\r
##### Merging behavioral/biometric data in Python\r
\r
For our analysis, we need values from both the unrestricted and [restricted HCP data](https://www.humanconnectome.org/study/hcp-young-adult/document/restricted-data-usage), which are available as separate CSV files. For easier handling, we merge them into a single CSV file:\r
\r
``` python\r
import pandas as pd\r
unrestricted = pd.read_csv("/path/to/unrestricted.csv")\r
restricted = pd.read_csv("/path/to/restricted.csv")\r
merged = pd.merge(restricted, unrestricted, on="Subject")\r
merged.to_csv("./merged.csv") # Save the merged DataFrame as a CSV file in the current directory\r
```\r
\r
##### Loading and preparing data in Python\r
\r
``` python\r
behav = 'BMI' # Which variable do we want to predict?\r
covars = ['Age_in_Yrs', 'Gender' ] # What variables do we want to correct for?\r
\r
behav_data = get_behav_data("./path/to/merged.csv", ids) # Loading of behavioral and biometrical data as a Pandas DataFrame from a CSV file\r
# We need to binarize gender for our analysis\r
behav_data["Gender"] = behav_data["Gender"].replace("F", 0)\r
behav_data["Gender"] = behav_data["Gender"].replace("M", 1)\r
\r
functional_data = convert_matrices_to_dataframe(nifti_dim_to_cifti_dim(get_nimg_data("./path/to/GAMBLING_win-tangent.nii.gz")), subj_ids) # Loading of correlation matrices as Pandas DataFrame\r
```\r
\r
##### Starting the Ray handler\r
\r
ray_handler() is a Python class through which data management and the starting and coordination of Ray Actors (i.e. the processes working in parallel) is being handled.\r
\r
``` python\r
n_folds = 128 # In this example, we use 128 folds, which is a good starting point\r
n_perm = 1000 # How many permutations are we planning to do later on?\r
\r
ray_handler = RayHandler(\r
    functional_data.copy(),\r
    behav_data.copy(),\r
    behav,\r
    covars,\r
    address="auto", # We assume that the main Ray process runs on the same host\r
    n_perm=n_perm,\r
) # You can safely ignore the PerformanceWarning messages\r
\r
ray_handler.add_kfold_indices(n_folds, clean=True) # By setting "clean" to True, we remove twins from the fold so they don't predict each other\r
ray_handler.upload_data() # Functional and behavioral data are uploaded into the shared storage to which Ray Actors have access\r
```\r
\r
##### Starting the analysis\r
\r
First we define the jobs for the actors; a job is a Python list object consisting of the following items: "job type", "fold number", "permutation number". The permutation number for the actual, i.e. unpermutated, data is "-1".\r
\r
``` python\r
job_list = [["fselection_and_prediction", fold, perm] for perm in [-1] for fold in range(n_folds)] # This is the job list without permutations\r
\r
# If we wanted to also compute the permutations (which takes a very long time), the job list can be created as follows:\r
#job_list = [["fselection_and_prediction", fold, perm] for perm in [-1, *range(n_perm)] for fold in range(n_folds)]\r
\r
ray_handler.start_actors(job_list) # Start computing\r
```\r
\r
##### Monitoring progress and retrieving results\r
\r
``` python\r
n_done, n_remaining, n_held = ray_handler.status() # Prints a status report (see screenshot)\r
\r
results = ray_handler.get_results(n=100) # Retrieving a huge number of results (e.g. when performing permutation analysis)\r
                                         # and especially from distributed Ray actors can take a long time. Specifying the\r
                                         # number of results (e.g. n=100) to be retrieved from the results store at once\r
                                         # allows for a display of progress\r
\r
# Optional: Save results into a file (NumPy format)\r
np.save("./GAMBLING_win-results.npy", results)\r
\r
# This file can be used to restore results\r
results = np.load("./GAMBLING_win-results.npy", allow_pickle=True).item()\r
```\r
\r
You might consider fetching results and saving them periodically with a simple loop:\r
\r
``` python\r
sleep_time = 1200 # Sleep for 20 minutes and then rerun loop\r
n_remaining = 1 # Set to something > 0 to get the loop started\r
results_path = get_safe_path("./GAMBLING_win-results", ".npy")\r
while n_remaining > 0: # Run until no jobs to be fetched are remaining\r
    n_done, n_remaining, n_held = ray_handler.status()\r
    if n_held > 0:\r
        results = ray_handler.get_results(n=100)\r
        # BEWARE: the file in results_path will be overwritten without asking\r
        # but we have used get_safe_path for risk mitigation\r
        print("\\nSaving results to {}...".format(results_path), end='', flush=True)\r
        np.save(results_path, results)\r
        print(" done.")\r
    else:\r
        print("\\nNo results to fetch and save.")\r
    print("Sleeping for {} seconds...".format(sleep_time))\r
    sleep(sleep_time) # Will sleep for sleep_time seconds\r
```\r
\r
\r
#### Check for completion\r
\r
Rarely single jobs or actors die before completion. Having run your analyses, you can check your results for completion as follows and rerun analyses as needed (the function ```check_for_completion``` will advise you on how do this):\r
\r
``` python\r
incomplete_jobs = check_for_completion(results)\r
```\r
\r
#### Presenting results\r
\r
To plot observed values against values as predicted by the GLM, use plot_predictions():\r
\r
``` python\r
perm = -1 # Permutation -1 selects unpermutated results\r
g = plot_predictions(results['prediction_results'][perm], tail="glm", color="green")\r
```\r
\r
\r
To plot permutation results, use plot_permutation_results(), which will take either a list of paths to saved results files (f.i. when you want to combine multiple permutation runs) or the prediction results dictionary, which we will use in the following example:\r
\r
``` python\r
# plot_permutation_results() will automatically remove incomplete permutations\r
# and return/print basic descriptive statistics (minimum and maximum r value, total\r
# number of permutations, and the minimum p value to be achieved with these permutations\r
\r
plot, min_value, max_value, count, min_p = plot_permutation_results(results['prediction_results'])\r
```\r
\r
For more presentation and plotting examples including static and interactive plots of predictive networks, see ```save.py``` in folder ```utils```.\r
\r
#### Overlap of predictive networks\r
\r
cpm_ray.py has a function ```get_overlap()``` to create overlap networks of different predictive networks. For example, if you have two different CPM results as generated by ```results = ray_handler.get_results()``` and saved in two files ```results_A.npy``` and ```results_B.npy```, you can do the following:\r
\r
``` python\r
# Load results into Python\r
results_A = np.load("/path/to/results_A.npy", allow_pickle=True).item()\r
results_B = np.load("/path/to/results_B.npy", allow_pickle=True).item()\r
\r
# Load coordinates of parcels for plotting\r
coords = np.loadtxt("/path/to/hcp-suite/data/parcellations/RenTianGlasser.coords")\r
\r
# Use overlap() function, which takes as its main input a list of results, in this case\r
# [results_A, results_B]. You can specify a result as the odd one out (e.g. odd_one_out=0\r
# for results_A), which means that result_A's tails will be switched, so that the positive\r
# tail result A will be overlapped with the negative tail of result B and vice versa.\r
#\r
# overlap() returns a bunch of dictionaries with the tail (positive and negative predictive\r
# networks)  as a primary key and usually several subkeys. These are more or less\r
# self-explanatory.\r
\r
overlap, degrees_sorted, top_n_edges, plot_dict, m_cons = get_overlap([results_A, results_B], odd_one_out=None, coords=coords, plot_top_nodes=True, top_n_edges=50)\r
\r
```\r
""" ;
