<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1251?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-01-21" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """# Point-based Individual Tree Delineation from 3D LiDAR Point Cloud Data.\r
\r
This module implements a lightweight and easy-to-use **_Point-based_** method for individual tree delineation from 3D point cloud data using pure C/C++.\r
\r
The source code files are included in folder **[TreeSeparation]**, which consists of a project generated from _Visual Studio 2015_. The **CLASS** for tree separation is named "FoxTree" and can be found in the respect **_FoxTree.h_** and **_FoxTree.cpp_** files. \r
\r
## Inupt\r
 The input of this module is **TREE Points** only, as illustrated by the figures below.\r
\r
![test-02](TestDatasets/test-02.png)\r
\r
![Another test data](TestDatasets/test-03.png)\r
\r
![One more test data](TestDatasets/test-04.png)\r
 \r
 The format of the tree points is **_*.xyz_**, such as:\r
 ``` javascript {.line-numbers}\r
          x            y         z          \r
      623772.9200 4834465.5900 77.7409     \r
         ...         ...        ...       \r
```\r
Note that if the original data does not have color information, either initiate the last three columns with arbitrary integers or modify the code on data loading. \r
\r
\r
## Parameters\r
There are three parameters have to be initialized for optimal individualization results:\r
\r
     \r
     *  Searching radius;\r
     *  Vertical resolution;\r
     *  Minimum number of points per cluster;\r
     \r
\r
As demonstrated by the **code snippet** below (Note that the parameters are based on geo-referenced point cloud data.):\r
\r
\r
``` javascript {.line-numbers}\r
	//Parameter settings\r
	\r
	const double radius = 1.0;  \r
	//Searching Radius, 1.0 meter;\r
	\r
	const double verticalResolution = 1.0;  \r
	//Vertical resolution of the layers, 1.0 meter;\r
	\r
	const int miniPtsPerCluster = 5; \r
	// Minimum number of points per cluster, 5 points;\r
```\r
\r
**Hints on Parameter Settings:**\r
\r
**_Radius_** should be in accordance with the average point density, i.e. to ensure there are a certain number of points within the radius.\r
\r
**_VerticalResolution_** depends on the overall point density and fineness of results.\r
\r
## Output\r
The output of this implementation is an ASCII format **_*.xyz_** file as well:\r
```javascript {.line-numbers}\r
	treeID        x            y         z         r      g     b \r
	  89     623942.8999 4833932.5500   77.8399   36      76    89\r
	  ...         ...        ...         ...      ...    ...    ...\r
```\r
Notably, the first column is the **index of tree** of which this point is assigned. The last three columns are randomly designated color for the points of a same tree.\r
\r
The individual tree delineation results are given as the figures below:\r
![Individual tree delineation results](Results/test-02-results-1.0-0.7-3.png)\r
![Individual tree delineation results](Results/test-02-results-1.0-0.7-3_01.png)\r
![Individual tree delineation results](Results/test-03-results-1.0-0.5-3.png)\r
![Individual tree delineation results](Results/test-04-results-1.0-0.8-5.png)\r
\r
## Declarations\r
In this implementation,  **nanoflann** is employed for **_KNN_** searching, which can be found from here [link](https://github.com/jlblancoc/nanoflann). \r
\r
