<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1322?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.1322.1" ;
    ns1:creator "zhantian xu" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-03-19" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-03-19" ;
    ns1:description """# PISAD - Phsaed Intraspecies Sample Anomalies Detection tool\r
\r
## Summary\r
\r
We developed PISAD, a tool designed to detect anomalies in cohort samples without requiring reference information. It is primarily divided into two stages. Stage 1: We select low-error data from the cohort and conduct reference-free SNP calling to construct a variant sketch. Stage 2: By comparing the k-mer counts of other cohort data to the variant sketch, we infer the relationships between the sample and other samples to detect the sample swap.\r
\r
## Dependencies\r
\r
recommend use conda to install\r
\r
- GCC (Tested on 8.5.0)\r
- gperftools(2.10)\r
- hdf5(1.14.3)\r
- boost(1.85.0)\r
\r
## Installation\r
\r
cloning the PISAD repository to your machine and enter its directory.\r
\r
```bash\r
 git clone https://github.com/ZhantianXu/PISAD.git\r
 cd pisad/\r
```\r
\r
Compiling should be as easy as:\r
\r
```bash\r
./configure && make\r
```\r
\r
To install in a specified directory:\r
\r
```bash\r
./configure --prefix=/PATH && make install\r
```\r
\r
## Usage\r
\r
##### Stage1: SNP callng :\r
\r
First, we select a low-error-rate sequencing dataset as the target sample for rapid SNP calling. It supports multi-threaded processing.\r
\r
Example:\r
\r
```bash\r
./run.sh -i /data/hg002.fastq.gz -m 0\r
```\r
\r
```bash\r
    Required parameters:\r
      -i:        Input files ( *.fastq or *.fastq.gz files)\r
      -m:        Heterozygosity parameter (0 for <1.2%, 1 otherwise)\r
    Optional parameters:\r
      -k:        kmer-size (default: 21)\r
      -t:        thread (default: 8)"\r
      -o:        Output prefix (defaults: first input file's prefix)\r
      -d1:       Directory for dsk files (default: current directory)\r
      -d2:       Directory for output plot (default: current directory)\r
      -d3:       Directory for SNP output (default: current directory)\r
      -h:        Show this help message\r
    Advanced optional parameters:\r
      -est:      est_kmercov (default: Estimated by algorithm)\r
      -cutoff:   cutoff threshold (defaults: 0.95)\r
      -het:      Initial heterozygosity (defaults: 0/0.12)\r
      -rho:      Initial rho value (defaults: 0.2)\r
      -setleft:  Left boundary of the heterozygous region (defaults: Estimated by algorithm)\r
      -setright: Right boundary of the heterozygous region (defaults: Estimated by algorithm)\r
```\r
\r
##### Stage1: construct variant sketch:\r
\r
Next, we convert the called SNPs into a variant sketch.\r
\r
```bash\r
./create -i /snp/hg002_21_2_4_pairex.snp\r
```\r
\r
```bash\r
    Required parameters:\r
      -i:        Input files ( .snp file)\r
    Optional parameters:\r
      -k:        kmer-size (default: 21)\r
      -l:        Filtering threshold (default: 21)\r
      -o:        Output prefix (defaults: current directory)\r
```\r
\r
##### Stage2: count the k-mers:\r
\r
we compare the k-mer counts of other cohort samples to the variant sketch to infer relationships between them. Files may be gzipped and multiple threads can be used.\r
\r
```bash\r
./pisadCount -s /fa/hg002.fa /data/hg003.fastq.gz\r
```\r
\r
```bash\r
    Usage: ./pisadCount -s [FASTA] [OPTION]... [FILES...]\r
    Required options:\r
        -s:         variant sketch (one or more)\r
    Optional options:\r
        -t:      Number of threads to run (default: 1)\r
        -m:      k-mer coverage threshold for early termination (default: inf)\r
        -i:      extra debug information\r
        -k:      k-mer size used (default: 21)\r
        -o:      Evaluation file path (defaults: current directory)\r
        -h:      Display this dialog\r
\r
```\r
\r
Here, the -s option allows inputting multiple FA files for variant sketching, separated by commas, such as `-s /fa/hg002.fa,/fa/hg001.fa`.\r
If your input file has a high coverage, you can also add the `-m` parameter to control the reading process and save time, such as `-m 10`.\r
\r
##### Stage2:Evaluate the samples:\r
\r
Input the statistics of samples to calculate their relationship and detect sample swaps.\r
\r
```bash\r
./pisadEval /homeb/xuzt/coverage/eval/hg002_hg003.txt > summary.tsv\r
```\r
\r
```bash\r
    Usage: ./pisadEval [OPTION]... [FILES...]\r
    Optional options:\r
        -t:      Number of threads to run(default: 1)\r
        -h:      Display this dialog\r
\r
```\r
""" ;
