    ns1:description """Digital Twin Component for Earthquakes (DTC-E) WorkFlow 2 (WF7502) generates synthetic shaking simulations (shakemaps) for seismic sources at different time scales, and automatically incorporates new data as it becomes available.\r
\r
The workflow consists of multiple steps (ST), datasets (DT), and software services (SS). Below is a simplified breakdown:\r
\r
**Data Ingestion & Preprocessing**\r
* ST750201: Assimilates real-time earthquake data from external sources.\r
* ST750202: Extracts earthquake source parameters.\r
* SS7502 - SS7504: Software services supporting data extraction.\r
\r
**HPC Simulations & Hazard Mapping**\r
* ST750203: Prepares input parameters for simulations.\r
* ST750204: Runs high-performance computing (HPC) earthquake simulations.\r
* SS7501: Simulating software salvus by mondaic.com.\r
* ST750207: Generates synthetic shaking maps based on ML (MLESmap).\r
\r
**Dynamic Updates & Urgent Computing**\r
* ST750205: Evaluates real-time event updates.\r
* ST750206: Enables urgent computing for time-sensitive simulations.\r
\r
**Post-Processing & Output Storage**\r
* ST750208: Post-processes the results.\r
* ST750209: Gathers final outputs.\r
* ST750210: Updates the Shake Maps Library (DT7502).""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1332" ;
    ns1:keywords "dt-geo",
        "geophysics",
        "solid earth" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E5] WF7502: Synthetic shaking simulation or Event-DT" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1332?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1333?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Andreas Fichtner",
        "Christian Böhm",
        "Johannes Kemper",
        "Marta Pienkowska",
        "Scott Keating" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-04-10" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """Digitial Twin Component for Earthquakes (DTC-E5) WorkFlow 1 (WF7501) uses new data to create a multi-scale model of the Earth, which is used to update the REVEAL/CSEMv3 velocity model. \r
The WF7501 workflow models an inversion-based update process for CSEMv3 (Computational Seismic Earth Model).\r
Below is a simple explanation of the STEPs:\r
\r
* **ST750101 - Data Catalog Update**: Collects external data sources and updates the database for further processing.\r
* **ST750102 - Inversion Setup**: Configures inversion parameters at local, regional, and global levels.\r
* **ST750103 - Model Extraction**: Extracts an initial computational model from the setup parameters.\r
* **ST750104 - Inversion Iterations**: Performs multiple iterations to refine the extracted model.\r
* **ST750105 - Model Update**: Updates the CSEM model using the results from the inversion process.\r
* **ST750106 - User Model Validation**: The refined model undergoes external validation to ensure accuracy and usability.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1333" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophysics" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E5] WF7501: Full waveform inversion of Earth model or Earth-DT" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1333?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1336?version=3> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.1336.3" ;
    ns1:creator "Lune Bellec",
        "Natasha Clarke" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-06-11" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-06-17" ;
    ns1:description """# Code for the high risk autism phenotype paper\r
[![MIT license](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-blue.svg)](https://lbesson.mit-license.org/)\r
\r
This repository implements a **fully reproducible pipeline** for the autism signature project. It uses `invoke` tasks and a Docker container for **consistent, cross-platform execution.**\r
\r
The entire workflow—data fetching, processing, and figure generation—can be **reproduced in a few commands.**\r
Much of the code in this repo originated from [ASD High Risk Endophenotype Code Supplement](https://github.com/surchs/ASD_high_risk_endophenotype_code_supplement) and was written by Sebastian Urchs and Hien Nguyen.\r
All data to reproduce the analysis can be downloaded from [![DOI](https://zenodo.org/badge/DOI/10.5281/zenodo.15517157.svg)](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15517157)  \r
\r
- The study uses data from ABIDE 1 and 2 datasets. Participants were matched using propensity score matching as part of another project, matching scripts can be found here [ASD Subtype Code Supplement](https://github.com/surchs/ASD_subtype_code_supplement/tree/master/scripts/pheno).\r
- Resting state functional connectivity data was preprocessed using NIAK, described in the paper. This study uses the seed maps.\r
- Using the following scripts the full analysis can be reproduced. Alternatively, to skip the data analysis part and recreate the figures, download only the results and atlas data from Zenodo.\r
\r
\r
## ?? Quick Start\r
\r
### 1⃣ Install invoke\r
\r
First, install `invoke`:\r
\r
```bash\r
pip install invoke\r
```\r
\r
You must also have either Docker or Apptainer installed to use container-based execution.\r
\r
### 4⃣ Clean Everything\r
\r
To remove all generated data:\r
\r
```bash\r
invoke clean-all\r
```\r
\r
## ?? Folder Structure\r
\r
| Folder                        | Description                                              |\r
| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\r
| `source_data/`                | Raw data: Atlases & fMRI data.                           |\r
| `output_data/`                | All generated outputs: Discovery results, figures, etc.  |\r
| `code/figures/`               | Jupyter notebooks used to generate all figures.          |\r
| `output_data/Figures/`        | Output folders for each figure notebook.                 |\r
| `tasks.py` / `tasks_utils.py` | The heart of the pipeline: all `invoke` tasks live here. |\r
\r
## Building the environment\r
\r
### Installing dependencies\r
To set up everything, ensure functional Python and R environments and run:\r
\r
```bash\r
invoke setup-all\r
```\r
\r
This:\r
\r
* sets up Python & R environments (if running locally);\r
* prepares the folder structure.\r
\r
**Note:**\r
This task assumes an Ubuntu-like OS. You still need to install R, Python, etc. See the Dockerfile for complete setup info.\r
\r
**Note 2:**\r
You can skip this if using the Docker container and running `docker-run` directly.\r
\r
### Create a Docker image\r
To build a Docker image:\r
\r
```bash\r
invoke docker-build\r
```\r
\r
To generate a compressed archive:\r
\r
```bash\r
invoke docker-archive\r
```\r
\r
### Create an Apptainer image\r
After building the Docker image, run:\r
\r
```bash\r
invoke apptainer-archive\r
```\r
\r
This builds the `.sif` image from the Docker daemon.\r
""" ;
