    ns1:description """<p align="center">\r
  <img src="gene_fetch_logo.svg" width="400" alt="gene_fetch_logo">\r
</p>\r
\r
[![PyPI version](https://img.shields.io/pypi/v/gene-fetch.svg)](https://pypi.org/project/gene-fetch/)\r
[![Install with bioconda](https://img.shields.io/badge/install%20with-bioconda-brightgreen.svg?style=flat)](http://bioconda.github.io/recipes/gene-fetch/README.html)\r
[![JOSS DOI](https://joss.theoj.org/papers/10.21105/joss.08456/status.svg)](https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.08456)\r
[![Github Action test](https://github.com/bge-barcoding/gene_fetch/workflows/Test%20gene-fetch/badge.svg)](https://github.com/bge-barcoding/gene_fetch/actions)\r
[![Zenodo archive DOI](https://zenodo.org/badge/DOI/10.5281/zenodo.16759414.svg)](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16759414)\r
\r
\r
# GeneFetch \r
Gene Fetch enables high-throughput retreival of sequence data from NCBI's GenBank sequence database based on taxonomy IDs (taxids) or taxonomic heirarchies (phylum->species). It can retrieve protein and/or nucleotide sequences for various 'supported' loci (including protein-coding genes (e.g., cox1, cytb, rbcl, matk) and rRNA genes (e.g., 16S, 18S). Gene Fetch can be run for 'unsupported' loci, although the quality of the returned sequence data cannot be guaranteed. \r
\r
---\r
\r
## Highlight features\r
- Fetch protein and/or nucleotide sequences from NCBI's GenBank database without constructing NCBI search terms.\r
- Handles both direct nucleotide sequence searching, and protein-linked nucleotide searches (CDS extraction includes fallback mechanisms for atypical annotation formats).\r
- Seqeunce matches are made by searching for the target gene and/ protein in the GenBank annotation (feature table). \r
- Contains customisable length filtering thresholds for protein and nucleotide sequences.\r
- Default "batch" mode processes multiple input taxa based on a user-specified CSV file, as well as "single" mode (-s/--single) for retrieving a specified number of target sequences for a particular taxon.\r
- Implements automatic taxonomy traversal ("batch" mode only), utilising the returned NCBI taxonomic lineage for a given taxid when sequences are not found at the input taxonomic level (i.e. If searching at a given taxid level (e.g., species) and no sequences are found, traverse 'up' a rank (species->phylum) until a suitable sequence is found).\r
- Validates fetched sequence taxonomy against input taxonomic heirarchy, avoiding potential taxonomic homonyms (i.e. when the same taxa name is used for different taxa across the tree of life).\r
- Handles complex sequence features (e.g., complement strands, joined sequences, WGS entries) in addition to 'simple' cds extaction (if --type nucleotide/both). The tool avoids "unverified" sequences and WGS entries not containing sequence data (i.e. master records).\r
- 'Checkpointing' functionality, so that if a run fails/crashes, gene-fetch can be rerun using the same arguments and parameters to resume from where it stopped (unless `--clean` is specified).\r
- When more than 50 matching GenBank records are found for a sample, the tool fetches summary information for all matches (using NCBI esummary API), orders the records by sequence length, and processes the longest sequences first.\r
- Can output corresponding genbank (.gb) files for each fetched nucleotide and/or protein sequences.\r
- Optional detail in FASTA sequence headers of retrieved sequences.\r
- Robust error handling, progress tracking, and logging, with compliance to NCBI API rate limits (10 requests/second). Caches taxonomy lookups for reduced API calls.\r
\r
---\r
