\r
## Installation\r
- Due to the risk of dependency conflicts, it's recommended to install Gene Fetch in a Conda environment.\r
- First Conda needs to be installed, which can be done from [here](https://www.anaconda.com/docs/getting-started/miniconda/install).\r
- Once installed:\r
```bash\r
# Create new environment\r
conda create -n gene-fetch\r
\r
# Activate environment\r
conda activate gene-fetch\r
```\r
\r
- Gene Fetch and all necessary dependencies can then be installed via [Bioconda](https://anaconda.org/bioconda/gene-fetch), [PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/gene-fetch/#description), or by specifying `environment.yaml`:\r
```bash\r
# Install via bioconda\r
conda install bioconda::gene-fetch\r
\r
# Or, install via pip\r
pip install gene-fetch\r
\r
# Or, via environment specification\r
conda env update --name gene-fetch -f environment.yaml --prune\r
\r
# Verify installation\r
gene-fetch --help\r
```\r
\r
- If you would rather clone this repository and run a standalone version of Gene Fetch for some reason, you can do that as follows:\r
```bash\r
# Clone the repository\r
git clone https://github.com/bge-barcoding/gene_fetch.git\r
cd gene_fetch\r
\r
# Activate conda environment (once created), and install gene-fetch (+ dependencies) via your preferred method. See `environment.yaml` for list of dependencies.\r
\r
# Run standalone Gene Fetch:\r
python /path/to/gene_fetch.py [options]\r
\r
```\r
\r
---\r
\r
## Recommended: Testing\r
- The Gene Fetch package includes some basic tests for each module that we recommend are run after installation.\r
```bash\r
# Clone the repository\r
git clone https://github.com/bge-barcoding/gene_fetch.git\r
cd gene_fetch\r
\r
# Install pytest\r
pip install pytest\r
\r
# [Optional] Locally install Gene Fetch in editable mode from source (when inside `gene_fetch`) - enables testing of source code in development\r
pip install -e .\r
\r
# Run tests\r
pytest\r
```\r
* This will take a few minutes to run the tests. You will get 1 warning regarding API credentials as these are not provided in the basic tests.\r
\r
---\r
\r
## Usage\r
```bash\r
gene-fetch --gene <gene_name> --type <sequence_type> --in <samples.csv> --out <output_directory> --email example@example.co.uk --api-key 1234567890\r
```\r
* `--help`: Show usage help and exit.\r
\r
### Required arguments\r
* `-g/--gene`: Name of gene to search for in NCBI GenBank database (e.g., cox1/16s/rbcl).\r
* `-t/--type`: Sequence type to fetch; 'protein', 'nucleotide', or 'both' ('both' will initially search and fetch a protein sequence, and then fetches the corresponding nucleotide CDS for that protein sequence).\r
* `-i/--in`: Path to input CSV file containing sample IDs and TaxIDs (see [Input](#input) section below).\r
* `-i2/--in2`: Path to alternative input CSV file containing sample IDs and taxonomic information for each sample (see [Input](#input) section below).\r
* `o/--out`: Path to output directory. The directory will be created if it does not exist.\r
* `e/--email` and `-k/--api-key`: Email address and associated API key for NCBI account. An NCBI account is required to run this tool (due to otherwise strict API limitations) - information on how to create an NCBI account and find your API key can be found [here](https://support.nlm.nih.gov/kbArticle/?pn=KA-05317).\r
### Optional arguments\r
* `-ps/--protein-size`: Minimum protein sequence length filter. Applicable to mode 'batch' and 'single' search modes (default: 500aa).\r
* `-ns/--nucleotide-size`: Minimum nucleotide sequence length filter. Applicable to mode 'batch' and 'single' search modes (default: 1000bp).\r
* `s/--single`: Taxonomic ID for 'single' sequence search mode (`-i` and `-i2` are ignored when run with `-s` mode). 'single' mode will fetch all (or N if specifying `--max-sequences`) target gene or protein sequences on GenBank for a specific taxonomic ID.\r
* `-ms/--max-sequences`: Maximum number of sequences to fetch for a specific taxonomic ID (only applies when run in 'single' mode).\r
* `-b/--genbank`: Saves genbank (.gb) files for fetched nucleotide and/or protein sequences to `genbank/` (applies when run in 'batch' or 'single' mode).\r
* `-c/--clear`: Forces clean (re)start by clearing output directory regardless of previous run parameters. If ommiting `--clear` and rerunning gene-fetch with the same arguments and parameters, checkpointing will be enabled.\r
* `--header`: Dictates the format of sequence headers in output FASTA files. 'basic' = '>ID' (default). 'detailed' = '>ID|taxid|accession_number|genbank_description|length'.\r
\r
---\r
\r
## Examples\r
Fetch both protein and nucleotide sequences for COI with default sequence length thresholds, and store the corresponding genbank records.\r
```\r
gene-fetch -e your.email@domain.com -k your_api_key \\\r
            -g cox1 -o ./output_dir -i ./data/samples.csv \\\r
            --type both --genbank\r
```\r
\r
Fetch COI nucleotide sequences using sample taxonomic information, applying a minimum nucleotide sequence length of 1000bp\r
```\r
gene-fetch -e your.email@domain.com -k your_api_key \\\r
            -g cox1 -o ./output_dir -i2 ./data/samples_taxonomy.csv \\\r
            --type nucleotide --nucleotide-size 1000\r
```\r
\r
Retrieve 100 available rbcL protein sequences >400aa for _Arabidopsis thaliana_ (taxid: 3702).\r
```\r
gene-fetch -e your.email@domain.com -k your_api_key \\\r
            -g rbcL -o ./output_dir -s 3702 \\\r
            --type protein --protein-size 400 --max-sequences 100\r
```\r
\r
---\r
\r
## Input\r
**Example 'samples.csv' input file (-i/--in)**\r
| ID | taxid |\r
| --- | --- |\r
| sample-1  | 177658 |\r
| sample-2 | 177627 |\r
| sample-3 | 3084599 |\r
\r
**Example 'samples_taxonomy.csv' input file (-i2/--in2)**\r
| ID | phylum | class | order | family | genus | species |\r
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\r
| sample-1  | Arthropoda | Insecta | Diptera | Acroceridae | Astomella | |\r
| sample-2 | Arthropoda | Insecta | Hemiptera | Cicadellidae | Psammotettix | Psammotettix sabulicola |\r
| sample-3 | Arthropoda | Insecta | Trichoptera | Limnephilidae | Dicosmoecus | Dicosmoecus palatus |\r
* Leave blank if taxonomic information not known/needed. At least one rank must be supplied for each sample.\r
\r
## Output\r
### 'Batch' mode\r
```\r
output_dir/\r
├── genbank/                    # Genbank (.gb) files for each fetched nucleotide and/or protein sequence.\r
│   ├── nucleotide/  \r
│   ├── protein/  \r
├── nucleotide/                 # Nucleotide sequences. Only populated if '--type nucleotide/both' utilised.\r
│   ├── sample-1.fasta   \r
│   ├── sample-2.fasta\r
│   └── ...\r
├── protein/                    # Protein sequences. Only populated if '--type protein/both' utilised.\r
│   ├── sample-1.fasta   \r
│   ├── sample-2.fasta\r
│   └── ...\r
├── sequence_references.csv     # Sequence metadata.\r
├── failed_searches.csv         # Failed search attempts (if any).\r
└── gene_fetch.log              # Log.\r
```\r
\r
**sequence_references.csv output example**\r
| ID | input_taxa | first_matched_taxid | first_matched_taxid_rank | protein_accession | protein_length | nucleotide_accession | nucleotide_length | matched_rank | ncbi_taxonomy | reference_name | protein_reference_path | nucleotide_reference_path |\r
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\r
| sample-1 | Apatania | 177658 | genus:Apatania | AHF21732.1 | 510 | KF756944.1 | 1530 | genus:Apatania | Eukaryota; ...; Apataniinae; Apatania | sample-1 | abs/path/to/protein_references/sample-1.fasta | abs/path/to/protein_references/sample-1_dna.fasta |\r
| sample-2 | Isoptena serricornis | 2719103 | species:Isoptena serricornis | QNE85983.1 | 518 | MT410852.1 | 1557 | species:Isoptena serricornis | Eukaryota; ...; Chloroperlinae; Isoptena | sample-2 | abs/path/to/protein_references/sample-2.fasta | abs/path/to/protein_references/sample-2_dna.fasta |\r
| sample-3 | Triaenodes conspersus | 1876143 | species:Triaenodes conspersus | YP_009526503.1 | 512 | NC_039659.1 | 1539 | genus:Triaenodes | Eukaryota; ...; Triaenodini; Triaenodes | sample-3 | abs/path/to/protein_references/sample-3.fasta | abs/path/to/protein_references/sample-3_dna.fasta |\r
```\r
* ID - The unique identifier (ID) for each sample (from the input CSV)\r
* input_taxa - The taxon name searched for (e.g., "Apatania" == taxid 177658), or the taxon name for the closest valid taxid found.\r
* first_matched_taxid - The NCBI taxonomic ID that was searched (same as the taxid from the --in CSV, or the closest valid taxid if using --in2 as input)\r
* first_matched_taxid_rank - The taxonomic rank and name of the first_matched_taxid (e.g., "genus:Astomella")\r
* protein_accession - The NCBI accession number of the protein sequence retrieved (if applicable)\r
* protein_length - Length of the protein sequence in amino acids (if applicable)\r
* nucleotide_accession - The NCBI accession number of the nucleotide sequence retrieved (if applicable)\r
* nucleotide_length - Length of the nucleotide sequence in base pairs (if applicable)\r
* matched_rank - The taxonomic rank where sequences were actually found (e.g., "family:Acroceridae" if no sequences existed at the the proceeding rank, and the search traversed up the taxonomy tree)\r
* ncbi_taxonomy - The complete NCBI taxonomic lineage for the retrieved sequence (semicolon-separated)\r
* reference_name - Copy of the ID (for reference purposes)\r
* protein_reference_path - Full file path to the saved protein FASTA file (if applicable)\r
* nucleotide_reference_path - Full file path to the saved nucleotide FASTA file (if applicable)\r
```\r
\r
### 'Single' mode\r
```\r
output_dir/\r
├── genbank/                         # Genbank (.gb) files for each fetched nucleotide and/or protein sequence.\r
├── nucleotide/                      # Nucleotide sequences. Only populated if '--type nucleotide/both' utilised.\r
│   ├── ACCESSION1_dna.fasta   \r
│   ├── ACCESSION2_dna.fasta\r
│   └── ...\r
├── ACCESSION1.fasta                 # Protein sequences.\r
├── ACCESSION2.fasta\r
├── fetched_nucleotide_sequences.csv # Sequence metadata. Only populated if '--type nucleotide/both' utilised.\r
├── fetched_protein_sequences.csv    # Sequence metadata. Only populated if '--type protein/both' utilised.\r
├── failed_searches.csv              # Failed search attempts (if any).\r
└── gene_fetch.log                   # Log.\r
```\r
\r
**fetched_protein|nucleotide_sequences.csv output example**\r
| ID | length | Description | searched_taxid\r
| --- | --- | --- | --- |\r
| PQ645072.1 | 1501 | Ochlerotatus nigripes isolate Pool11 cytochrome c oxidase subunit I (COX1) gene, partial cds; mitochondrial | 508662 |\r
| PQ645071.1 | 1537 | Ochlerotatus nigripes isolate Pool10 cytochrome c oxidase subunit I (COX1) gene, partial cds; mitochondrial | 508662 |\r
| PQ645070.1 | 1501 | Ochlerotatus impiger isolate Pool2 cytochrome c oxidase subunit I (COX1) gene, partial cds; mitochondrial | 508662 |\r
| PQ645069.1 | 1518	| Ochlerotatus impiger isolate Pool1 cytochrome c oxidase subunit I (COX1) gene, partial cds; mitochondrial | 508662 |\r
\r
---\r
\r
## Running Gene Fetch on a cluster\r
- See 'gene_fetch.sh' for running gene_fetch.py on a HPC cluster (SLURM job schedular). \r
- Edit 'mem' and/or 'cpus-per-task' to set memory and CPU/threads - allocating lots of CPUs is unecessary as Gene Fetch is not paralellised (yet). The tool should run well with 4-10G memory and 1-2 CPUs.\r
- Change paths and variables as needed.\r
- Run 'gene_fetch.sh' with:\r
```\r
sbatch gene_fetch.sh\r
```\r
\r
---\r
\r
## Supported targets\r
GeneFetch includes the following 'hard-coded' search terms with common name variations for 'smarter' searching of the targets listed below. \r
- cox1/COI/cytochrome c oxidase subunit I\r
- cox2/COII/cytochrome c oxidase subunit II\r
- cox3/COIII/cytochrome c oxidase subunit III\r
- cytb/cob/cytochrome b\r
- nd1/NAD1/NADH dehydrogenase subunit 1\r
- nd2/NAD2/NADH dehydrogenase subunit 2\r
- rbcL/RuBisCO/ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase large subunit\r
- matK/maturase K\r
- psbA/photosystem II protein D1\r
- 16S ribosomal RNA/16s\r
- SSU/18s\r
- LSU/28s\r
- 23s\r
- 12S ribosomal RNA/12s\r
- ITS (ITS1-5.8S-ITS2)\r
- ITS1/internal transcribed spacer 1\r
- ITS2/internal transcribed spacer 2\r
- tRNA-Leucine/trnL\r
\r
\r
Gene/protein targets not listed can also be searched, however, Gene Fetch will implement a more generic search term/strategy with `{target}[Title] OR {target}[Gene] OR {target}[Protein Name]`.\r
Additional targets can be added if required - see `self._rRNA_genes` and '`self._protein_coding_genes` dictionaries within 'class config' (in `src/gene_fetch/core.py`) for example search terms to construct your own. You are welcome to open an [Issue](https://github.com/bge-barcoding/gene_fetch/issues/new) or create a pull request with your search term for inclusion into the main Gene Fetch release (see [Contributions and guidelines](https://github.com/bge-barcoding/gene_fetch?tab=readme-ov-file#contributions-and-guidelines) section below.\r
\r
---\r
\r
## Benchmarking\r
| Sample Description | Run Mode | Target | Input File | Data Type | Memory | CPUs | Run Time (hh:mm:ss) |\r
|--------------------|----------|--------|------------|-----------|--------|------|----------|\r
| 570 Arthropod samples | Batch | COI | taxonomy.csv | Both | 4G | 1 | 01:34:47 |\r
| 570 Arthropod samples | Batch | COI | samples.csv | Both (+ genbank) | 4G | 1 | 01:42:37 |\r
| 570 Arthropod samples | Batch | COI | samples.csv | Nucleotide | 4G | 1 | 1:07:53  |\r
| 570 Arthropod samples | Batch | ND1 | samples.csv | Nucleotide (>500bp) | 4G | 1 | 1:23:26 |\r
| All available (30) _A. thaliana_ sequences | Single | rbcL | N/A | Protein (>300aa) | 4G | 1 | 00:00:25 |\r
| 1000 Culicidae sequences | Single | COI | N/A | nucleotide (>500bp) | 4G | 1 | 0031:05 |\r
| 1000 _M. tubercolisis_ sequences | Single | 16S | N/A | nucleotide | 4G | 1 | 01:23:54 |\r
* All benchmarking runs were performed on a SLURM-managed HPC cluster running Debian 12 ("Bookworm), with each job allocated a modest 1 CPU and 4 GB RAM.\r
\r
