<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1359?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:applicationSubCategory <http://edamontology.org/topic_2269>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3307>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3474> ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-05-09" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-05-09" ;
    ns1:description """# CausalCoxMGM\r
Implementation of CausalCoxMGM algorithm and scripts for analysis of simulated and real-world biomedical datasets.\r
\r
## Installation\r
\r
To install CoxMGM and CausalCoxMGM, run the following command in the terminal:\r
\r
```\r
R CMD INSTALL rCausalMGM\r
```\r
\r
or alternatively:\r
\r
```\r
R CMD INSTALL rCausalMGM/rCausalMGM_1.0.tar.gz\r
```\r
\r
## Demonstration of CausalCoxMGM with the WHAS500 dataset\r
\r
\r
First, we begin by loading the necessray R packages for this analysis.\r
\r
\r
``` r\r
library(rCausalMGM)\r
library(survival)\r
library(dplyr)\r
library(survminer)\r
library(ggplot2)\r
```\r
\r
Next, we load the WHAS500 dataset and format the data appropriately for analysis by CausalCoxMGM. First, we create our censored variables, Survival and LengthOfStay, using the `Surv` function from the `survival` R package. Then, we exclude features that are not clinical measurements or patient outcomes, and convert discrete variables into factors.\r
\r
\r
``` r\r
data <- read.csv('cvd/whas500.csv', row.names=1)\r
\r
data$LengthOfStay <- Surv(data$los, 1-data$dstat)\r
\r
data$Survival <- Surv(data$lenfol, data$fstat)\r
\r
data <- data %>% select(-c("admitdate", "disdate", "fdate", "dstat", "lenfol", "fstat", "year", "los"))\r
\r
data <- data %>% mutate_at(c("cvd", "afb", "sho", "chf", "av3", "gender", "miord", "mitype"), factor)\r
\r
head(data)\r
```\r
\r
```\r
##   age gender hr sysbp diasbp      bmi cvd afb sho chf av3 miord mitype\r
## 1  83      0 89   152     78 25.54051   1   1   0   0   0     1      0\r
## 2  49      0 84   120     60 24.02398   1   0   0   0   0     0      1\r
## 3  70      1 83   147     88 22.14290   0   0   0   0   0     0      1\r
## 4  70      0 65   123     76 26.63187   1   0   0   1   0     0      1\r
## 5  70      0 63   135     85 24.41255   1   0   0   0   0     0      1\r
## 6  70      0 76    83     54 23.24236   1   0   0   0   1     0      0\r
##   LengthOfStay Survival\r
## 1            5    2178+\r
## 2            5    2172+\r
## 3            5    2190+\r
## 4           10      297\r
## 5            6    2131+\r
## 6           1+        1\r
```\r
\r
Next, we perform stratified 5-fold cross-validation to select model hyperparameters. Cross-validation folds are stratified to have approximately the same number of all-cause mortality and hospital discharge events. We measure model performance based on the total deviance of the censored outcomes. Rather than performing a grid search over the hyperparameters, we perform a random search.\r
\r
\r
``` r\r
set.seed(43)\r
\r
idx00 <- which(data$Survival[,2]==0 & data$LengthOfStay[,2]==0)\r
idx10 <- which(data$Survival[,2]==1 & data$LengthOfStay[,2]==0)\r
idx01 <- which(data$Survival[,2]==0 & data$LengthOfStay[,2]==1)\r
idx11 <- which(data$Survival[,2]==1 & data$LengthOfStay[,2]==1)\r
\r
foldid <- rep(0, 500)\r
foldid[idx00] <- sample(((1:length(idx00))-1) %% 5 + 1)\r
foldid[idx10] <- sample(((1:length(idx10))-1) %% 5 + 1)\r
foldid[idx01] <- sample(((1:length(idx01))-1) %% 5 + 1)\r
foldid[idx11] <- sample(((1:length(idx11))-1) %% 5 + 1)\r
\r
table(foldid, data$Survival[,2])\r
```\r
\r
```\r
##       \r
## foldid  0  1\r
##      1 57 44\r
##      2 57 43\r
##      3 57 43\r
##      4 57 43\r
##      5 57 42\r
```\r
\r
``` r\r
table(foldid, data$LengthOfStay[,2])\r
```\r
\r
```\r
##       \r
## foldid  0  1\r
##      1  8 93\r
##      2  8 92\r
##      3  8 92\r
##      4  8 92\r
##      5  7 92\r
```\r
\r
``` r\r
lambdas <- runif(100, 0.05, 0.5)\r
\r
alphas <- runif(100, 0.01, 0.25)\r
\r
loglik <- matrix(0, 100, 5)\r
size <- matrix(0, 100, 5)\r
for (k in 1:5) {\r
    ig.path <- coxmgmPath(data[foldid!=k,], lambdas=lambdas, rank=F)\r
    idx <- 0\r
    for (ig in ig.path$graphs) {\r
        idx <- idx + 1\r
        g <- fciStable(data[foldid!=k,], initialGraph=ig,\r
                       alpha=alphas[idx], orientRule="maxp", rank=F)\r
        mb <- g$markov.blankets$Survival\r
        size[idx,k] <- length(mb)\r
        if (length(mb)==1) {\r
            mb <- c(1)\r
        }\r
        f <- as.formula(paste("Survival ~", paste(mb, collapse=" + ")))\r
        res <- coxph(f, data[foldid!=k,])\r
        test.risk <- predict(res, newdata=data[foldid==k,])\r
        res.test <- coxph(Survival ~ offset(test.risk), data[foldid==k,])\r
        loglik[idx,k] <- -as.numeric(logLik(res.test))\r
\r
        mb <- g$markov.blankets$LengthOfStay\r
        size[idx,k] <- size[idx,k] + length(mb)\r
        if (length(mb)==1) {\r
            mb <- c(1)\r
        }\r
        f <- as.formula(paste("LengthOfStay ~", paste(mb, collapse=" + ")))\r
        res <- coxph(f, data[foldid!=k,])\r
        test.risk <- predict(res, newdata=data[foldid==k,])\r
        res.test <- coxph(LengthOfStay ~ offset(test.risk), data[foldid==k,])\r
        loglik[idx,k] <- loglik[idx,k] + -as.numeric(logLik(res.test))\r
    }\r
}\r
\r
sizeMean <- rowMeans(size)\r
loglikMean <- rowMeans(loglik)\r
loglikSd <- apply(loglik, 1, sd)\r
\r
plot(sizeMean, loglikMean, pch=19, col='red')\r
```\r
\r
![plot of chunk modelselectcv](figure/modelselectcv-1.png)\r
\r
``` r\r
minIdx <- which.min(loglikMean)\r
```\r
\r
Now that we have selected the best set of hyperparameters, we learn the final causal graphical model of all-cause mortality and hospital discharge after hospitilazation with acute myocardial infarction. This is done in two stages: first, we learn the undirected CoxMGM that serves as an initial estimate of the adjacencies in the causal graph. Second, we use FCI-Max to prune adjacencies and orient edges.\r
\r
\r
``` r\r
ig.path <- coxmgmPath(data, lambda=lambdas)\r
\r
g <- fciStable(data, initialGraph=ig.path$graphs[[minIdx]],\r
               alpha=alphas[minIdx], verbose=T, orientRule="maxp")\r
```\r
\r
```\r
## Starting FCI-Stable algorithm...\r
##   Starting FAS Stable...\r
##     Searching at depth 0...\r
##     Searching at depth 1...\r
##     Searching at depth 2...\r
##     Searching at depth 3...\r
##     Searching at depth 4...\r
##   FAS Stable Elapsed Time =  0.029 s\r
##   RFCI adjacency pruning...\r
##   Starting Posssible DSep search\r
##     Starting Conservative Orientations...\r
##       Filling Triple Map...\r
##     Orienting colliders...\r
##     Checking Possible-Dsep sets...\r
##   Starting Final Orientations...\r
##     Filling Triple Map...\r
##   Orienting colliders...\r
##   Orienting implied edges...\r
##   FCI-Stable Elapsed Time =  0.25 s\r
```\r
\r
``` r\r
g\r
```\r
\r
```\r
## Algorithm:  CoxMGM-FCI-Max \r
## Nodes:  15 \r
## Edges:  17 \r
##   Unoriented:  4 \r
##   Partially Oriented:  6 \r
##   Directed:  6 \r
##   Bidirected:  1 \r
## lambda = {0.3173977, 0.3173977, 0.3173977, 0.3173977, 0.3173977}\r
## alpha =  0.02566852\r
```\r
\r
Finally, we can generate a simple plot of the causal graphical model in R.\r
\r
\r
``` r\r
plot(g, nodeAttr=list(fontsize=36))\r
```\r
\r
![plot of chunk finalmodelplot](figure/finalmodelplot-1.png)\r
\r
## License\r
\r
The following data files are under the CC0 public domain:\r
\r
```\r
whas500.csv\r
metabric.rna.full.csv\r
metabric.rna.erp.full.csv\r
metabric.rna.ern.full.csv\r
meta_cohort_common_genes.rds\r
```\r
\r
While all other files composing CausalCoxMGM are under the GPL-3.0 license.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1359" ;
    ns1:license "GPL-3.0" ;
    ns1:name "CausalCoxMGM" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1359?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

