\r
## <a name="getting-started"></a>Getting Started\r
\r
To get started with BVSim, follow these steps to install and run the simulator:\r
\r
```sh\r
# Create an envrionment called BVSim and install the dependencies\r
conda create -n BVSim python=3.11 numpy pandas biopython scipy seaborn psutil\r
conda activate BVSim\r
# Run the following to install pysam or use the latest guide\r
conda config --add channels defaults\r
conda config --add channels conda-forge\r
conda config --add channels bioconda\r
conda install pysam\r
# Installzation\r
## Clone the repository in your home path\r
cd your_home_path\r
git clone https://github.com/YongyiLuo98/BVSim.git\r
## Navigate to the ~/BVSim/main directory and install the package\r
pip install your_home_path/BVSim/main/.\r
\r
# Verify the installation in your home path\r
cd your_home_path\r
python -m BVSim --help\r
python -m BVSim -h\r
\r
## Run a toy example with a specified reference in the cloned folder\r
conda activate BVSim\r
python -m BVSim -ref 'your_home_path/BVSim/empirical/sub_hg19_chr1.fasta' -seed 0 -rep 0 -write -snp 2000\r
## If you prefer using the default reference, simply execute\r
cd your_home_path\r
python -m BVSim\r
\r
\r
# Generate variations with specific parameters\r
cd your_home_path\r
python -m BVSim -seed 1 -rep 1 -snp 2000\r
\r
# To write out the relative positions, use the following command\r
python your_home_path/BVSim/main/write_SV.py your_home_path/BVSim/save/ BV_1_con0_chr1_SVtable.csv BV_1_con0_chr1_tem_ins_dic.npy\r
\r
# Create a block intervals BED file\r
cd your_home_path\r
echo -e "0\\t1000\\n3000\\t4000" > block_intervals.bed\r
\r
# Run the simulator with block regions\r
cd your_home_path\r
python -m BVSim -seed 1 -rep 1 -write -snp 2000 -block_region_bed_url block_intervals.bed\r
```\r
\r
## <a name="Installation"></a>Installation\r
### Create an envrionment called BVSim and install the dependencies\r
To start with, you need to install the dependent packages in an environment, for example called BVSim.\r
```bash\r
# Create an envrionment called BVSim and install the dependencies\r
conda create -n BVSim python=3.11 numpy pandas biopython scipy seaborn psutil\r
conda activate BVSim\r
# Run the following to install pysam or use the latest guide\r
conda config --add channels defaults\r
conda config --add channels conda-forge\r
conda config --add channels bioconda\r
conda install pysam\r
```\r
### Clone the Repository\r
Next, you need to clone the BVSim repository to your local machine. Execute the following command in your home directory:\r
```bash\r
cd your_home_path\r
git clone https://github.com/YongyiLuo98/BVSim.git\r
```\r
### Navigate to the Main Directory and Install the Package\r
Next, navigate to the .../BVSim/main/ directory to install the package:\r
```bash\r
pip install your_home_path/BVSim/main/.\r
```\r
### Verify the Installation\r
After installation, you can verify it from your home directory. Execute the following commands:\r
```bash\r
cd\r
python -m BVSim --help\r
python -m BVSim -h\r
```\r
Note: You can only call BVSim in the cloned repository directory, while the installation must take place in the BVSim/main/ directory.\r
#### Toy Example (Uniform mode):\r
```bash\r
conda activate BVSim\r
python -m BVSim -ref 'your_home_path/BVSim/empirical/sub_hg19_chr1.fasta' -seed 0 -rep 0 -write -snp 2000\r
```\r
or you can use the default reference to test the installation by type the following in your home path. If you do not give a saving path, the outputs will go to "your_home_path\\BVSim\\save\\".\r
\r
```bash\r
cd your_home_path\r
python -m BVSim \r
```\r
## <a name="parameters"></a>Functions and Parameters\r
\r
Five modes: uniform, uniform parallel, csv, wave, wave_region\r
\r
### <a name="shared-parameters"></a>Shared Parameters\r
The BVSim package provides several functions (modes) and parameters for simulating genetic variations. Here is a table that introduces all the functions and different parameters:\r
\r
| Parameter | Type | Description | Default |\r
| --- | --- | --- | --- |\r
| `-ref` | str | Input reference file | '.../BVSim/empirical/sub_hg19_chr1.fasta' |\r
| `-save` | str | Saving path | .../BVSim/save/ |\r
| `-seed` | int | Seed for random number generator | 999 |\r
| `-times` | int | Number of times | 10 |\r
| `-rep` | int | Replication ID | 5 |\r
| `-sv_trans` | int | Number of trans SV | 5 |\r
| `-sv_inver` | int | Number of inversion SV | 5 |\r
| `-sv_dup` | int | Number of tandem duplication | 5 |\r
| `-sv_del` | int | Number of SV deletion | 5 |\r
| `-sv_ins` | int | Number of SV insertion | 5 |\r
| `-snp` | float | SNV number or probability | 5 |\r
| `-snv_del` | float | SNV deletion number or probability | 5 |\r
| `-snv_ins` | float | SNV insertion number or probability | 5 |\r
| `-notblockN` | bool | Do not Block N positions | False |\r
| `-write` | bool | Write full results | False |\r
| `-delmin` | int | Minimum deletion length | 50 |\r
| `-delmax` | int | Maximum deletion length | 60 |\r
| `-insmin` | int | Minimum insertion length | 50 |\r
| `-insmax` | int | Maximum insertion length | 450 |\r
| `-dupmin` | int | Minimum duplication length | 50 |\r
| `-dupmax` | int | Maximum duplication length | 450 |\r
| `-invmin` | int | Minimum inversion length | 50 |\r
| `-invmax` | int | Maximum inversion length | 450 |\r
| `-dupmin` | int | Minimum duplication length | 50 |\r
| `-dupmax` | int | Maximum duplication length | 450 |\r
| `-transmin` | int | Minimum translocation length | 50 |\r
| `-transmax` | int | Maximum translocation length | 450 |\r
| `-block_region_bed_url` | str | local path of the block region BED file | None |\r
\r
#### <a name="output"></a>Output Naming Conventions\r
When you run the simulation tool, the output files are named based on the sequence name you input or the parameter `rep` you set (repetition number). Below is a summary of the output files you can expect:\r
\r
1. **FASTA File**:  \r
   The output FASTA file will be named as follows:\r
```\r
BV_<rep>_seq_<seqname>.fasta\r
```\r
This file contains the simulated sequence.\r
\r
2. **VCF File**:  \r
The VCF file will be named:\r
```\r
BV_<rep>_seq_<seqname>.vcf\r
```\r
This file stores the simulated variations.\r
\r
3. **SV Table**:  \r
The SV table will have different naming conventions depending on whether you choose to include relative positions:\r
- If you include relative positions (by using the `-write` flag):\r
  ```\r
  BV_<rep>_seq_<seqname>_SVtable_full.csv\r
  ```\r
- If you do not include relative positions:\r
  ```\r
  BV_<rep>_seq_<seqname>_SVtable.csv\r
  ```\r
\r
4. **Numpy File**:  \r
The numpy file that records all inserted segments we need to update the relative positions will be named:\r
```\r
BV_<rep>_seq_<seqname>_tem_ins_dic.npy\r
```\r
#### <a name="write"></a>Write the Relative Positions of Simulated Variations\r
If you choose not to generate the relative positions during the initial simulation run (i.e., you do not include the `-write` flag), the columns for relative positions in the SV table will be empty. However, you can still update these relative positions later using the saved intermediate files.\r
##### Steps to Write Relative Positions After Simulation\r
1. **Run the Initial Simulation**:  \r
For example, you can execute:\r
```bash\r
cd your_home_path\r
python -m BVSim -seed 1 -rep 1 -snp 2000\r
```\r
In this case you generated default number of elementary SVs and micro indels, as well as 20000 SNPs saved in the default directory with `BV_1_seq_chr1_SVtable.csv`, `BV_1_seq_chr1_tem_ins_dic.npy`.\r
\r
2. **Update Relative Positions**:\r
You can then run the following command to generate a table with the relative positions:\r
```bash\r
python your_home_path/BVSim/main/write_SV.py your_home_path/BVSim/save/ BV_1_seq_chr1_SVtable.csv BV_1_seq_chr1_tem_ins_dic.npy\r
```\r
This command will create a file called called `full_BV_1_seq_chr1_SVtable.csv` in the same directory, which will contain the relative positions for all variations with respect to the consensus sequence.\r
By following this naming convention and steps, you can easily manage and update your output files as needed.\r
#### <a name="block"></a>User-defined Block Regions with No Variations\r
The input of the '-block_region_bed_url' should be two columns of positions(start;end) without headers seperated by '\\t'. To create a bed file, you can refer to the following example. In this case, positions from 0 to 999, from 3000 to 3999 cannot have any variation, so called blocked.\r
\r
#### Toy Example:\r
```bash\r
cd your_home_path\r
echo -e "0\\t1000\\n3000\\t4000" > block_intervals.bed\r
# uniform.py\r
cd your_home_path\r
python -m BVSim -seed 1 -rep 1 -write -snp 2000 -block_region_bed_url block_intervals.bed\r
```\r
\r
### <a name="uniform-mode"></a>Uniform Mode\r
If you do not call any of the following parameters (-csv, -cores, -len_bins, -wave), the simulation will be generated one by one uniformly.\r
\r
#### Toy Example (Uniform mode):\r
```bash\r
conda activate BVSim\r
python -m BVSim -ref 'hg19_chr1.fasta' -seed 0 -rep 0 -write -snp 2000\r
```\r
### <a name="complex-sv-mode"></a>Complex SV Mode\r
Add -csv to your command, 18 types of Complex Structure Variations can be generated.\r
\r
* ID1: Tandem Inverted Duplication (TanInvDup)\r
* ID2: Dispersed Inverted Duplication (DisInvDup)\r
* ID3: Dispersed Duplication (DisDup)\r
* ID4: Inversion with 5’ or 3’ Flanking Deletion (DEL+INV/INV+DEL)\r
* ID5: 5’ Deletion and Dispersed Inverted Duplication (DEL+DisInvDup)\r
* ID6: 5’ Deletion and Dispersed Duplication (DEL+DisDup)\r
* ID7: Tandem Duplication and 3’ Deletion (TanDup+DEL)\r
* ID8: Tandem Inverted Duplication and 3’ Deletion (TanInvDup+DEL)\r
* ID9: Tandem Duplication, Deletion and Inversion (TanDup+DEL+INV)\r
* ID10: Tandem Inverted Duplication, Deletion and Inversion (TanInvDup+DEL+INV)\r
* ID11: Paired-Deletion Inversion (DEL+INV+DEL)\r
* ID12: Inversion with 5’ Flanking Duplication (DUP+INV)\r
* ID13: Inversion with 3’ Flanking Duplication (INV+DUP)\r
* ID14: Paired-Duplication Inversion (DUP+INV+DUP)\r
* ID15: Inversion with 5’ Flanking Duplication and 3’ Flanking Deletion (DUP+INV+DEL)\r
* ID16: Inversion with 5’ Flanking Deletion and 3’ Flanking Duplication (DEL+INV+DUP)\r
* ID17: Inverted Duplication with Flanking Triplication (DupTripDup-INV)\r
* ID18: Insertion with Deletion (INSdel)\r
#### Toy Example (CSV mode):\r
```bash\r
cd your_home_path\r
python -m BVSim -ref 'your_home_path/BVSim/empirical/sub_hg19_chr1.fasta' -save your_saving_url -seed 1 -rep 1 -csv -write -snp 2000\r
```\r
#### <a name="parameters-for-csv-mode"></a>Parameters for CSV Mode\r
The lengths of the CSVs follow different Gaussian distributions with modifiable means (-mu) and standard deviations (-sigma).\r
| Parameter | Type | Description | Default |\r
| --- | --- | --- | --- |\r
| `-csv_num` | int | Number for each type of CSV, superior to -csv_total_num | 0 |\r
| `-csv_total_num` | int | Total number for CSV, assign number of each type by empirical weights | 0 |\r
| `-num_ID1_csv to -num_ID18_csv` | int | Number of respective CSV types | 5 |\r
| `-mu_ID1 to -mu_ID18` | int | Mean of Gaussian distribution of CSV length | 1000 |\r
| `-sigma_ID1 to -sigma_ID18` | int | Standard deviation of Gaussian distribution of CSV length | 100 |\r
\r
### <a name="uniform-parallel-mode"></a>Uniform Parallel Mode\r
Add -cores, -len_bins to your command, and write a .job file (task01.job) as follows (-c 5 means 5 cores, should be the same as -cores 5), parallel simulation will be allowed.\r
\r
#### Toy Example (Uniform-parallel mode): task01.job\r
```bash\r
#!/bin/bash\r
#SBATCH -J uniform_parallel\r
#SBATCH -N 1 -c 5\r
#SBATCH --output=output.txt\r
#SBATCH --error=err.txt\r
\r
source /opt/share/etc/miniconda3-py39.sh\r
conda activate BVSim\r
cd your_home_path\r
python -m BVSim -ref your_home_path/hg19/hg19_chr21.fasta -save your_home_path/test_data/BVSim/task03/ -cores 5 -len_bins 500000 -rep 3 -snp 200 -snv_del 200 -snv_ins 200 -write\r
conda deactivate\r
```\r
Submit the job file by:\r
```bash\r
sbatch task01.job\r
```\r
#### <a name="parameters-for-uniform-parallel-mode"></a>Parameters for Uniform parallel Mode\r
\r
| Parameter | Type | Description | Default |\r
| --- | --- | --- | --- |\r
| `-cores` | int | Number of kernels for parallel processing | 1 |\r
| `-len_bins` | int | Length of bins for parallel processing | 50000 |\r
\r
### <a name="wave-mode"></a>Wave Mode\r
\r
In Wave mode, users can provide a `.bed` file generated from an empirical `.vcf` file (for example, from HG002) or multiple BED files derived from samples of a selected population (such as the 15 Cell samples). This functionality allows you to generate non-uniform insertions and deletions with various options.\r
\r
#### <a name="requirements-for-the-bed-file"></a>User-defined Sample(s) and Input BED File Requirements\r
\r
The BED file must adhere to the following requirements:\r
\r
- **First Column**: Location (genomic position)\r
- **Second Column**: DEL/INS label (indicating if the variation is a deletion or insertion)\r
- **Third Column**: Length (absolute value of the variation)\r
\r
Each column should be separated by a tab character (`\\t`) and must not include headers. Additionally, each BED file should represent variations on the same sequence.\r
\r
#### <a name="generating-a-bed-file-for-a-single-sample-in-wave-mode"></a>Generate a BED File for a Single Sample\r
\r
To generate a single input BED file from the HG002 `.vcf` file of chromosome 21, you can use the following commands in your terminal:\r
\r
```bash\r
# Download the VCF file and its index\r
wget https://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ReferenceSamples/giab/release/AshkenazimTrio/HG002_NA24385_son/NIST_SV_v0.6/HG002_SVs_Tier1_v0.6.vcf.gz \r
wget https://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ReferenceSamples/giab/release/AshkenazimTrio/HG002_NA24385_son/NIST_SV_v0.6/HG002_SVs_Tier1_v0.6.vcf.gz.tbi\r
\r
# Activate the bcftools environment\r
conda activate bcftools\r
\r
# Generate the BED file using bcftools and awk\r
bcftools view -H -r 21 -i 'SVTYPE="INS" || SVTYPE="DEL"' /home/adduser/data/test_data/TGS/hg002/HG002_SVs_Tier1_v0.6.vcf.gz | \\\r
awk -v OFS='\\t' '{\r
    split($8, a, ";");\r
    for (i in a) {\r
        if (a[i] ~ /^SVTYPE/) {\r
            split(a[i], b, "=");\r
            svtype = b[2];\r
        }\r
        else if (a[i] ~ /^SVLEN/) {\r
            split(a[i], c, "=");\r
            svlen = c[2];\r
            if (svlen < 0) svlen = -svlen;  # Extract the absolute value of SV length\r
        }\r
    }\r
    print $2, svtype, svlen;  # Print the location, SV type, and absolute SV length\r
}' > /home/adduser/data/test_data/TGS/hg002/chr21_SV_Tier1.bed\r
```\r
##### <a name="job-submission-for-wave-mode-single-sample"></a>Job Submission for Single Sample (BED Format)\r
\r
To utilize this single BED file, users should call '-indel_input_bed' in the command. Below is the example of a SLURM job script that you can use to run the Wave mode simulation with single empirical data:\r
\r
```bash\r
#!/bin/bash\r
#SBATCH -J full_chr21_parallel\r
#SBATCH -N 1 -c 5\r
#SBATCH --output=output_chr21_wave.txt\r
#SBATCH --error=err_chr21_wave.txt\r
\r
source /opt/share/etc/miniconda3-py39.sh\r
conda activate BVSim\r
cd your_home_path\r
python -m BVSim -ref your_home_path/hg19/hg19_chr21.fasta -save your_home_path/test_data/BVSim -seed 0 -rep 2 -cores 5 -len_bins 500000 -wave -indel_input_bed your_home_path/hg002/chr21_SV_Tier1_2.bed -mode empirical -snp 2000 -snv_del 1000 -snv_ins 100 -write\r
conda deactivate\r
```\r
Submit the job file by:\r
```bash\r
sbatch task02_single.job\r
```\r
#### <a name="generating-bed-files-for-multiple-samples-in-wave-mode"></a>Generating BED Files for Multiple Samples\r
\r
In this section, we will outline the steps to generate `.bed` files for multiple cell samples from the original Excel spreadsheet, using the 15 Cell samples as an example.\r
\r
##### Step 1: Download the Original Excel File\r
\r
First, download the Excel file containing the cell samples data:\r
\r
```python\r
import os\r
\r
# Download the Excel file\r
os.system('wget https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S0092867418316337-mmc1.xlsx')\r
\r
```\r
##### Step 2: Load and View the Data\r
Next, load the Excel file into a Pandas DataFrame and view the first few rows:\r
```python\r
import pandas as pd\r
\r
# Read the Excel file into a DataFrame\r
file_path = '1-s2.0-S0092867418316337-mmc1.xlsx'\r
df = pd.read_excel(file_path, sheet_name=0)  # Choose the correct sheet based on the file\r
\r
# Display the first 5 rows of the DataFrame\r
print(df.head(5))\r
```\r
##### Step 3: Filter the Data\r
Extract the required columns and rename the first column:\r
```python\r
# Extract the necessary columns\r
columns_to_keep = ['#CHROM', 'POS', 'END', 'ID', 'SVTYPE', 'SVLEN', 'MERGE_SAMPLES']\r
cell_df = df[columns_to_keep]\r
\r
# List of all sample strings\r
samples = ['CHM1', 'CHM13', 'HG00514', 'HG00733', 'NA19240', 'HG02818', 'NA19434', 'HG01352', 'HG02059', 'NA12878', 'HG04217', 'HG02106', 'HG00268', 'AK1', 'HX1']\r
# selected population: the African population\r
AFR_samples = ['NA19240', 'HG02818', 'NA19434']\r
\r
# Specify the columns to save in the BED file\r
columns_to_save = ['POS', 'SVTYPE', 'SVLEN']\r
\r
# Extract rows where CHROM equals 'chr21'\r
chr21_df = cell_df[cell_df['CHROM'] == 'chr21']\r
\r
# Display the first 10 rows for verification\r
print(chr21_df.head(10))\r
\r
# Generate BED files for each sample in the AFR_samples list\r
for sample in AFR_samples:\r
    # Create a new DataFrame containing only rows where 'MERGE_SAMPLES' contains the current sample\r
    sample_df = chr21_df[chr21_df['MERGE_SAMPLES'].str.contains(sample)]\r
\r
    # Specify the path for the new BED file\r
    bed_file_path = f'.../BVSim/empirical/{sample}_chr21.bed'\r
\r
    # Save the specified columns to a BED file\r
    sample_df[columns_to_save].to_csv(bed_file_path, sep='\\t', header=False, index=False)\r
\r
```\r
#### <a name="job-submission-for-wave-mode-multiple-samples"></a>Job Submission for Multiple Samples (BED Format)\r
\r
We provide an example of a Job submission script using SLURM for running the Wave mode with BVSim. This script utilizes the generated multiple sample BED files. Below is the example of a SLURM job script that you can use to run the Wave mode simulation with multiple samples:\r
\r
```bash\r
#!/bin/bash\r
#SBATCH -J wave\r
#SBATCH -N 1 -c 5\r
#SBATCH --output=/home/project18/code/BVSim_code/wave2_out.txt\r
#SBATCH --error=/home/project18/code/BVSim_code/wave2_err.txt\r
\r
source /opt/share/etc/miniconda3-py39.sh\r
conda activate BVSim\r
cd /home/project18/\r
\r
python -m BVSim -ref your_home_path/hg38/chr21.fasta \\\r
-save your_home_path/BVSim/task01/ -seed 0 -rep 1 -cores 5 \\\r
-len_bins 500000 -wave -mode empirical -snp 2000 -snv_del 1000 -snv_ins 100 \\\r
-write -file_list NA19240_chr21 HG02818_chr21 NA19434_chr21\r
\r
conda deactivate\r
```\r
#### <a name="important-note-on-file-placement"></a>Important Note on File Placement\r
Ensure that both the single sample and multiple sample BED files are placed in the .../BVSim/empirical/ directory. This organization simplifies the command structure, allowing you to specify only the base names of the files (without extensions) directly in the -file_list option, as demonstrated in the script above.\r
\r
#### <a name="parameters-for-wave-mode"></a>Parameters for Wave Mode\r
\r
| Parameter | Type | Description | Default |\r
| --- | --- | --- | --- |\r
| `-cores` | int | Number of kernels for parallel processing | 1 |\r
| `-len_bins` | int | Length of bins for parallel processing | 50000 |\r
| `-wave` | bool | Run Wave.py script | False |\r
| `-mode` | str | Mode for calculating probabilities | 'probability' |\r
| `-sum` | bool | Total indel SV equals sum of the input bed | False |\r
| `-indel_input_bed` | str | Input single BED file | None |\r
| `-file_list` | str | Input list of multiple BED files | None |\r
\r
##### Mode and Sum Parameters\r
\r
The `-mode` parameter determines how the simulation calculates probabilities for insertions and deletions. It accepts two values:\r
\r
- **'probability'**: In this mode, probabilities for insertions and deletions are derived from the empirical data provided in the input BED files. The total number of variations can be defined by the `-sum` parameter. If `-sum` is set to `True`, the total number of insertions or deletions will be the maximum of the calculated empirical total or the specified values in `-sv_ins` or `-sv_del`. This allows for flexibility in controlling the total number of SVs in the simulation.\r
\r
- **'empirical'**: When set to this mode, the simulation directly uses the empirical values from the input data without any probability calculations. The total number of variations will be the sum of the provided empirical data.\r
\r
The `-sum` parameter, when enabled, alters the total number of insertions and deletions based on the specified empirical data. If disabled, the simulation uses the fixed total values defined in `-sv_ins` and `-sv_del`, regardless of the empirical input.\r
\r
\r
### <a name="wave-region-mode"></a>Wave Region Mode\r
\r
In Wave region mode, you can specify different INDEL probabilities using a BED file defined by `region_bed_url`. For example, if you want to increase the insertion and deletion probabilities in the tandem repeat (TR) regions of hg19, you can follow these steps.\r
\r
#### <a name="step-1-extract-tr-regions"></a>Extract User-defined Regions (e.g. TR region) and Generate the BED File\r
\r
First, extract the TR regions' positions from UCSC and create a BED file with two columns (start; end) separated by a tab character (`\\t`).\r
\r
You can generate the BED file using the following commands:\r
\r
```bash\r
# Download the TR regions data\r
wget https://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ReferenceSamples/giab/release/references/GRCh37/resources/hg19.simpleRepeat.bed.gz \r
wget https://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ReferenceSamples/giab/release/references/GRCh37/resources/hg19.simpleRepeat.bed.gz.tbi\r
\r
# Extract the relevant columns and create the BED file\r
zcat hg19.simpleRepeat.bed.gz | awk 'BEGIN{OFS="\\t"} {print $1, $2, $3}' > your_home_path/hg002/windows_TR.bed\r
\r
# Merge overlapping intervals and remove duplicates\r
bedtools sort -i your_home_path/hg002/windows_TR.bed | bedtools merge -i stdin | awk 'BEGIN{OFS="\\t"} {$4="TR"; print}' | uniq > your_home_path/hg002/windows_TR_unique.bed\r
\r
# Filter for chromosome 21\r
awk '$1 == "chr21"' your_home_path/hg002/windows_TR_unique.bed > your_home_path/hg002/windows_TR_unique_chr21.bed\r
\r
# Create a final BED file with start and end positions\r
awk '{print $2 "\\t" $3}' your_home_path/hg002/windows_TR_unique_chr21.bed > your_home_path/hg002/chr21_TR_unique.bed\r
```\r
#### <a name="job-submission-for-wave-region-mode-single-sample"></a>Job Submission for Single Sample (BED Format)\r
In this example, we set the seed to `0` and use a replication ID of `4`. The job is configured to utilize `5` cores for parallel processing, with a bin size of `500,000`. We will generate `10,000` SNPs, along with `100` micro deletions and `100` micro insertions. The probabilities for these insertions and deletions are specified in the input BED file (`-indel_input_bed`) using the empirical mode (`-mode`). Additionally, we have set the probabilities for insertions (`-p_ins_region`) and deletions (`-p_del_region`) to approximately `0.6` for the total located in the TR region defined by `-region_bed_url`.\r
\r
```bash\r
#!/bin/bash\r
#SBATCH -J full_chr21_parallel\r
#SBATCH -N 1 -c 5\r
#SBATCH --output=output_chr21_wave_region.txt\r
#SBATCH --error=err_chr21_wave_region.txt\r
\r
source /opt/share/etc/miniconda3-py39.sh\r
conda activate BVSim\r
cd your_home_path\r
python -m BVSim -ref your_home_path/hg19/hg19_chr21.fasta -save your_home_path/test_data/BVSim -seed 0 -rep 4 -cores 5 -len_bins 500000 -wave_region -indel_input_bed your_home_path/hg002/chr21_SV_Tier1.bed -mode empirical -snp 10000 -snv_del 100 -snv_ins 100 -write -p_del_region 0.6 -p_ins_region 0.6 -region_bed_url your_home_path/hg002/chr21_TR_unique.bed\r
conda deactivate\r
```\r
\r
Submit the job file using the following command:\r
```bash\r
sbatch task03.job\r
```\r
#### <a name="parameters-for-wave-region-mode"></a>Parameters for Wave Region Mode\r
The table below summarizes the parameters available for Wave region mode:\r
| Parameter | Type | Description | Default |\r
| --- | --- | --- | --- |\r
| `-cores` | int | Number of kernels for parallel processing | 1 |\r
| `-len_bins` | int | Length of bins for parallel processing | 50000 |\r
| `-wave` | bool | Run Wave.py script | False |\r
| `-mode` | str | Mode for calculating probabilities | 'probability' |\r
| `-sum` | bool | Total number of insertions and deletions equals sum of the input bed | False |\r
| `-indel_input_bed` | str | Input single BED file | None |\r
| `-file_list` | str | Input list of multiple BED files | None |\r
| `-wave_region` | bool | Run Wave_TR.py script | False |\r
| `-p_del_region` | float | Probability of SV DEL in the user-defined region for deletion | 0.5 |\r
| `-p_ins_region` | float | Probability of SV INS in the user-defined region for insertion | 0.5 |\r
| `-region_bed_url` | str | Path of the BED file for the user-defined region | 'your_home_path/hg002/chr21_TR_unique.bed' |\r
\r
### <a name="human-genome"></a>Human Genome\r
For the human genome, we derive the length distributions of SVs from HG002 and the 15 representative samples. For SNPs, we embed a learned substitution transition matrix from the dbSNP database. With a user-specified bin size, BVSim learns the distribution of SV positions per interval. It can model the SVs per interval as a multinomial distribution parameterized by the observed frequencies in HG002 (GRCh37/hg19 as reference) or sample the SV numbers per interval from a Gaussian distribution with the mean and standard deviation computed across the 15 samples (GRCh38/hg38 as reference). Calling ‘-hg19’ or ‘-hg38’ and specifying the chromosome name can activate the above procedures automatically for the human genome.\r
\r
In the following example, we use 5 cores and 500,000 as length of the intervals. The reference is chromosome 21 of hg19, so we call "-hg19 chr21" in the command line to utilize the default procedure. In addition, we generated 1,000 SNPs, 99 duplications, 7 inversions, 280 deletions, and 202 insertions. The ratio of deletions/insertions in the tandem repeat regions with respect to the total number is 0.810/0.828. We also set the minimum and maximum lengths of some SVs.\r
#### Toy example (-hg19)\r
```bash\r
#!/bin/bash\r
#SBATCH -J 0_hg19_chr21\r
#SBATCH -N 1 -c 5\r
#SBATCH --output=output.txt\r
#SBATCH --error=err.txt\r
\r
source /opt/share/etc/miniconda3-py39.sh\r
conda activate BVSim\r
cd your_home_path\r
python -m BVSim -ref your_home_path/hg19/hg19_chr21.fasta -save your_home_path/test_data/BVSim/ -seed 0 -rep 0 -cores 5 -len_bins 500000 -hg19 chr21 -mode probability -snp 1000 -sv_trans 0 -dup 99 -sv_inver 7 -sv_del 280 -sv_ins 202 -snv_del 0 -snv_ins 0 -p_del_region 0.810 -p_ins_region 0.828 -region_bed_url /home/project18/data/test_data/TGS/hg002/chr21_TR_unique.bed -delmin 50 -delmax 2964912 -insmin 50 -insmax 187524\r
conda deactivate\r
```\r
\r
\r
## <a name="uninstallation"></a>Uninstallation for Updates\r
To update to the latest version of BVSim, you can uninstall and delete the cloned files. Then, try to clone from the new repository and install again.\r
```bash\r
cd your_home_path\r
pip uninstall BVSim\r
```\r
