    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1786?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-07-01" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """# CWL + RO-Crate Workflow Descriptions\r
\r
This repository stores computational workflows described using the **Common Workflow Language (CWL)** and enriched with metadata using **Research Object Crate (RO-Crate)** conforming to the **Workflow Run RO-Crate** profile.\r
\r
Each workflow is contained in its own directory (e.g., `WF5201`, `WF6101`, ...). Inside each workflow directory you will typically find at least:\r
\r
- The **CWL workflow definition** (with the same name as the directory, e.g., `WF5201.cwl`).\r
- The **RO-Crate metadata file** (`ro-crate-metadata.json`).\r
\r
Additional files supporting the workflow may also be included.\r
\r
## Overview\r
\r
This document explains how to represent workflows by combining:\r
\r
- **CWL (Common Workflow Language):** Used to define the computational steps, data flows, and tools.\r
- **RO-Crate:** Used to capture associated metadata (e.g., authorship, licenses, software, datasets) for the workflow.\r
\r
By separating the abstract workflow definition from its metadata description, you can leverage existing tools for visualization, editing, and validation of your workflows while maintaining a clear structure.\r
\r
## Our Approach\r
\r
We represent workflows using a combination of CWL and RO-Crate:\r
\r
- **CWL:** Captures the abstract definition of the workflow, detailing its computational steps, data flows, and the tools utilized. It does not include the implementation details of each operation.\r
- **RO-Crate:** Provides rich metadata for the overall repository, the workflow file(s), software, and datasets. This metadata allows you to understand the context, provenance, and related details of the workflow components.\r
\r
This separation provides flexibility by keeping the execution details (CWL) distinct from descriptive metadata (RO-Crate), yet they remain tightly connected.\r
\r
## Describing a Workflow using CWL + RO-Crate\r
\r
To fully describe a workflow, you must separate the **workflow definition** (using CWL) from the **metadata description** (using RO-Crate).\r
\r
### Defining the CWL Workflow\r
\r
1. **Identify Global Inputs and Outputs:**  \r
   Decide on the data that enters the workflow (inputs) and the final results (outputs). Optionally, include intermediate outputs if they are of interest.\r
\r
2. **Create the CWL File:**  \r
   Write a CWL file in YAML format. Start with file metadata such as:\r
\r
   ```yaml\r
   cwlVersion: v1.2\r
   class: Workflow\r
\r
   requirements:\r
     MultipleInputFeatureRequirement: {}\r
     SubworkflowFeatureRequirement: {}\r
   ```\r
\r
   > [NOTE]\r
   > The `requirements` section may vary depending on your workflow. For example, if you use sub-workflows, you must include the `SubworkflowFeatureRequirement`.\r
\r
3. **Declare Global Inputs and Outputs:**\r
\r
   ```yaml\r
   inputs:\r
     DT5210: Directory\r
     DT5211: Directory\r
\r
   outputs:\r
     DT5208:\r
       type: Directory\r
       outputSource: SS5213/DT5208\r
   ```\r
\r
   > [NOTE]\r
   > Although `Directory` is commonly used to represent a dataset, you might choose a different type. Refer to the CWL documentation for additional types.\r
\r
### Defining Workflow Steps\r
\r
Each workflow step (or subworkflow) follows a consistent structure:\r
\r
```yaml\r
SS5205:\r
  in:\r
    DT5210: DT5210\r
  run:\r
    class: Operation\r
    inputs:\r
      DT5210: Directory\r
    outputs:\r
      DT5201: File\r
      DT5203: Directory\r
  out:\r
    - DT5201\r
    - DT5203\r
```\r
\r
Key elements are:\r
\r
- **`in`:** Defines which data this step requires.\r
- **`run`:**\r
  - For operations: Uses the `Operation` class to abstract away the underlying execution details.\r
  - For subworkflows: Points to another CWL file.\r
- **`out`:** Lists the output data produced by the step.\r
\r
### Connecting Steps via Data Dependencies\r
\r
CWL does not require an explicit execution order. Instead, dependencies are determined by connecting outputs to inputs:\r
\r
```yaml\r
ST520102:\r
  in:\r
    DT5201: ST520101/DT5201\r
  run: ST520102.cwl\r
  out:\r
    - DT5255\r
```\r
\r
This connection means `ST520102` depends on the output (`DT5201`) of `ST520101` and will execute after it, while still allowing independent steps to run in parallel.\r
\r
## Validating Your Workflow and Metadata\r
\r
- **CWL Validation:**  \r
  Use [cwltool](https://github.com/common-workflow-language/cwltool) to check your CWL files for syntax errors and to generate a graphical visualization (using Graphviz `dot` format) for verifying the workflow structure.\r
\r
- **RO-Crate Validation:**  \r
  Validate your `ro-crate-metadata.json` file with tools such as the [RO-Crate Validator](https://github.com/crs4/rocrate-validator) and explore your RO-Crate interactively with [ro-crate-html-js](https://github.com/Language-Research-Technology/ro-crate-html-js).\r
\r
---\r
\r
## Additional Resources\r
\r
- [CWL Official Guide and Getting Started](https://www.commonwl.org/getting-started/)\r
- [CWL User Guide](https://www.commonwl.org/user_guide/)\r
- [RO-Crate Official Documentation](https://www.researchobject.org/ro-crate/)\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1786" ;
    ns1:keywords "lava" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-V3] WF5301: Lava flow" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1786?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1787?version=8> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:applicationSubCategory <http://edamontology.org/topic_3174>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3301>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3697>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3837> ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.1787.8" ;
    ns1:creator "Alem Gusinac",
        "Annemarie Boleij",
        "Jos Boekhorst",
        "Thomas Ederveen" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-01-21" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-01-21" ;
    ns1:description """[![CI-stub-run](https://github.com/CMG-GUTS/metabiomx/actions/workflows/nf-build-stub.yaml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/CMG-GUTS/metabiomx/actions/workflows/nf-build-stub.yaml)\r
[![Nextflow](https://img.shields.io/badge/nextflow%20DSL2-%E2%89%A524.10.0-23aa62.svg?labelColor=000000)](https://www.nextflow.io/)\r
[![run with docker](https://img.shields.io/badge/run%20with-docker-0db7ed?labelColor=000000&logo=docker)](https://www.docker.com/)\r
[![run with singularity](https://img.shields.io/badge/run%20with-singularity-1d355c.svg?labelColor=000000)](https://sylabs.io/docs/)\r
[![nf-test](https://img.shields.io/badge/tested_with-nf--test-337ab7.svg)](https://code.askimed.com/nf-test)\r
\r
## Introduction: **metaBIOMx**\r
\r
The metagenomics microbiomics pipeline is a best-practice suite for the decontamination and annotation of sequencing data obtained via short-read shotgun sequencing. The pipeline contains [NF-core modules](https://github.com/nf-core/modules) and other local modules that are in the similar format. It can be runned via both docker and singularity containers.\r
\r
## Pipeline summary\r
The pipeline is able to perform different taxonomic annotation on either (single/paired) reads or contigs. The different subworkflows can be defined via `--bypass_<method>` flags, a full overview is shown by running `--help`. By default the pipeline will check if the right databases are present in the right formats, when the path is provided. If this is not the case, compatible databases will be automatically downloaded.\r
\r
For both subworkflows the pipeline will perform read trimming via [Trimmomatic](https://github.com/timflutre/trimmomatic) and/or [AdapterRemoval](https://github.com/MikkelSchubert/adapterremoval), followed by human removal via [Kneaddata](https://huttenhower.sph.harvard.edu/kneaddata/). Before and after each step the quality control will be assessed via [fastqc](https://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/projects/fastqc/) and a [multiqc](https://github.com/MultiQC/MultiQC) report is created as output. Then taxonomy annotation is done as follows:\r
\r
**Read annotation**\r
- paired reads are interleaved using [BBTools](https://archive.jgi.doe.gov/data-and-tools/software-tools/bbtools/).\r
- [MetaPhlAn3](https://huttenhower.sph.harvard.edu/metaphlan/) and [HUMAnN3](https://huttenhower.sph.harvard.edu/humann/) are used for taxonomy and functional profiling.\r
- taxonomy profiles are merged into a single BIOM file using [biom-format](https://github.com/biocore/biom-format).\r
\r
**Contig annotation**\r
- read assembly is performed via [SPAdes](http://cab.spbu.ru/software/spades/).\r
- Quality assesment of contigs is done via [Busco](https://busco.ezlab.org/).\r
- taxonomy profiles are created using [CAT](https://github.com/dutilh/CAT).\r
- Read abundance estimation is performed on the contigs using [Bowtie2]() and [BCFtools](http://samtools.github.io/bcftools/bcftools.html).\r
- Contigs are selected if a read can be aligned against a contig and a BIOM file is generated using [biom-format](https://github.com/biocore/biom-format).\r
\r
## Installation\r
> [!NOTE]\r
> Make sure you have installed the latest [nextflow](https://www.nextflow.io/docs/latest/install.html#install-nextflow) version! \r
\r
Clone the repository in a directory of your choice:\r
```bash\r
git clone https://github.com/CMG-GUTS/metabiomx.git\r
```\r
\r
The pipeline is containerised, meaning it can be runned via docker or singularity images. No further actions need to be performed when using the docker profile, except a docker registery needs to be set on your local system, see [docker](https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/). In case singularity is used, images are automatically cached within the project directory.\r
\r
## Usage\r
Since the latest version, metaBIOMx works with both a samplesheet (CSV) format or a path to the input files. Preferably, samplesheets should be provided.\r
```bash\r
nextflow run main.nf --input <samplesheet.csv> -work-dir work -profile singularity\r
nextflow run main.nf --input <'*_{1,R1,2,R2}.{fq,fq.gz,fastq,fastq.gz}'> -work-dir work -profile singularity\r
```\r
You can also try to run the test data set in `tests/data` folder, these are also available via the `-profile test` but then the databases will be by default downloaded in the `tests/db` folder and you might want to specify a folder of your own choice.\r
\r
> [!NOTE]\r
> Tests data should be runned with the flags `--bypass_trim` and `--bypass_decon`, which is default on `true` in the `conf/test.config`\r
\r
### ?? Sample Metadata File Specification\r
\r
metaBIOMx expects your sample input data to follow a **simple, but strict** structure to ensure compatibility and allow upfront validation. The input should be provided as a **CSV** file where **each entry = one sample** with specified sequencing file paths. Additional properties not mentioned here will be ignored by the validation step.\r
\r
### **Properties and Validation Rules**\r
\r
#### ?? Required properties\r
\r
| Property     | Type   | Rules / Description                                                                                   |\r
|--------------|--------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\r
| `sample_id`     | string | Unique sample ID with no spaces (`^\\S+$`). Serves as an identifier.                                  |\r
| `forward_read` | string | File path to forward sequencing read. Must be non-empty string matching FASTQ gzipped pattern. File must exist. |\r
\r
#### ?? Optional property\r
\r
| Property       | Type   | Rules / Description                                                                                   |\r
|----------------|--------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\r
| `reverse_read` | string | File path to reverse sequencing read. Same constraints as `forward_read`. Required if specified.   |\r
\r
#### ?? Pattern‑based columns \r
You can define extra variables using special prefixes:\r
- **`CONTRAST_...`** → grouping/category labels used in differential comparisons  \r
  Example: `CONTRAST_Treatment` with values `Drug` / `Placebo`\r
These prefixes are used to generate an automated `OmicFlow` report with alpha, beta diversity and compositional plots. For more information see [OmicFlow](https://github.com/agusinac/OmicFlow).\r
\r
### Example cases\r
#### ?? Read annotation\r
```bash\r
nextflow run main.nf \\\r
    --input <samplesheet.csv> \\\r
    # (optional) --bypass_trim \\\r
    # (optional) --bypass_decon \\\r
    --bypass_contig_annotation \\\r
    -work-dir work \\\r
    -profile singularity\r
```\r
\r
#### ?? Contig annotation\r
```bash\r
nextflow run main.nf \\\r
    --input <samplesheet.csv> \\\r
    # (optional) --bypass_trim \\\r
    # (optional) --bypass_decon \\\r
    --bypass_read_annotation \\\r
    -work-dir work \\\r
    -profile singularity\r
```\r
\r
In case you only have assemblies and wish to perform contig annotation:\r
```bash\r
nextflow run main.nf \\\r
    --input <samplesheet.csv> \\\r
    --bypass_assembly \\\r
    --bypass_read_annotation \\\r
    -work-dir work \\\r
    -profile singularity\r
```\r
\r
## Automatic database setup\r
The pipeline requires a set of databases which are used by the different tools within this workflow. The user is required to specify the location in where the databases will be downloaded. It is also possible to download the databases manually. The `configure` subworkflow will evaluate the database format and presence of the compatible files automatically.\r
```bash\r
nextflow run main.nf \\\r
    --bowtie_db path/to/db/bowtie2 \\\r
    --metaphlan_db path/to/db/metaphlan \\\r
    --humann_db path/to/db/humann \\\r
    --catpack_db path/to/db/catpack \\\r
    --busco_db path/to/db/busco_downloads \\\r
    -work-dir <work/dir> \\\r
    -profile <singularity,docker>\r
```\r
\r
## Support\r
\r
If you are having issues, please [create an issue](https://github.com/CMG-GUTS/metabiomx/issues)\r
""" ;
