<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1808?version=2> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Jens Krumsieck" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-08-24" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-08-24" ;
    ns1:description """# SciWIn Client Demo\r
A basic Workflow using [SciWIn Client (`s4n`)](https://github.com/fairagro/m4.4_sciwin_client) can be created with the commands hereafter. This guide assumes the usage of unix based operating systems, however Windows should work, too. If not please [open an issue](https://github.com/fairagro/m4.4_sciwin_client/issues/new).\r
\r
## Installation\r
[![GitHub Release](https://img.shields.io/github/v/release/fairagro/m4.4_sciwin_client)](https://github.com/fairagro/m4.4_sciwin_client/releases/latest)\r
\r
The latest Version of `s4n` can be installed using the following command:\r
```\r
curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -LsSf https://fairagro.github.io/m4.4_sciwin_client/get_s4n.sh | sh \r
```\r
\r
Specific Versions can be installed with the following command, by replacing the version tag with a version of choice.\r
```\r
curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -LsSf https://github.com/fairagro/m4.4_sciwin_client/releases/download/v0.6.0/s4n-installer.sh | sh\r
```\r
\r
The Installation can be verified using `s4n -V`.\r
SciWIn Client comes with a lot of commands. In this demo the `init`, `tool`, `workflow` and `execute` commands will be showcased.\r
```\r
 _____        _  _    _  _____         _____  _  _               _   \r
/  ___|      (_)| |  | ||_   _|       /  __ \\| |(_)             | |   \r
\\ `--.   ___  _ | |  | |  | |  _ __   | /  \\/| | _   ___  _ __  | |_  \r
 `--. \\ / __|| || |/\\| |  | | | '_ \\  | |    | || | / _ \\| '_ \\ | __|\r
/\\__/ /| (__ | |\\  /\\  / _| |_| | | | | \\__/\\| || ||  __/| | | || |_  \r
\\____/  \\___||_| \\/  \\/  \\___/|_| |_|  \\____/|_||_| \\___||_| |_| \\__|\r
\r
Client tool for Scientific Workflow Infrastructure (SciWIn)\r
Documentation: https://fairagro.github.io/m4.4_sciwin_client/\r
\r
Version: 0.8.0\r
\r
Usage: s4n <COMMAND>\r
\r
Commands:\r
  init         Initializes project folder structure and repository\r
  create       Creates a new CWL File or Workflow\r
  list         Lists either all CWL Files or details to a given file [aliases: ls]\r
  remove       Removes a CWL File from the workflows Directory [aliases: rm]\r
  connect      Connects a workflow node\r
  disconnect   Disconnects a workflow node\r
  visualize    Visualizes a workflow\r
  save         Saves a workflow\r
  install      Installs a workflow as submodule [aliases: i]\r
  uninstall    Removes an installed workflow\r
  execute      Execution of CWL Files locally or on remote servers [aliases: ex]\r
  annotate     Annotate CWL files\r
  completions  Generate shell completions\r
  help         Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)\r
\r
Options:\r
  -h, --help     Print help\r
  -V, --version  Print version\r
```\r
\r
## Demo Repository\r
The [Demo Repository](https://github.com/fairagro/sciwin_client_demo) mainly contains two folders `data` and `code`. The result workflow will  download election data, print election results as `barplot`, convert input data into a `geojson` file, and maps it onto the `geojson` data resulting in a `choropleth` graph. See the images for the final outputs.\r
\r
![result](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fairagro/m4.4_sciwin_client_demo/refs/heads/complete_run/election.png)\r
![result](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fairagro/m4.4_sciwin_client_demo/refs/heads/complete_run/plot.png)\r
\r
## Creating the CommandLineTools\r
First of all, we start, by creating a new `s4n` project.\r
```\r
s4n init\r
```\r
\r
CWL mainly describes processes in CommandLineTools which later can be connected into Workflows. CommandLineTools are essentially wrappers for commands that would usually be executed in the command line. CWL uses a special YAML structure to describe those processes.\r
\r
```cwl\r
#!/usr/bin/env cwl-runner\r
\r
cwlVersion: v1.2\r
class: CommandLineTool\r
\r
requirements:\r
- class: DockerRequirement\r
  dockerPull: osgeo/gdal:ubuntu-full-3.6.3\r
- class: InlineJavascriptRequirement\r
\r
inputs:\r
- id: districts_geojson\r
  type: string\r
  default: districts.geojson\r
  inputBinding:\r
    position: 0\r
- id: data_braunschweig\r
  type: Directory\r
  default:\r
    class: Directory\r
    location: ../../data/braunschweig\r
  inputBinding:\r
    position: 1\r
- id: lco\r
  type: string\r
  default: RFC7946=YES\r
  inputBinding:\r
    prefix: -lco\r
\r
outputs:\r
- id: districts\r
  type: File\r
  outputBinding:\r
    glob: $(inputs.districts_geojson)\r
\r
baseCommand: ogr2ogr\r
```\r
\r
However it may is tedious to write those files by hand. That is where `s4n` comes to the rescue. A Command that would normally happen on the command line just needs to be prefixed with `s4n tool create`. Examples can be found at the [documentation](https://fairagro.github.io/m4.4_sciwin_client/examples/tool-creation/).\r
\r
To create Tools based of the Python scripts in the `code` Directory a virtual environment needs to be created using \r
```bash\r
python3 -m venv .venv\r
source .venv/bin/activate\r
pip install plotly pandas kaleido==0.2.1 matplotlib\r
```\r
\r
The next step is to download election data using a series of API calls for which luckily already a script exists. The script downloads the data from `votemanager.kdo.de` and writes the `csv` to stdout.\r
A tool can be created easily be prefixing the python call. However we also need to escape the `>` using a backslash for it to properly work\r
```bash\r
s4n create python code/download_election_data.py --ags 03101000 --election "Bundestagswahl 2025" \\> data.csv\r
```\r
\r
The written csv file lacks the header information of which party results correspond to which column. Therefore we use the `get_feature_info` script and create a tool as follows:\r
```bash\r
s4n create python code/get_feature_info.py --data data.csv\r
```\r
\r
With this information the election plot can be outputted. The script `plot_election` does the job and accepts the json file from `get_feature_info` and the aforementioned csv.\r
```bash\r
s4n create -c Dockerfile --container-tag pyplot --enable-network python code/plot_election.py --data data.csv --features features.json\r
```\r
\r
## Combining the Tools into a workflow\r
The three CommandLineTools now will be combined into an automated pipeline. A barebones workflow can be generated by using the create command. This command is optional, as the `connect` command also triggers creation of a workflow.\r
```bash\r
s4n create --name demo\r
```\r
The workflow that is being built looks like the graph represented in the following image\r
\r
![the resulting workflow](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fairagro/m4.4_sciwin_client_demo/refs/heads/complete_run/workflow_interim.svg)\r
\r
First of all a connection between the donwload script and `get_feature_info` as well as `plot_election` is created by\r
```bash\r
s4n connect demo --from download_election_data/data --to get_feature_info/data\r
s4n connect demo --from download_election_data/data --to plot_election/data\r
```\r
To get the correct values for `--from` and `--to` the command `s4n tool ls -a` can be used.\r
\r
The plot tool also needs the feature information, so the next step is to combine both tools:\r
\r
```bash\r
s4n connect demo --from get_feature_info/features --to plot_election/features\r
```\r
\r
To use the workflow it needs inputs and outputs. In this demo's tools there are a lot of inputs, but some have default values. That means only neccesary connections have to be made. For the creation of inputs the `--from` value neeeds to start with `@inputs`.\r
```bash\r
s4n connect demo --from @inputs/election --to download_election_data/election\r
s4n connect demo --from @inputs/ags --to download_election_data/ags\r
```\r
\r
Adding outputs follows the same logic, however `@outputs` is used in `--to`\r
```bash\r
s4n connect demo --from plot_election/election --to @outputs/bar\r
```\r
\r
Saving the workflow is neccessary to have a clean git history for further creating CommandLineTools.\r
```bash\r
s4n save demo\r
```\r
\r
During the creation `s4n workflow status demo` can always be used to view the connection status.\r
\r
## Adding additional steps\r
The next tool uses [GDAL](https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geospatial_Data_Abstraction_Library) to convert the shape file in `data/braunschweig` to a `geojson` file. The Command one would typically use would be\r
```bash\r
ogr2ogr districts.geojson data/braunschweig -lco RFC7946=YES\r
# s4n command\r
s4n create ogr2ogr districts.geojson data/braunschweig -lco RFC7946=YES\r
```\r
\r
However we might not have gdal installed on our machine, so we request `s4n` to not run the command. Therefore `s4n` needs to be told what file will be written with `-o` and for later usage a docker image is specified using `-c`.\r
```bash\r
s4n create --name shp2geojson --no-run -o districts.geojson -c osgeo/gdal:ubuntu-full-3.6.3 ogr2ogr districts.geojson data/braunschweig -lco RFC7946=YES\r
```\r
This correct creation of the tool can be tested using \r
```bash\r
s4n execute local workflows/shp2geojson/shp2geojson.cwl \r
```\r
\r
The outputted file now needs to be committed to move on\r
```bash\r
git add . && git commit -m "Execution of shp2geojson"\r
```\r
\r
In the last step the plot tool needs to be created. In this tool `plotly` is used to create a `choropleth` graph based on the outputs of the preceeding steps. The packages installed to the virtual environment are needed here. A Dockerfile to use is already in the repo.\r
```bash\r
s4n create -c Dockerfile --container-tag pyplot --enable-network python code/plot_map.py --geojson districts.geojson --csv data.csv --feature F3 --on gebiet-nr:BEZNUM --output_name plot\r
```\r
\r
## Adding the new tools to Workflow\r
The two new tools will now be added to the workflow.\r
\r
![the final resulting workflow](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fairagro/m4.4_sciwin_client_demo/refs/heads/complete_run/workflow_final.svg)\r
\r
Knowing that the plot tool needs the geojson, a connection from the geojson output to the corresponding input can be created.\r
```bash\r
s4n connect demo --from shp2geojson/districts --to plot_map/geojson\r
```\r
\r
As the plot step also needs the election data, another connection can be created.\r
```bash\r
s4n connect demo --from download_election_data/data --to plot_map/csv\r
```\r
\r
Now we need to wire up the inputs. The input connections for `ags`, `election`, `feature`and `shapes` will be created as follows:\r
```bash\r
s4n connect demo --from @inputs/feature --to plot_map/feature\r
s4n connect demo --from @inputs/shapes --to shp2geojson/data_braunschweig\r
```\r
\r
The last step is to add the output to the workflow. Only the `png` file is desired.\r
```bash\r
s4n connect demo --from plot_map/plot --to @outputs/map\r
```\r
\r
The final workflow needs to be saved.\r
```bash\r
s4n save demo\r
```\r
\r
## Workflow Execution\r
We want to clean our workspace by deleting the outputs we created by creating the CommandLineTools. For the execution a parameter file will be created using the `s4n execute make-template` command.\r
```bash\r
s4n execute make-template workflows/demo/demo.cwl > inputs.yml\r
```\r
\r
This needs to be updated using the correct input values:\r
```yaml\r
ags: "03101000"\r
election: Bundestagswahl 2025\r
shapes:\r
  class: Directory\r
  location: data/braunschweig\r
feature: F3\r
```\r
\r
Execution of the Workflow is done by\r
```bash\r
s4n execute local workflows/demo/demo.cwl inputs.yml\r
```""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1808" ;
    ns1:license "MIT" ;
    ns1:name "SciWIn Client Demo" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1808?version=2> ;
    ns1:version "2" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1809?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.1809.1" ;
    ns1:creator "Thomas Barba" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-07-15" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-07-15" ;
    ns1:description """# DUNE: Deep feature extraction by UNet-based Neuroimaging-oriented autoEncoder\r
\r
A versatile neuroimaging encoder that captures brain complexity across multiple diseases: cancer, dementia and schizophrenia.\r
\r
## Overview\r
\r
DUNE (Deep feature extraction by UNet-based Neuroimaging-oriented autoEncoder) is a neuroimaging-oriented deep learning model designed to extract deep features from multisequence brain MRIs, enabling their processing by basic machine learning algorithms. This project provides an end-to-end solution from DICOM conversion to low-dimensional feature extraction that captures clinically relevant patterns across several neurological conditions including cancer, dementia, and schizophrenia.\r
\r
## Pipeline Architecture\r
\r
The pipeline consists of the following sequential stages:\r
\r
1. **DICOM to NIfTI Conversion**: Transforms medical DICOM images into the NIfTI format for analysis (skipped if NIfTI files are provided directly)\r
2. **Preprocessing**: Prepares images through brain extraction (optional if already done), bias field correction, and spatial standardization\r
3. **Feature Extraction**: Uses a UNet-based autoencoder (without skip connections) to extract low-dimensional embeddings\r
\r
Each stage can be run independently based on your specific needs.\r
\r
## Installation\r
\r
### Prerequisites\r
\r
- Python 3.8+\r
- PyTorch 1.7+\r
- ANTs (Advanced Normalization Tools)\r
- DICOM2NIFTI\r
- TorchIO\r
- Nibabel\r
- Click\r
\r
### ANTs Installation\r
\r
DUNE requires ANTs (Advanced Normalization Tools) for brain image preprocessing. ANTs is not installable via pip and requires separate installation:\r
\r
- Installation instructions are available on the [ANTs GitHub repository](https://github.com/ANTsX/ANTs)\r
- Ensure the ANTs binaries are in your PATH before running DUNE\r
\r
### Setup\r
\r
```bash\r
# Clone the repository\r
git clone https://github.com/gevaertlab/DUNE.git\r
cd DUNE\r
\r
# Install dependencies\r
pip install -r requirements.txt\r
\r
# Ensure ANTs binaries are in your PATH\r
# For example:\r
export ANTSPATH=/path/to/ANTs/bin/\r
export PATH=${ANTSPATH}:$PATH\r
```\r
\r
## Usage\r
\r
### Initialize Workspace\r
\r
```bash\r
python -m src.main init /path/to/workspace\r
```\r
\r
### Process a Case\r
\r
```bash\r
# Process a DICOM folder\r
python -m src.main process /path/to/dicom/folder /path/to/output\r
\r
# Process a NIfTI file with brain extraction\r
python -m src.main process /path/to/image.nii.gz /path/to/output\r
\r
# Process a NIfTI file skipping brain extraction (if already performed)\r
python -m src.main process /path/to/image.nii.gz /path/to/output --skip-brain-extraction\r
\r
# Keep preprocessed files in the output\r
python -m src.main process /path/to/image.nii.gz /path/to/output --keep-preprocessed\r
\r
# Process without creating log files\r
python -m src.main process /path/to/image.nii.gz /path/to/output --no-logs\r
\r
# Process a folder containing multiple NIfTI files\r
python -m src.main process /path/to/nifti/folder /path/to/output\r
\r
# Only perform feature extraction (skip DICOM conversion and preprocessing)\r
python -m src.main process /path/to/preprocessed.nii.gz /path/to/output --features-only\r
```\r
\r
### Output Structure\r
\r
The pipeline produces a simplified output structure:\r
\r
1. **For a single file input (e.g., my_image.nii.gz)**:\r
   ```\r
   output_dir/\r
   ├── my_image_features.csv              # Extracted features\r
   ├── my_image_preprocessed.nii.gz       # (Optional) Preprocessed image if --keep-preprocessed is used\r
   └── logs/                              # Processing logs (unless --no-logs is used)\r
   ```\r
\r
2. **For a directory input (e.g., case_dir/)**:\r
   ```\r
   output_dir/\r
   ├── case_dir/\r
   │   ├── features/\r
   │   │   ├── sequence1_features.csv     # Features for sequence 1\r
   │   │   └── sequence2_features.csv     # Features for sequence 2\r
   │   └── preprocessed/                  # (Optional) Only if --keep-preprocessed is used\r
   │       ├── sequence1_preprocessed.nii.gz\r
   │       └── sequence2_preprocessed.nii.gz\r
   └── logs/                              # Processing logs (unless --no-logs is used)\r
   ```\r
\r
### Feature Files Structure\r
\r
Each generated feature file is a CSV containing 3,072 features extracted from the bottleneck layer of the DUNE autoencoder. The files are structured as follows:\r
\r
- The first column contains the sequence identifier\r
- The remaining columns (3,072) contain the extracted features\r
\r
Example of a feature file structure:\r
\r
| sequence_id        | feature_0 | feature_1 | feature_2 | ... | feature_3071 |\r
| ------------------ | --------- | --------- | --------- | --- | ------------ |\r
| patient001-T1_POST | 0.0821    | -0.1427   | 0.2984    | ... | 0.0193       |\r
\r
These low-dimensional embeddings capture essential brain structure information that can be used for downstream machine learning tasks like classification, regression, or clustering.\r
\r
### Configuration\r
\r
The pipeline can be customized through a YAML configuration file:\r
\r
```yaml\r
# Paths to resources\r
paths:\r
  scripts:\r
    preprocessing: "scripts/preprocessing"\r
  templates: "data/templates"\r
  models: "data/models"\r
\r
# Preprocessing options\r
preprocessing:\r
  parameters:\r
    brain_extraction:\r
      threshold: 0.5\r
    bias_correction:\r
      iterations: 4\r
  skip_brain_extraction: false\r
  keep_preprocessed: false\r
\r
# Model options\r
model:\r
  weights_file: "U-AE.pt"\r
  input_size: [256, 256, 256]\r
\r
# Output options\r
output:\r
  enable_logs: true\r
\r
# Pipeline control options\r
pipeline:\r
  features_only: false  # If true, only performs feature extraction\r
```\r
\r
## Alternative Model Architectures\r
\r
This repository also contains alternative autoencoder architectures that were evaluated in our paper:\r
\r
1. **U-AE** (default): UNet without skip connections - produces the most informative embeddings\r
2. **UNET**: UNet with skip connections - best for image reconstruction\r
3. **U-VAE**: Variational UNet without skip connections\r
4. **VAE**: Fully connected variational autoencoder\r
\r
To use an alternative architecture, specify it in your configuration file:\r
\r
```yaml\r
model:\r
  architecture: "UNET"  # Options: "U-AE", "UNET", "U-VAE", "VAE"\r
  weights_path: "data/models/UNET.pt"\r
```\r
Or when running the feature extraction directly:\r
\r
```bash\r
python src/pipeline/feature_extraction.py input.nii.gz output.csv --architecture UNET\r
```\r
\r
## Command Line Options\r
\r
| Option                          | Description                                       |\r
| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\r
| `--config`, `-c`                | Path to configuration file                        |\r
| `--verbose`, `-v`               | Enable verbose output                             |\r
| `--skip-brain-extraction`, `-s` | Skip brain extraction step                        |\r
| `--keep-preprocessed`, `-p`     | Keep preprocessed NIfTI files in output           |\r
| `--no-logs`                     | Disable writing log files                         |\r
| `--features-only`, `-f`         | Only perform feature extraction                   |\r
\r
## Pipeline Flexibility\r
\r
DUNE offers flexibility to handle different workflow scenarios:\r
\r
1. **Complete Pipeline**: Convert DICOM to NIfTI, preprocess images, and extract features\r
2. **Partial Processing**: Start with NIfTI files and apply preprocessing and feature extraction\r
3. **Minimal Preprocessing**: Skip brain extraction if your images are already skull-stripped\r
4. **Feature Extraction Only**: If you already have fully preprocessed NIfTI files, you can extract features directly\r
\r
This adaptability allows DUNE to fit into existing neuroimaging workflows and accommodate different data preparation stages.\r
\r
## Project Structure\r
\r
```\r
DUNE\r
├── data\r
│   ├── models           # Pre-trained model weights\r
│   └── templates        # Registration templates (MNI, OASIS)\r
├── scripts\r
│   └── preprocessing    # ANTs-based preprocessing scripts\r
├── src\r
│   ├── pipeline         # Core pipeline components\r
│   │   ├── dicom.py     # DICOM conversion and NIfTI handling\r
│   │   ├── preprocessing.py # Image preprocessing\r
│   │   └── feature_extraction.py # Feature extraction with BrainAE model\r
│   ├── utils            # Utility modules\r
│   │   ├── file_handling.py # File operations\r
│   │   └── logger.py    # Logging functionality\r
│   └── main.py          # CLI interface and pipeline orchestration\r
└── config.yaml          # Default configuration\r
```\r
\r
## The DUNE Model\r
\r
DUNE's feature extraction is powered by a UNet-based autoencoder architecture without skip connections (U-AE):\r
\r
- **Encoder**: Compresses the 3D brain volume through multiple convolutional blocks\r
- **Bottleneck**: Creates a compact latent representation of the brain structure (low-dimensional embeddings)\r
- **Decoder**: Reconstructs the original image from the latent representation\r
\r
This unsupervised approach learns to capture both obvious and subtle imaging features, creating a numerical "fingerprint" of each scan that preserves important structural information while dramatically reducing dimensionality.\r
\r
## Citation\r
\r
If you use DUNE in your research, please consider citing:\r
\r
```\r
@article{barba2025dune,\r
  author = {Barba T, Bagley BA, Steyaert S, Carrillo-Perez F, Sadée C, Iv M, Gevaert O},\r
  title = {DUNE: a versatile neuroimaging encoder captures brain complexity across three major diseases: cancer, dementia and schizophrenia},\r
  year = {2025},\r
  url = {https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.02.24.25322787v1.full}\r
}\r
```\r
\r
