    ns1:description """> [!NOTE]\r
> All data files in the `src/ethos/tokenize/maps` directory are under the CC0 public domain waiver.\r
\r
# ETHOS - EHR foundation model\r
\r
This repository implements Adaptive Risk Estimation System (ARES) for Hospital Mortality, ICU\r
Admission, Prolonged Length of Stay, and Composite (HM+IU+PLoS). In addition, it contains all the\r
experiments conducted in our paper ([preprint](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06124)). It builds on our\r
previous work on EHR foundation models by completely\r
reimplementing [ETHOS](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-024-01235-0) (formerly\r
available [here](https://github.com/ipolharvard/ethos-paper)) to achieve higher performance, improved usability and robustness.\r
\r
## Features\r
\r
- In this release, we demonstrate that ETHOS outperforms the baselines presented in our paper and\r
  introduce ARES, a method for explaining patient health trajectories. We test ETHOS on the MIMIC-IV\r
  with the MIMIC-IV-ED extension dataset on all most common tasks in the domain.\r
- A key feature of ETHOS is zero-shot prediction, where known patient health trajectories are\r
  extrapolated to generate future patient health trajectories (fPHT). This process is repeated to\r
  derive probability estimates.\r
- The current main model architecture that we use is GPT2 (no bias). Feel free to experiment\r
  with architectures specifically suited for EMR.\r
- ETHOS is not a natural language model. It uses a specialized language designed to succinctly\r
  describe patient health events, which we believe helps the model learn better representations and\r
  token relationships.\r
- This implementation uses [MEDS](https://github.com/Medical-Event-Data-Standard/meds) as an\r
  intermediate data representation.\r
- We provide a full pipeline that includes tokenization, training, and inference.\r
- We invite everyone to a discussion in the Issues section of this repository.\r
\r
<p align="center">\r
<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06124">\r
  <img src="./figures/ethos_ares_workflow.png" width="70%" alt="ETHOS-ARES workflow">\r
</a>\r
</p>\r
\r
## Paper reproducibility\r
\r
We provide the complete code necessary to reproduce all experiments presented in the paper.\r
\r
Additionally, all precomputed inference results of our experiments are available in\r
`results.tar.gz` [[GigaDB (1.1GB)]](https://doi.org/10.5524/102752).\r
Once unpacked in the project's root directory, this file will allow generating\r
all the figures in the paper using the notebooks: `notebooks/figures.ipynb` and\r
`notebooks/trajectories.ipynb`. The test set on which these results were inferred can be easily\r
recreated by running the MEDS extraction and tokenization pipelines (see below). MEDS guarantees the\r
same data split if run on the same data with the same configuration (see `scripts/meds`).\r
\r
We do not publish the tokenized dataset or the pretrained model due to restrictions on MIMIC\r
derivatives, and we conclude that these components are not suitable for publication on PhysioNet.\r
\r
## Pre-tokenization step\r
\r
ETHOS tokenization uses an intermediate\r
format [MEDS](https://github.com/Medical-Event-Data-Standard/meds), extracted via\r
the [MEDS_transforms](https://github.com/mmcdermott/MEDS_transforms) pipeline. Scripts for running\r
this pipeline are located in `scripts/meds`.\r
\r
Below is an example command to run the extraction (where `$suffix` should be "ed" or empty). In case\r
of `ed`, it requires the MIMIC-IV-ED extension to be present in the input directory:\r
\r
```bash\r
export N_WORKERS=7\r
\r
# Please define output_dir, strongly suggested is <PROJECT_ROOT>/data\r
suffix="ed" # or ""\r
\r
bash run_mimic.sh \\\r
    "$MIMIC_IV_DIR" \\\r
    "$output_dir/mimic-2.2-premeds" \\\r
    "$output_dir/mimic-2.2-meds-$suffix" \\\r
    "$suffix"\r
```\r
\r
Note, that using 7 workers for the tokenization requires around 250GB of RAM peak usage. You can\r
reduce the memory requirement by reducing the number of workers.\r
\r
In the [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06124), data is split into 90% training and 10% testing.\r
It can be adjusted in the `scripts/meds/mimic/configs/extract_MIMIC.yaml` file. Note, that keys:\r
`train`, `tuning` and `held_out` have to be always present in the config file, but can be set to\r
null.\r
\r
```yaml\r
split_and_shard_subjects:\r
    ...\r
    split_fracs:\r
        train: 0.9\r
        test: 0.1\r
        tuning: null\r
        held_out: null\r
```\r
\r
Once, the data extraction is complete, you can tokenize using the `ethos_tokenize` command, demonstrated\r
in `scripts/run_tokenization.sh`. Ensure the file hierarchy matches what the script expects, or\r
modify the script accordingly before running.\r
\r
## Installation\r
\r
[Optional] We strongly encourage the use of a virtual environment, for example, Conda:\r
To create a new conda env:\r
\r
```bash\r
conda create --name ethos python=3.12\r
conda activate ethos\r
```\r
\r
Fetch the project and set it up in the development mode (`-e`) and install all necessary\r
dependencies for running notebooks and scripts by executing:\r
\r
```bash\r
git clone https://github.com/ipolharvard/ethos-ares\r
cd ethos-ares\r
pip install -e .[jupyter]\r
```\r
\r
## Package Scripts\r
\r
After installing the package, the below commands will become available for running from the command line.\r
\r
1. `ethos_tokenize` - tokenizes data in the MEDS format into Patient Health Timelines.\r
\r
Example of tokenization with parameters used for MIMIC-IV.\r
\r
```bash\r
ethos_tokenize -m worker='range(0,7)' \\  # spawns 7 workers\r
    input_dir=$input_dir/train \\  # `input_dir` is the path to the MEDS data\r
    output_dir=$output_dir \\\r
    out_fn=train\r
\r
ethos_tokenize -m worker='range(0,2)' \\\r
    input_dir=$input_dir/test \\\r
    vocab=$output_dir/train \\  # uses vocab created on the training split\r
    output_dir=$output_dir \\\r
    out_fn=test\r
```\r
\r
See the full example in `scripts/run_tokenization.sh`.\r
\r
2. `ethos_train` - runs the model training.\r
\r
Example of training a model in the 8GPU setting.\r
\r
```bash\r
torchrun --no_python --standalone --nproc_per_node=8 ethos_train \\\r
  data_fp=$data_path/train \\\r
  val_size=6 \\ # uses the last 6M tokens of train as the validation dataset\r
  batch_size=$BATCH_SIZE \\\r
  max_epochs=300 \\\r
  out_dir="$data_path/models/${model_name}" # the path to save model checkpoints\r
```\r
\r
See the full example in `scripts/run_training.sh`.\r
\r
3. `ethos_infer` - runs the inference of a chosen downstream tasks.\r
\r
Example of running the zero-shot inference for the 30-day readmission task with 32 repetitions per sample in the 8GPU setting.\r
\r
```bash\r
ethos_infer \\\r
    task=readmission \\ # see ethos.inference.constants for all available tasks\r
    model_fp=$model_dir/$model/best_model.pt \\\r
    input_dir=$dataset_dir/test \\\r
    output_dir=results/$task_name/$dataset_$model \\\r
    output_fn=rep_size_32_\\$(date +%Y-%m-%d_%H-%M-%S) \\\r
    rep_num=32 \\\r
    n_gpus=8\r
```\r
\r
Example of running the zero-shot inference for the ICU admission task on the random 40% of the whole test set.\r
\r
```bash\r
ethos_infer \\\r
    task=icu_admission \\\r
    model_fp=$model_dir/$model/best_model.pt \\\r
    input_dir=$dataset_dir/test \\\r
    output_dir=results/$task_name/$dataset_$model \\\r
    output_fn=rep_size_8\\$(date +%Y-%m-%d_%H-%M-%S) \\\r
    rep_num=8 \\\r
    subset=0.4\r
```\r
\r
Example of generating a synthetic dataset with the same demographic properties as the `train` dataset. Requires the `ethos_synth` step to convert it into a fully-fledged dataset that can be used for training.\r
\r
```bash\r
ethos_infer \\\r
    task=synthetic \\\r
    model_fp=$model_dir/$model/best_model.pt \\\r
    input_dir=$dataset_dir/train \\\r
    output_dir=results/$task_name/$dataset_$model \\\r
    output_fn=synthetic_\\$(date +%Y-%m-%d_%H-%M-%S) \\\r
    save_generated_tokens=true \\ # it is crucial to saves the trajectories\r
    n_gpus=8\r
```\r
\r
See the full example in `scripts/run_inference.sh`.\r
\r
## Cite us\r
\r
If you use ETHOS or ETHOS-ARES in your research, please cite our work:\r
\r
[[1]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06124)\r
Renc, P., Grzeszczyk, M. K., Oufattole, N., Goode, D., Jia, Y., Bieganski, S., ... & Sitek, A. (\r
2025).\r
Foundation Model of Electronic Medical Records for Adaptive Risk Estimation. arXiv preprint arXiv:\r
2502.06124.\r
\r
```\r
@misc{renc2025ehrfoundationmodel,\r
      title={Foundation Model of Electronic Medical Records for Adaptive Risk Estimation},\r
