    ns1:name "NanoFreeLunch" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1858?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1862?version=5> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:applicationSubCategory <http://edamontology.org/topic_0622>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3325> ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-07-23" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-07-23" ;
    ns1:description """# Introduction\r
\r
**CBRA** (CIBERER Bioinformatics for Rare diseases Analysis) is a workflow optimized for the analysis of rare diseases, designed to detect SNVs, INDELs, CNVs and SVs in targeted sequencing data (CES/WES) as well as whole genome sequencing (WGS).\r
\r
This pipeline is developed using Nextflow, a workflow management system that enables an easy execution across various computing environments. It uses Docker or Singularity containers, simplifying setup and ensuring reproducibility of results. The pipeline assigns a container to each process, which simplifies the management and updating of software dependencies. When possible, processes are sourced from nf-core/modules, promoting reusability across all nf-core pipelines and contributing to the broader Nextflow community.\r
\r
# Pipeline summary\r
\r
The pipeline can perform the following steps:\r
\r
- **Mapping** (`mapping = true`) of the reads to reference (BWA-MEM) \r
- Process BAM file (`GATK MarkDuplicates`, `GATK BaseRecalibrator` and `GATK ApplyBQSR`)\r
- **Single Nucleotide Variant (SNVs) calling** (`variant_calling = true`) with the following tools:\r
\r
  - GATK4 Haplotypecaller (`run_gatk = true`). This subworkflow includes:\r
    - **GATK4 Haplotypecaller**.\r
    - **Hard Filters** and **VarianFiltration** to mark PASS variants. More information [here](docs/variant_calling.md).\r
    - **Bcftools Filter** to keep PASS variants on chr1-22, X, Y.\r
    - **Split Multialletic**.\r
  - Dragen (`run_dragen = true`). This subworkflow includes:\r
    - **GATK4 Calibratedragstrmodel**\r
    - **GATK4 Haplotypecaller** with `--dragen-mode`.\r
    - **VarianFiltration** with `--filter-expression "QUAL < 10.4139" --filter-name "DRAGENHardQUAL"`to mark PASS variants. More information [here](https://gatk.broadinstitute.org/hc/en-us/articles/4407897446939--How-to-Run-germline-single-sample-short-variant-discovery-in-DRAGEN-mode).\r
    - **Bcftools Filter** to keep PASS variants on chr1-22, X, Y.\r
    - **Split Multialletic**.\r
  - DeepVariant (`run_deepvariant = true`). This subworkflow includes:\r
    - **DeepVariant makeexamples**: Converts the input alignment file to a tfrecord format suitable for the deep learning model.\r
    - **DeepVariant callvariants**: Call variants based on input tfrecords. The output is also in tfrecord format, and needs postprocessing to convert it to vcf.\r
    - **DeepVariant postprocessvariants**: Convert variant calls from callvariants to VCF, and also create GVCF files based on genomic information from makeexamples. More information [here](https://github.com/nf-core/modules/tree/master/modules/nf-core/deepvariant).\r
    - **Bcftools Filter** to keep PASS variants on chr1-22, X, Y.\r
    - **Split Multialletic**.\r
\r
  - In addition to these three variant callers, a trio analysis can be performed with option `trio_analysis = true`. Based on [GATK guides](https://gatk.broadinstitute.org/hc/en-us/articles/360035531432-Genotype-Refinement-workflow-for-germline-short-variants). This subworkflow includes: \r
    - **GATK4 Haplotypecaller**.\r
    - **GATK4 Genomicsdbimport** to merge GVCFs from multiple samples\r
    - **GATK4 Genotypegvcfs** to perform joint genotyping\r
    - **Hard Filters** and **VarianFiltration** to mark PASS variants. More information [here](docs/variant_calling.md).\r
    - **Bcftools Filter** to keep PASS variants on chr1-22, X, Y.\r
    - **GATK4 Calculategenotypeposteriors** to calculate genotype posterior probabilities given the family \r
    - **GATK4 Variantfiltration** based on genotypeposterior GQ<20\r
    - **GATK4 Variantannotator** to annotate possible de novo mutations in trios\r
    - **Filter proband ref**: filter variants that are REF in the proband\r
    - **Split Multialletic**.\r
\r
  \r
- **Additional analysis:** Expansion Hunter (`--run_expansionhunter true`) for targeted genotyping of short tandem repeats (STRs) and flanking variants.\r
\r
- **Merge and integration** of the vcfs obtained with the different tools.\r
- **Annotation of SNVs** (`annotation = true`) of the variants:\r
  - Regions of homozygosity (ROHs) with [AUTOMAP](https://github.com/mquinodo/AutoMap)\r
  - Effect of the variants with [Ensembl VEP](https://www.ensembl.org/info/docs/tools/vep/index.html) using the flag `--everything`, which includes the following options: `--sift b, --polyphen b, --ccds, --hgvs, --symbol, --numbers, --domains, --regulatory, --canonical, --protein, --biotype, --af, --af_1kg, --af_esp, --af_gnomade, --af_gnomadg, --max_af, --pubmed, --uniprot, --mane, --tsl, --appris, --variant_class, --gene_phenotype, --mirna`\r
  - Postvep format VEP tab demilited output and filter variants by minor allele frequency (`--maf`).\r
  - You can enhance the annotation by incorporating gene rankings from [GLOWgenes](https://www.translationalbioinformaticslab.es/tblab-home-page/tools/glowgenes), a network-based algorithm developed to prioritize novel candidate genes associated with rare diseases. To add GLOWgenes, set the parameter `--glowgenes true`. GLOWgenes will run using the list of genes specified in the `--gen_list` parameter (an example [here](https://github.com/CIBERER/CBRA/blob/devel/assets/gene_list.csv)). If you already have a GLOWgenes ranking file, you can add it directly using `--glowgenes_ranking`. Precomputed rankings based on PanelApp gene panels are available [here](https://github.com/TBLabFJD/GLOWgenes/blob/master/precomputed_panelAPP/GLOWgenes_precomputed_panelAPP.tsv). To include a specific GLOWgenes ranking, use the option `--glowgenes_ranking (path to the panel.txt)`, for example: `--glowgenes_ranking https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TBLabFJD/GLOWgenes/refs/heads/master/precomputed_panelAPP/GLOWgenes_prioritization_Neurological_ciliopathies_GA.txt`. \r
\r
  - Additionally, you can include the Gene-Disease Specificity Score (SGDS) using: `--sgds`. This score ranges from 0 to 1, where 1 indicates a gene ranks highly for only a few specific diseases (high specificity), and 0 indicates the gene consistently ranks highly across many diseases (low specificity). \r
\r
- **Copy number variants (CNVs) calling** (`cnvs = true`), with the following steps:\r
  - **Bed file filtering**: Module to filter the bed file used for targered sequencing, to keep only the regions with a length > `--min_target` (default 20) and to exclude the regions in `--chromosomes` (default 'chrX,X,chrY,Y,chrM,MT'). \r
  - **Software for detecting CNVs**: These tools require a set of samples sequenced in the same batch in order to detect changes in coverage that indicate the presence of a CNV.\r
    - [ExomeDepth](https://github.com/vplagnol/ExomeDepth) (`exomedepth = true`). \r
    - [panelcn.MOPS](https://github.com/bioinf-jku/panelcn.mops) (`panelcmops = true`).\r
    - [CoNVaDING](https://github.com/molgenis/CoNVaDING) (`convading = true`).\r
  - **Combining the results from the various CNVs caller**\r
  - **CNVs Annotation**: [AnnotSV](https://lbgi.fr/AnnotSV/) is used to annotate the merged results. AnnotSV needs the annotations files. They can be downloaded using `annotsv_install_annotations = true`. The path to the notes folder can be specified using `--annotsv_annotations folder_path`. If `--annotsv_annotations` is not specified, the annotations files will be downloadad directly. \r
\r
\r
# Usage\r
\r
First, prepare a samplesheet with your input data:\r
\r
```\r
sample,fastq_1,fastq_2\r
SAMPLE_PAIRED_END,/path/to/fastq/files/AEG588A1_S1_L002_R1_001.fastq.gz,/path/to/fastq/files/AEG588A1_S1_L002_R2_001.fastq.gz\r
```\r
\r
Each row represents a pair of paired end fastq files. \r
\r
You can run the pipeline using: \r
\r
```\r
nextflow run CBRA/main.nf \\\r
   -profile <docker/singularity/.../institute> \\\r
   --input samplesheet.csv \\\r
   --outdir <OUTDIR>\r
```\r
\r
For more details and further functionality, please refer to the [usage](docs/usage.md) documentation.\r
\r
\r
# Pipeline output\r
\r
For details about the output files and reports, please refer to the [output](docs/output.md) documentation.\r
\r
# Credits\r
\r
CBRA was developed within the framework of a call for intramural cooperative and complementary actions (ACCI) funded by CIBERER (Biomedical Research Network Centre for Rare Diseases).\r
\r
**Main Developer**\r
- [Yolanda Benítez Quesada](https://github.com/yolandabq)\r
\r
**Coordinator**\r
- [Carlos Ruiz Arenas](https://github.com/yocra3)\r
\r
**Other contributors**\r
- [Graciela Uría Regojo](https://github.com/guriaregojo)\r
- [Pedro Garrido Rodríguez](https://github.com/pedro-garridor)\r
- [Rafa Farias Varona](https://github.com/RafaFariasVarona)\r
- [Pablo Minguez](https://github.com/pminguez)\r
- [Daniel Lopez](https://github.com/dlopez-bioinfo)\r
- [Cristina Arias Sardá](https://github.com/CrisAriasSarda)\r
\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1862" ;
    ns1:keywords "bioinformatics",
        "genomics",
        "impact-isciii",
        "nextflow" ;
