<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1865?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:applicationSubCategory <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0622> ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-08-12" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-01-27" ;
    ns1:description """# WHALE: (W)orkflow for (H)uman-genome (A)nalysis of (L)ong-read (E)xperiments\r
\r
## Introduction\r
\r
**WHALE** is a bioinformatics pipeline based on Nextflow and nf-core for long-read DNA sequencing analysis. It takes a samplesheet as input and performs quality control, alignment, variant calling and annotation.\r
\r
## Pipeline summary\r
\r
1. Read QC ([`FastQC`](https://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/projects/fastqc/))\r
2. Present QC for raw reads ([`MultiQC`](http://multiqc.info/))\r
3. Alignment ([`Minimap2`](https://github.com/lh3/minimap2))\r
4. Variant calling\r
    - Single Nucleotide Variant (SNV) calling ([`DeepVariant`](https://github.com/google/deepvariant), [`Clair3`](https://github.com/HKU-BAL/Clair3), [`NanoCaller`](https://github.com/WGLab/NanoCaller))\r
    - Structural Variant (SV) calling ([`Sniffles2`](https://github.com/fritzsedlazeck/Sniffles), [`CuteSV`](https://github.com/tjiangHIT/cuteSV), [`SVIM`](https://github.com/eldariont/svim))\r
5. Merge variant calling\r
6. Annotation\r
    - SNV annotation ([`VEP`](https://github.com/Ensembl/ensembl-vep))\r
    - SV annotation ([`AnnotSV`](https://github.com/lgmgeo/AnnotSV))\r
\r
## Usage\r
\r
First, prepare a samplesheet with your input data. Depending on which step of the analysis you want to run, the input data type can be: fastq, bam (and bai), vcf or bed. The samplesheet should look as follows:\r
\r
`samplesheet.csv`:\r
\r
```csv\r
sample,fastq\r
A123,/path/to/your/input/file/A123.fastq.gz\r
B456,/path/to/your/input/file/B456.fastq.gz\r
```\r
\r
There are two types of full analysis:\r
- SNV analysis: -profile snv_analysis\r
- SV analysis: -profile sv_analysis\r
    \r
  Each full analysis can start with:\r
  - Alignment: --step mapping (input data type: fastq) (default)\r
  - Variant calling: --step variant_calling (input data type: bam and bai)\r
    \r
A specific step of the analysis can be executed:\r
- SNV calling (and merge): -profile snv_calling (input data type: bam and bai)\r
- SV calling (and merge): -profile sv_calling (input data type: bam and bai)\r
- SNV annotation: -profile snv_annotation (input data type: vcf)\r
- SV annotation: -profile sv_annotation (input data type: bed)\r
\r
Profiles to use in the CCC (UAM):\r
- -profile uam,singularity,batch\r
- -profile uam_allcontigs,singularity,batch\r
\r
Profiles to use in the server:\r
- -profile tblabserver,singularity\r
- -profile tblabserver_allcontigs,singularity\r
\r
## Examples\r
\r
SNV and SV analysis starting with variant calling in the server:\r
\r
```bash\r
nextflow run WHALE \\\r
   -profile snv_analysis,sv_analysis,tblabserver,singularity \\\r
   --input samplesheet.csv \\\r
   --outdir <OUTDIR> \\\r
   --step variant_calling\r
```\r
\r
SV calling in the CCC:\r
\r
```bash\r
nextflow run WHALE \\\r
   -profile sv_calling,uam,singularity,batch \\\r
   --input samplesheet.csv \\\r
   --outdir <OUTDIR>\r
```\r
\r
## Pipeline output\r
\r
**WHALE** will create the following subdirectories in the output directory:\r
- alignment\r
- snv_calling\r
  - snv_merge\r
- snv_annotation\r
- sv_calling\r
  - sv_merge\r
- sv_annotation\r
  - overlapping_sv_samples\r
- multiqc\r
- pipeline_info\r
\r
## Citations\r
\r
An extensive list of references for the tools used by the pipeline can be found in the [`CITATIONS.md`](CITATIONS.md) file.\r
\r
Illustration by [Yolanda Benítez](https://github.com/yolandabq)\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1865" ;
    ns1:keywords "bioinformatics",
        "genomics",
        "impact-isciii",
        "long-read-sequencing",
        "nextflow" ;
    ns1:license "MIT" ;
    ns1:name "WHALE" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1865?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1866?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:applicationSubCategory <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0622> ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-08-12" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-01-27" ;
    ns1:description """# GLOWgenes\r
Prioritization of gene diseases candidates by disease-aware evaluation of heterogeneous evidence networks\r
Visit www.glowgenes.org for more information\r
\r
## Citing\r
de la Fuente L, Del Pozo-Valero M, Perea-Romero I, Blanco-Kelly F, Fernández-Caballero L, Cortón M, Ayuso C, Mínguez P. Prioritization of New Candidate Genes for Rare Genetic Diseases by a Disease-Aware Evaluation of Heterogeneous Molecular Networks. International Journal of Molecular Sciences. 2023; 24(2):1661. https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms24021661\r
\r
## Requirements\r
\r
R (tested with version 3.5.0). \r
R packages: optparse, caret\r
\r
Python 2.7 or 3.6\r
\r
Python packages: numpy (tested with version 1.11.0), pandas (tested with version 0.19.0), scipy (tested with version 0.18.1), sklearn (tested with version 0.0), networkx (tested with version 3.0)\r
\r
## Obtaining network files\r
Download network files from: Minguez, Pablo (2022): GLOWgenesNets.zip. figshare. Dataset. https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.21408393.v1\r
\r
You could also generate your own networks or selected a subset from theose provided by GLOWgenes\r
\r
## Editing networks config file\r
Edit networks_knowledgeCategories.cfg file with your complete directory route to the network files\r
e.g. substitute PATH by home/pablo/GLOWgenesNets in every line, as in: /PATH/coexpressionCOXPRESdbEXT_HGNCnets.txt\r
\r
## Running GLOWgenes\r
\r
usage: GLOWgenes.py [-h] -i INPUT -n NETWORKS -o OUTPUT [-t] [-p]\r
                    [-f FILTERING] [-en EXPNORM] [-co CUTOFF] [-r RATIO]\r
\r
\r
python GLOWgenes.py -i diseaseGenes.txt -n networks.cfg -o outputdir -p\r
\r
Use complete paths to avoid errors\r
\r
## Parameters\r
\r
Mandatory parameters:\r
\r
**-i --input INPUT**\r
File listing known associated disease genes\r
\r
**-n --networks NETWORKS**\r
Evidence network config file. Three tab-separated fields: network path, network name, network category\r
\r
[Default network config file](networks_knowledgeCategories.cfg)\r
\r
DEFAULT NETWORK CONFIG FILE IS LOCATED AT TEST FOLDER\r
\r
**-o --output OUTPUT**\r
Output directory\r
\r
**-p, --panelapp**       \r
Disease-associated genes in PanelApp format\r
\r
Gene Panels from PanelApp can be download from https://panelapp.genomicsengland.co.uk/panels/.\r
\r
**-t, --timeprinted**     \r
Knowledge accumulation approach.\r
  \r
**-f FILTERING, --filtering FILTERING**\r
List of candidate genes. Edges involving genes not listed here are filtered from networks\r
  \r
**-en EXPNORM, --expnorm EXPNORM**\r
Expression levels file. Two tab-separated fields: gene name, expression level               \r
\r
\r
**-co CUTOFF, --cutoff CUTOFF**\r
Maximum seed initialization value when considering gene expression levels. Range 0-1\r
  \r
  \r
**-r RATIO, --ratio RATIO**\r
Training ratio for random training/test splits\r
\r
## Running an example\r
Within directory example you have full intructions to test GLOWgenes\r
""" ;
