<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1954?version=4> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.1954.4" ;
    ns1:creator "Evangelos Karatzas",
        "Martin Beracochea" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-01-19" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-10-17" ;
    ns1:description """## Introduction\r
\r
**nf-core/proteinfamilies** is a bioinformatics pipeline that generates protein families from amino acid sequences and/or updates existing families with new sequences.\r
It takes a protein fasta file as input, clusters the sequences and then generates protein family Hidden Markov Models (HMMs) along with their multiple sequence alignments (MSAs).\r
Optionally, paths to existing family HMMs and MSAs can be given (must have matching base filenames one-to-one) in order to update with new sequences in case of matching hits.\r
\r
### Check quality and pre-process\r
\r
Generate input amino acid sequence statistics with ([`SeqFu`](https://github.com/telatin/seqfu2/)) and pre-process them (i.e., gap removal, convert to upper case, validate, filter by length, replace special characters such as `/`, and remove duplicate sequences) with ([`SeqKit`](https://github.com/shenwei356/seqkit/))\r
\r
### Create families\r
\r
1. Cluster sequences ([`MMseqs2`](https://github.com/soedinglab/MMseqs2/))\r
2. Perform multiple sequence alignment (MSA) ([`FAMSA`](https://github.com/refresh-bio/FAMSA/) or [`mafft`](https://github.com/GSLBiotech/mafft/))\r
3. Optionally, clip gap parts of the MSA ([`ClipKIT`](https://github.com/JLSteenwyk/ClipKIT/))\r
4. Generate family HMMs and fish additional sequences into the family ([`hmmer`](https://github.com/EddyRivasLab/hmmer/))\r
5. Optionally, remove redundant and/or merge similar families by comparing family representative sequences against family models with ([`hmmer`](https://github.com/EddyRivasLab/hmmer/))\r
6. Optionally, from the remaining families, remove in-family redundant sequences by strictly clustering with ([`MMseqs2`](https://github.com/soedinglab/MMseqs2/)) and discarding non-cluster representatives\r
7. If in-family redundancy was not removed, reformat the `.sto` full MSAs to `.fas`, for downstream analyses compatibility, with ([`HH-suite3`](https://github.com/soedinglab/hh-suite))\r
8. Optionally, infer sequence phylogeny, by calculating the maximum parsimonious likelihood estimation trees for the final full MSAs with ([`CMAPLE`](https://github.com/iqtree/cmaple))\r
9. Present statistics for remaining/updated family size distributions and representative sequence lengths ([`MultiQC`](http://multiqc.info/))\r
\r
### Update families\r
\r
1. Find which families to update by comparing the input sequences against existing family models with ([`hmmer`](https://github.com/EddyRivasLab/hmmer/))\r
2. For non hit sequences, continue in the [`Create families`](#create-families) paragraph above. For hit sequences and families continue to point 3 below\r
3. Extract family sequences ([`SeqKit`](https://github.com/shenwei356/seqkit/)) and concatenate with filtered hit sequences of each family\r
4. Optionally, remove in-family redundant sequences by strictly clustering with ([`MMseqs2`](https://github.com/soedinglab/MMseqs2/)) and keeping cluster representatives\r
5. Perform multiple sequence alignment (MSA) ([`FAMSA`](https://github.com/refresh-bio/FAMSA/) or [`mafft`](https://github.com/GSLBiotech/mafft/))\r
6. Optionally, clip gap parts of the MSA ([`ClipKIT`](https://github.com/JLSteenwyk/ClipKIT/))\r
7. Update family HMM with ([`hmmer`](https://github.com/EddyRivasLab/hmmer/))\r
\r
### Prepare downstream samplesheets\r
\r
Optionally, prepare the downstream samplesheets for the `nf-core/proteinfold` and `nf-core/proteinannotator` pipelines.\r
\r
## Usage\r
\r
> [!NOTE]\r
> If you are new to Nextflow and nf-core, please refer to [this page](https://nf-co.re/docs/usage/installation) on how to set-up Nextflow. Make sure to [test your setup](https://nf-co.re/docs/usage/introduction#how-to-run-a-pipeline) with `-profile test` before running the workflow on actual data.\r
\r
First, prepare a samplesheet with your input data that looks as follows:\r
\r
`samplesheet.csv`:\r
\r
```csv\r
sample,fasta,existing_hmms_to_update,existing_msas_to_update\r
CONTROL_REP1,input/mgnifams_input_small.faa,,\r
```\r
\r
Each row contains a fasta file with amino acid sequences (can be zipped or unzipped).\r
Optionally, a row may contain tarball archives (tar.gz) of existing families' HMM and MSA folders, in order to be updated.\r
In this case, the HMM and MSA files must be matching in numbers and in base filenames (not the extension).\r
Hit families/sequences will be updated, while no hit sequences will create new families.\r
\r
Now, you can run the pipeline using:\r
\r
```bash\r
nextflow run nf-core/proteinfamilies \\\r
   -profile <docker/singularity/.../institute> \\\r
   --input samplesheet.csv \\\r
   --outdir <OUTDIR>\r
```\r
\r
> [!WARNING]\r
> Please provide pipeline parameters via the CLI or Nextflow `-params-file` option. Custom config files including those provided by the `-c` Nextflow option can be used to provide any configuration _**except for parameters**_; see [docs](https://nf-co.re/docs/usage/getting_started/configuration#custom-configuration-files).\r
\r
For more details and further functionality, please refer to the [usage documentation](https://nf-co.re/proteinfamilies/usage) and the [parameter documentation](https://nf-co.re/proteinfamilies/parameters).\r
\r
## Pipeline output\r
\r
To see the results of an example test run with a full size dataset refer to the [results](https://nf-co.re/proteinfamilies/results) tab on the nf-core website pipeline page.\r
For more details about the output files and reports, please refer to the\r
[output documentation](https://nf-co.re/proteinfamilies/output).\r
\r
## Credits\r
\r
nf-core/proteinfamilies was originally written by Evangelos Karatzas.\r
\r
We thank the following people for their extensive assistance in the development of this pipeline:\r
\r
- [Martin Beracochea](https://github.com/mberacochea)\r
\r
## Contributions and Support\r
\r
If you would like to contribute to this pipeline, please see the [contributing guidelines](.github/CONTRIBUTING.md).\r
\r
For further information or help, don't hesitate to get in touch on the [Slack `#proteinfamilies` channel](https://nfcore.slack.com/channels/proteinfamilies) (you can join with [this invite](https://nf-co.re/join/slack)).\r
\r
## Citations\r
\r
If you use nf-core/proteinfamilies for your analysis, please cite the article as follows:\r
\r
> **nf-core/proteinfamilies: A scalable pipeline for the generation of protein families.**\r
>\r
> Evangelos Karatzas, Martin Beracochea, Fotis A. Baltoumas, Eleni Aplakidou, Lorna Richardson, James A. Fellows Yates, Daniel Lundin, nf-core community, Aydin Buluç, Nikos C. Kyrpides, Ilias Georgakopoulos-Soares, Georgios A. Pavlopoulos & Robert D. Finn\r
>\r
> _biorxiv._ 2025 Aug. doi: [10.1101/2025.08.12.670010](https://dx.doi.org/10.1101/2025.08.12.670010).\r
\r
You can cite the nf-core/proteinfamilies zenodo record for a specific version using the following doi: [10.5281/zenodo.14881993](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14881993).\r
\r
An extensive list of references for the tools used by the pipeline can be found in the [`CITATIONS.md`](CITATIONS.md) file.\r
\r
You can cite the `nf-core` publication as follows:\r
\r
> **The nf-core framework for community-curated bioinformatics pipelines.**\r
>\r
> Philip Ewels, Alexander Peltzer, Sven Fillinger, Harshil Patel, Johannes Alneberg, Andreas Wilm, Maxime Ulysse Garcia, Paolo Di Tommaso & Sven Nahnsen.\r
>\r
> _Nat Biotechnol._ 2020 Feb 13. doi: [10.1038/s41587-020-0439-x](https://dx.doi.org/10.1038/s41587-020-0439-x).\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1954" ;
    ns1:keywords "bioinformatics",
        "nextflow",
        "nf-core",
        "protein-families",
        "protein-sequences",
        "proteins" ;
    ns1:license "MIT" ;
    ns1:name "nf-core/proteinfamilies" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1954?version=4> ;
    ns1:version "4" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1955?version=2> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:applicationSubCategory <http://edamontology.org/topic_0080>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0196>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3301> ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.1955.2" ;
    ns1:creator "Ryo Mameda",
        "Sora Yonezawa" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-02" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """\r
![GitHub last commit (branch)](https://img.shields.io/github/last-commit/RyoMameda/workflow_cwl/main)\r
![Status](https://img.shields.io/badge/status-development-yellow)\r
[![cwltool](https://img.shields.io/badge/cwltool-3.1.20250110105449-success)](https://github.com/common-workflow-language/cwltool/releases/tag/3.1.20250110105449)\r
[![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-blue.svg)](./LICENSE)\r
![Version](https://img.shields.io/badge/version-1.0-brightgreen)\r
[![Open in Dev Containers](https://img.shields.io/static/v1?label=Dev%20Containers&message=python3.11&color=blue&logo=docker)](https://github.com/yonesora56/plant2human/tree/main/.devcontainer)\r
\r
# Gene Expression Analysis Workflow in Complex Microbiomes\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
## Workflow Schema \r
- more details: [Optimization of Mapping Tools and Investigation of Ribosomal RNA Influence for Data-Driven Gene Expression Analysis in Complex Microbiomes](https://doi.org/10.3390/microorganisms13050995)\r
\r
![image](./image/microorganisms-13-00995-g001.png)\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
### 1. Overview of the Workflow\r
\r
This analysis focuses on transcriptional profiling of complex microbiomes. It requires both metagenomic and metatranscriptomic NGS short-read data, along with annotation reference information (e.g., ribosomal RNA sequences and referenced protein databases, listed below). The metagenomic and metatranscriptomic reads should be derived from the same microbiome samples. Assembled metagenomic contigs are then used as reference sequences to map both types of reads, enabling gene-level quantification.\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
### 2. Minimum Requirements\r
\r
- `Docker`\r
- `cwltool`\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
### 3. Workflow Component\r
\r
This analysis workflow is composed of three sub-workflows; metagenomic contig assembling, reads mapping and annotation. \r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
#### Metagenomic contig assembling\r
\r
In this process, the following steps are performed:\r
\r
\r
1. Assembly process using `Megahit`. \r
2. Prediction Protein sequences using `Prodigal`.\r
3. Statical analysis of contigs useing `SeqKit`.\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
#### Reads mapping\r
\r
In this process, the following steps are performed:\r
\r
1. Mapping process using `BWA MEM`.\r
2. Statical analysis of mapping results using `SAMtools`\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
#### Annotation\r
\r
In this process, the following steps are performed:\r
\r
1. Searching contaminated ribosomal RNA sequences using `BLAST`.\r
2. Searching referenced proteins using `DIAMOND`.\r
3. Creation GTF formated file contained annotation informations.\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
### 4. Test Dataset and Your Own Dataset\r
\r
- If you are testing with the following files, please place them in the `Data` directory!\r
- You can also obtain metagenomic and metatranscriptomic FASTQ files either by downloading them from public databases or by using your own samples, and then place them in your `Data` directory.\r
\r
#### Metagenome data\r
\r
- [SRR27548858](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/?term=SRR27548858)\r
\r
#### Metatranscriptome data\r
\r
- [SRR27548863](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/?term=SRR27548863)\r
- [SRR27548864](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/?term=SRR27548864)\r
- [SRR27548865](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/?term=SRR27548865)\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
### 5. Annotation References\r
\r
These reference files are used in the BLAST and DIAMOND processes. The downloaded files are available in the `Data` directory (accessed on September 17, 2025). If you wish to use the latest versions of the references, please download them using the following scripts.\r
\r
```bash\r
# rRNA data from SILVA website (release138.1; accessed on 17,September,2025)\r
curl -O https://ftp.arb-silva.de/release_138.1/Exports/SILVA_138.1_LSUParc_tax_silva.fasta.gz\r
curl -O https://ftp.arb-silva.de/release_138.1/Exports/SILVA_138.1_SSUParc_tax_silva.fasta.gz\r
\r
# Swiss-Prot data from UniProt for diamond makedb process (accessed on 17,September,2025)\r
curl -O https://ftp.uniprot.org/pub/databases/uniprot/current_release/knowledgebase/complete/uniprot_sprot.fasta.gz\r
\r
# Pfam data from InterPro (accessed on 17,September,2025)) for hmmscan proess. Appling HMMER process in this workflow is on going, however this process takes time. This step will be optional.\r
# curl -O https://ftp.ebi.ac.uk/pub/databases/Pfam/current_release/Pfam-A.hmm.gz\r
```\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
### 6. Command Execution\r
\r
We recommend creating a `cache` directory to store cache and intermediate files. Since metagenomic and metatranscriptomic reads are mapped to contigs, the assembled results can be reused to reduce analytical costs. The `cwltool` properly recognizes caches when the `--cachedir` option is specified.\r
\r
```bash\r
# main workflow\r
\r
cwltool --debug --cachedir <cache directory> --outdir <output directory> ./Worlkflow/main_w.cwl ./config/main_w.yml\r
\r
```\r
\r
&nbsp;\r
\r
### 7. based shell script & python script\r
\r
GitHub: https://github.com/RyoMameda/workflow\r
\r
This workflow is developed at [DBCLS BioHackathon 2025](https://github.com/dbcls/bh25/), and the preprint of developing project is https://doi.org/10.37044/osf.io/qd5sz_v1.\r
""" ;
