    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-03" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """# WorkflowHub Knowledge Graph\r
\r
A tool to generate a knowledge graph from a source of RO Crates. By default, this tool sources and generates an RDF graph of crates from [WorkflowHub](https://workflowhub.eu/). \r
\r
[![Docker build and push](https://github.com/workflowhub-eu/workflowhub-graph/actions/workflows/push-docker.yaml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/workflowhub-eu/workflowhub-graph/actions/workflows/push-docker.yaml)\r
[![Python linting and tests](https://github.com/workflowhub-eu/workflowhub-graph/actions/workflows/lint-and-test.yaml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/workflowhub-eu/workflowhub-graph/actions/workflows/lint-and-test.yaml)\r
[![Build and publish knowledge graph](https://github.com/workflowhub-eu/workflowhub-graph/actions/workflows/publish-kg.yaml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/workflowhub-eu/workflowhub-graph/actions/workflows/publish-kg.yaml)\r
\r
## v1.0.0\r
This version of the workflow was used to generate version 2025-08-29 of the WorkflowHub Knowledge Graph (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16995374).\r
\r
It is documented by the report "EuroScienceGateway MS6: Integrated EuroScienceGateway knowledge graph" https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16992674.\r
\r
## Getting Started\r
\r
This tool is run as a Snakemake workflow. We recommend building a Docker container to run the workflow:\r
\r
```bash \r
docker build -t knowledgegraph .\r
```\r
\r
Then, you can run the workflow using the following command:\r
\r
```bash\r
docker run --rm -v ./workflow-output:/app/output --user $(id -u):$(id -g) knowledgegraph\r
```\r
\r
Where `./workflow-output` is the directory where the output will be stored (already created for you in this repo) and the `--user` flag ensures that the output files are created with the correct permissions.\r
\r
## Structure\r
\r
![workflow dag](docs/images/dag.svg)\r
\r
- **`source_ro_crates`**: This rule sources RO crates from the WorkflowHub API (`source_crates.py`) \r
- **`create_graph`**: This rule merges the individual RO crates into a single RDF graph\r
- **`enrich_graph`**: This rule processes the base graph and adds additional metadata from external sources e.g. WikiData, Orcid\r
- **`merge_graphs`**: This rule merges the base graph and enrichment graphs\r
- **`consolidate`**: This rule collapses duplicate entries around canonical objects to make the graph easier to navigate\r
\r
[!TIP]\r
\r
This diagram is generated with:\r
\r
`docker run --entrypoint '' knowledgegraph snakemake --dag | dot -Tsvg > docs/images/dag.svg`\r
\r
## Visualisation / exploration \r
\r
Bundled in this repo is a stack which allows the knowledge graph to be explored visually and interactively.\r
\r
The containers in the stack provide:\r
- A triplestore to make SPARQL queries against\r
- A visualisation tool\r
- A one-shot tool to configure the visualisation tool\r
\r
To view the visualisation run:\r
\r
```bash\r
# run the workflow as above\r
cd vis\r
docker compose down -v # clears configuration, skip if first run, refine if confident with Docker\r
docker compose up\r
# View visualisation on localhost:4200\r
```\r
\r
## Contributing\r
\r
### Coding Style\r
\r
- **Code Formatting**: We use [Python Black](https://black.readthedocs.io/en/stable/) for code formatting. Please format your code using Black before submitting a pull request (PR)\r
- **Type Hinting**: Please use type hints ([PEP 484](https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0484/)), and docstrings ([PEP 257](https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0257/)) in methods and classes.\r
\r
### Branching Strategy\r
\r
- **Branch Naming**: When working on a new feature or bug fix, create a branch from `develop`. e.g. `feature/description` or `bugfix/description`.\r
- **Development Branch**: The `develop` branch is currently our main integration branch. Features and fixes should target `develop` through PRs.\r
- **Feature Branches**: These feature branches should be short-lived and focused. Once done, please create a pull request to merge it into `develop`.\r
\r
## License\r
\r
[BSD 2-Clause License](https://opensource.org/license/bsd-2-clause)\r
""" ;
