    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1985?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description "The workflow will primarily focus on estimation of the maximum magnitude using various deterministic and statistical models available in the literature. The workflow consists of 5 steps. The first four steps belong to the CB-AGEF1 and ultimately aim at creation of the advanced seismic catalog. The last 5th step (belonging to the CBAGEF3-1) takes the available hydraulic data and advanced seismicity catalog derived in steps 1-4 and performs the actual assessment of the maximum magnitude. Step 1 is an application existing on the platform performing picking of raw waveform data. The output of the Step 1 is passed to the Step 2 (Phase Association module, in development) which gathers the picks from waveform data originating from different stations and performs the coincidence trigger. The output sets of picks are then passed to the 3rd step which performs assessment of earthquake location using the TRMLoc module already available on the EPISODES platform. The information on the location event is then passed to step 4 which uses waveform data and location data to calculate basic source parameters. Steps 1-4 are perpetually performed and provide updates to the advanced seismic catalog. The advanced seismic catalog is then used in Step 5 to perform the assessment of the maximum magnitude. Last 5th step of the workflow is its key element - an application (software module) for the EPISODES platform to compute the maximum magnitude estimates based on the variety of deterministic and probabilistic models available in the literature. The input of the application is catalog data (location, time and magnitude) and hydraulic parameters (time and injection rate). The output data include general info (index and time) of time series, computed parameters as time series (e.g. b-value and seismogenic index), maximum magnitude estimates with corresponding standard errors of estimations and other supplementary parameters (number of events, cumulative fluid injection and seismic efficiency ratio). The maximum magnitude models consist of McGarr (2014), Hallo et al. (2014), Li et al. (2022), van der Elst et al. (2016) and Shapiro et al. (2013). In any model, where the Gutenberg-Richter (GR) b-value is needed there are four possibilities: constant b-value equal to 1, maximum-likelihood estimation assuming (a) the left-hand side truncated exponential (Aki 1965) and (b) the tapered GR (Pareto) frequency-magnitude distribution (Kagan 2002) and ‘b-positive’ estimator of van der Elst (2021). The user can compute simultaneously a maximum magnitude assessment using any combination of four b-values and, for example in van der Elst model, with different confidence levels. The application is at the moment tested on the EPISODES platform and is being implemented to be an official, stand-alone application as well." ;
    ns1:identifier "1985" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-AGEF] WF8103: Forecasting maximum magnitude" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1985?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1986?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """The workflow DT-AGEF4 will produce seismic hazard maps related to the nearfuture anthropogenic seismicity (induced or triggered seismicity). The anthropogenic seismicity hazard maps will be related to the time-varying technological factors. The rationales behind the proposed workflow are (Lasocki, S., Proc. Sixth Int. Symp. on Rockburst and Seismicity in Mines, 2005; Lasocki, S. and Orlecka-Sikora, B., Tectonophys., 2008; Orlecka-Sikora, B., Tectonophys., 2008; Lasocki, S., Rockburst Mechanisms, Monitoring, Warning, and Mitigation (Xia-Ting Feng, ed.), Butterworth-Heinemann (Elsevier), Oxford, 2017):\r
1. Anthropogenic seismicity (AS) occurrence is partially controlled by time-varying operation works. Due to the time evolution, probabilistic representations of AS also change in time. Rockmass fracturing in an active source zone will be stationary unless conditions responsible for this process change.\r
2. Activity of AS zones will cease after exploitation, to which they are linked. New zones will appear at locations where exploitation will commence. The source zones in AS are finite in both space and time. Results of the DT-AGEF4 are, in this case, predictions concerning a specified time period in the future.\r
3. The DT-AGEF4 scheme is the general scheme of a short-term ground vibrations forecast that assumes that probabilistic functions describing activity in the whole lifetime of the future seismic zone do not change over a short D time. In result, the problem is solved for the sought value amax, with the exceedance probability p in a single seismic zone, where a is Peak Ground Acceleration (PGA) or Spectral Amplitude (SA). \r
4. In the AGEF4 we estimate possible impacts due to seismic zone that will be active in the future, during the period [D1,D1+D]. In general, however, nothing or not much is known about zone activity during period [D1,D1+D]. In this situation we make use of estimates of probabilistic properties of seismic zones active in the past. The zones active in the past become activity models for future zones. Usually, many activity models for a single future zone are selected. Each alternative model has a’priori probability of realization. Here we propose to use a Multi-Model Ensembles Approach.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1986" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-AGEF] WF8104: Forecasting induced shake map" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1986?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1987?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Johannes Kemper",
        "Laurentiu Danciu",
        "Nicolas Schmid" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """## Overview\r
This repository contains the **Common Workflow Language (CWL)** and **RO-Crate metadata** definition for **WF7101**, the **Data Input** workflow of **DTC-E1**, developed under the [DT-GEO project](https://dtgeo.eu).  \r
\r
The **Data Input workflow (WF7101)** serves as the entry point for the DTC-E1 pipeline. It collects, validates, and prepares essential datasets for downstream workflows, including **Seismogenic Source Models (WF7102)**, **Computational Hazard (WF7103)**, and **Computational Risk (WF7104)**.  \r
\r
It integrates multiple **data sources (DT7101–DT7103)** representing crustal faults, subduction zones, and active fault datasets, and ensures standardized outputs ready for subsequent modeling workflows.\r
\r
## Workflow Structure\r
\r
The workflow is composed of **steps (ST)** and **datasets (DT)**, following DT-GEO’s standardized schema. The general data flow is:\r
\r
1. **Workflow Inputs**\r
   - **DT7101** – Active fault data repository.  \r
   - **DT7102** – Regional crustal fault dataset.  \r
   - **DT7103** – Subduction zone dataset.\r
\r
2. **Preprocessing Steps**\r
   - **ST710101–ST710105:** Validate, harmonize, and convert data into uniform formats compatible with downstream workflows.\r
\r
3. **Workflow Outputs**\r
   - **SUBDUCTION_ZONES** – Standardized subduction zone model dataset.  \r
   - **CRUSTAL_FAULT_MODEL** – Cleaned and validated crustal fault data.  \r
   - **ACTIVE_FAULT_DATASET** – Finalized active fault model used by WF7102.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1987" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophysics" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E1] WF7101: Data Input Workflow" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1987?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1988?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Johannes Kemper",
        "Laurentiu Danciu",
        "Nicolas Schmid" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:description """## Overview\r
This repository contains the **Common Workflow Language (CWL)** and **RO-Crate metadata** definition for **WF7102**, the **Seismogenic Source Models** workflow of **DTC-E1**, developed under the [DT-GEO project](https://dtgeo.eu).  \r
\r
The **WF7102** workflow is responsible for **updating and generating the Seismogenic Source Model (SSM)** by integrating multiple geological datasets. It processes outputs from the **Data Input Workflow (WF7101)** and combines them with additional model data to create an updated representation of earthquake-generating sources used in seismic hazard modeling.  \r
\r
## Workflow Structure\r
\r
The workflow comprises a series of **datasets (DT)** and **steps (ST)** that progressively refine and update the Seismogenic Source Model.\r
\r
1. **Workflow Inputs**\r
   - **DT7104** – Seismogenic Source Model of the *2020 European Seismic Hazard Model (ESHM20)*.  \r
   - **ACTIVE_FAULT_DATASET** – Output from WF7101 describing active fault geometries.  \r
   - **CRUSTAL_FAULT_MODEL** – Validated crustal fault data.  \r
   - **SUBDUCTION_ZONES** – Harmonized subduction zone dataset.  \r
\r
2. **Processing Steps**\r
   - **ST710201:** Initial data ingestion from DT7104 and input datasets.  \r
   - **ST710202:** Preprocessing and format harmonization.  \r
   - **ST710203:** Integration of active fault, crustal fault, and subduction zone models.  \r
   - **ST710204:** Consistency and completeness checks across regions.  \r
   - **ST710205:** Final model assembly and export of the updated Seismogenic Source Model.\r
\r
3. **Workflow Output**\r
   - **DT7108** – *Updated Seismogenic Source Model*, ready for downstream computational hazard workflows (WF7103).\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1988" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophysics" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E1] WF7102: Seismogenic Source Models" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1988?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1989?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Johannes Kemper",
        "Laurentiu Danciu",
        "Nicolas Schmid" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """## Overview\r
This repository contains the **Common Workflow Language (CWL)** and **RO-Crate metadata** definition for **WF7103**, the **Computational Hazard** workflow of **DTC-E1**, developed under the [DT-GEO project](https://dtgeo.eu).  \r
\r
The **WF7103** workflow performs **probabilistic seismic hazard computations** by combining seismogenic source models with computational hazard software. It integrates outputs from the **Seismogenic Source Models Workflow (WF7102)** and base datasets such as the *2020 European Seismic Hazard Model (ESHM20)* to produce hazard maps and related outputs that quantify ground-shaking probabilities across regions.\r
\r
## Workflow Structure\r
\r
The workflow follows a modular structure, using standardized **datasets (DT)** and computational **steps (ST)** to run regional-scale seismic hazard simulations.\r
\r
1. **Workflow Inputs**\r
   - **DT7104** – Seismogenic Source Model of the *2020 European Seismic Hazard Model (ESHM20)*.  \r
   - **DT7108** – *Updated Seismogenic Source Model* generated by WF7102.  \r
\r
2. **Processing Steps**\r
   - **ST710301:** Prepare input model configurations and hazard parameters.  \r
   - **ST710302:** Run seismic hazard simulations using the OpenQuake engine.  \r
   - **ST710303:** Post-process hazard outputs (e.g., ground motion fields, hazard curves).  \r
   - **ST710304:** Validate and archive the results for subsequent risk modeling (WF7104).\r
\r
3. **Workflow Output**\r
   - **DT7109** – *Seismic Hazard Results* containing probabilistic ground-shaking maps, hazard curves, and summary statistics.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1989" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophysics" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E1] WF7103: Computational Hazard" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1989?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1990?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Johannes Kemper",
        "Laurentiu Danciu",
        "Nicolas Schmid" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:description """## Overview\r
This repository contains the **Common Workflow Language (CWL)** and **RO-Crate metadata** definition for **WF7104**, the **Computational Risk** workflow of **DTC-E1**, developed under the [DT-GEO project](https://dtgeo.eu).  \r
\r
The **WF7104** workflow performs **seismic risk computations**, transforming seismic hazard results into quantifiable risk metrics such as loss maps and loss curves. It integrates outputs from the **Computational Hazard Workflow (WF7103)** with exposure and vulnerability datasets from the *2020 European Seismic Risk Model (ESRM20)* to estimate potential economic and structural losses due to earthquakes.\r
\r
## Workflow Structure\r
\r
The workflow builds on standardized **datasets (DT)** and computational **steps (ST)** to assess seismic risk across regions.\r
\r
1. **Workflow Inputs**\r
   - **DT7105** – Input files for the *2020 European Seismic Risk Model (ESRM20)*.  \r
   - **DT7108** – *Updated Seismogenic Source Model* generated by WF7102 and used for consistency with hazard calculations.  \r
\r
2. **Processing Steps**\r
   - **ST710401:** Preprocess exposure and vulnerability data.  \r
   - **ST710402:** Integrate hazard results with exposure and vulnerability datasets.  \r
   - **ST710403:** Run loss estimation simulations using OpenQuake.  \r
   - **ST710404:** Post-process results into loss maps, loss curves, and statistical summaries.  \r
\r
3. **Workflow Output**\r
   - **DT7110** – *Seismic Risk Results: Loss Maps and Loss Curves*, suitable for visualization and decision-support systems.\r
\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1990" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophysics" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E1] WF7104: Computational Risk" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1990?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1991?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Johannes Kemper",
        "Margarita Segou" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:description """## Overview\r
This repository contains the **Common Workflow Language (CWL)** and **RO-Crate metadata** definition for **WF7201**, the **AI-based Seismic Catalogue Generation** workflow of **DTC-E2**, developed under the [DT-GEO project](https://dtgeo.eu).  \r
\r
**WF7201** integrates advanced machine learning models for seismic signal detection and catalog generation within a unified, scalable computational framework. It builds upon **[QuakeFlow](https://github.com/AI4EPS/QuakeFlow)** — a deep-learning-based earthquake monitoring pipeline — to automate waveform ingestion, event detection, association, location, and catalog generation.\r
\r
The workflow enables near-real-time seismic catalogue creation from waveform data, supporting downstream digital twin components for earthquake analysis and forecasting.\r
\r
## Workflow Structure\r
\r
The workflow integrates several sequential stages for data ingestion, AI-driven detection, and catalog production:\r
\r
1. **Workflow Inputs**\r
   - **WAVEFORMS** – Continuous seismic waveform data from multiple stations.  \r
   - **DT7205 (AlpArray Catalogue)** – Example dataset from the *AlpArray Network* (EIDA download via ObsPy).  \r
\r
2. **Processing Steps**\r
   - **ST720101:** Waveform ingestion and pre-processing (e.g., filtering, normalization).  \r
   - **ST720102:** AI-based phase picking using neural network models from QuakeFlow.  \r
   - **ST720103:** Event association to link picks to potential earthquake events.  \r
   - **ST720104:** Event location and magnitude estimation.  \r
   - **ST720105:** High-resolution catalogue generation and export.  \r
\r
3. **Workflow Output**\r
   - **HIGH_RESOLUTION_CATALOG** – AI-generated earthquake catalogue containing high-precision event locations and magnitudes.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1991" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E2] WF7201: AI-based Seismic Catalogue Generation (QuakeFlow)" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1991?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1992?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Johannes Kemper",
        "Leila Mizrahi",
        "Nicolas Schmid" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """## Overview\r
This repository contains the **Common Workflow Language (CWL)** and **RO-Crate metadata** definition for **WF7202**, the **Short-Term Earthquake Forecasting (STEF)** workflow of **DTC-E2**, developed under the [DT-GEO project](https://dtgeo.eu).  \r
\r
The **WF7202** workflow generates **short-term earthquake forecasts** by combining real-time and high-resolution seismic event catalogs with the **Epidemic-Type Aftershock Sequence (ETAS)** model. It enables dynamic, data-driven forecast updates based on the latest seismic activity, contributing to the earthquake digital twin’s predictive capabilities.\r
\r
## Workflow Structure\r
\r
The workflow integrates **data ingestion**, **forecast computation**, and **testing** components that operate together to produce and validate earthquake forecasts.\r
\r
1. **Workflow Inputs**\r
   - **DT7202 – Swiss National Earthquake Catalogue**: Regional earthquake catalog provided by the Swiss Seismological Service (SED).  \r
   - **DT7203 – EMSC Catalog**: European catalog from the EMSC.  \r
   - **DT7201 – Short-term Earthquake Forecasts**: Previously issued forecast data for model updates.  \r
\r
2. **Processing Steps**\r
   - **ST720201:** Data ingestion from real-time catalog sources.  \r
   - **ST720202:** Model calibration and ETAS parameter estimation.  \r
   - **ST720203:** Short-term forecast generation.  \r
   - **ST720204:** Evaluation and testing using the CSEP framework.  \r
   - **ST720205:** Forecast visualization and dissemination through a web service.  \r
\r
3. **Workflow Outputs**\r
   - **DT7201 – Short-term Earthquake Forecasts**: Gridded forecast probabilities and simulated catalogs.  \r
   - **DT7204 – Forecast Test Results**: CSEP testing outputs for model performance assessment.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1992" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophysics" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E2] WF7202: Short-Term Earthquake Forecasting" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1992?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1993?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Graeme Weatherill",
        "Johannes Kemper",
        "Riccardo Zaccarelli" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:description """## Overview\r
This repository contains the **Common Workflow Language (CWL)** and **RO-Crate metadata** definition for **WF7301**, the **Ground Motion Data Processing and Hazard Product Generation** workflow of **DTC-E3**, developed under the [DT-GEO project](https://dtgeo.eu).  \r
\r
The **WF7301** workflow automates the **collection, processing, and validation of ground motion data** to produce updated **hazard and risk products**. It integrates observational datasets with computational tools for model calibration, anomaly detection, and intensity model testing.\r
\r
The workflow supports both real-time and historical seismic data ingestion, providing consistent data products for subsequent digital twin simulations and validation efforts.\r
\r
## Workflow Structure\r
\r
1. **Workflow Inputs**\r
   - **DT7301 – Observed Ground Motion Data**: Ground motion waveforms and intensity measurements from ORFEUS (ESM, RRSM, EIDA).  \r
   - **Simulation Dataset**: Synthetic ground motion simulations generated from computational models.  \r
\r
2. **Processing Steps**\r
   - **ST730101:** Data ingestion and preprocessing using *Stream2Segment*.  \r
   - **ST730102:** Ground motion intensity calculation and statistical correction.  \r
   - **ST730103:** Model validation and ground motion prediction testing with *eGSIM*.  \r
   - **ST730104:** Seismic anomaly detection using *SDAAS* (Simple ML tool for anomaly scoring).  \r
   - **ST730105:** Integration of observed and simulated data for hazard and risk product updates.  \r
\r
3. **Workflow Outputs**\r
   - **DT7303 – Hazard and Risk Products**: Processed and validated datasets containing ground shaking intensities, hazard curves, and derived risk products.\r
\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1993" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophysics" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E3] WF7301: Ground Motion Models" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1993?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1994?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Johannes Kemper",
        "Nico Schliwa" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """## Overview\r
This repository contains the **Common Workflow Language (CWL)** and **RO-Crate metadata** definition for **WF7401**, the **Dynamic Source Inversion** workflow of **DTC-E4**, developed under the [DT-GEO project](https://dtgeo.eu).  \r
\r
The **WF7401** workflow performs **dynamic source inversion of earthquakes** using seismic and geodetic data. It prepares input data, executes high-performance computing (HPC) inversions, and extracts rupture models to characterize the physical parameters of earthquake sources.\r
\r
The workflow leverages the **FD3D_TSN** inversion code, combining a quasi-dynamic boundary element solver and a Bayesian inversion framework with Parallel Tempering Monte Carlo to estimate model parameters and uncertainties.\r
\r
## Workflow Structure\r
\r
1. **Workflow Inputs**\r
   - **DT7401 – Dynamic Source Inversion Input**: Input dataset including fault geometry, velocity models, and seismic and geodetic data from arrays of seismic and GPS stations.  \r
\r
2. **Processing Steps**\r
   - **ST740101:** Input data preparation and format standardization.  \r
   - **ST740102:** Configuration of the FD3D_TSN inversion model.  \r
   - **ST740103:** Execution of HPC-based inversion for rupture dynamics.  \r
   - **ST740104:** Post-processing and extraction of rupture parameters (prestress, friction, misfits).  \r
   - **ST740105:** Generation of final model products and metadata export.  \r
\r
3. **Workflow Outputs**\r
   - **DT7402 – Dynamic Source Inversion Output**: Model input parameters, rupture characteristics, and misfit statistics in text and binary formats.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1994" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophysics" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E4] WF7401: Dynamic Source Inversion Workflow" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1994?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1995?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Bertrand Delouis",
        "Jean-Paul Ampuero",
        "Johannes Kemper" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:description """## Overview\r
This repository contains the **Common Workflow Language (CWL)** and **RO-Crate metadata** definition for **WF7402**, the **Kinematic Inversion** workflow of **DTC-E4**, developed under the [DT-GEO project](https://dtgeo.eu).  \r
\r
The **WF7402** workflow performs **automatic kinematic source inversion** using seismic and geodetic data to produce slip models and kinematically-informed dynamic rupture simulations. It integrates multiple data sources and inversion tools to reconstruct the spatial and temporal evolution of fault slip during an earthquake event.\r
\r
This workflow supports rapid, automated inversion following real or simulated earthquakes, contributing to the DT-GEO digital twin framework for dynamic earthquake modeling and validation.\r
\r
## Workflow Structure\r
\r
1. **Workflow Inputs**\r
   - **DT7403 – Seismograms, GPS offsets, Fault geometry, and Velocity model**: Input dataset including fault geometry, velocity models, and seismic and geodetic observations from arrays of seismic and GPS stations.  \r
\r
2. **Processing Steps**\r
   - **ST740201:** Input data ingestion and preparation.  \r
   - **ST740202:** Fault geometry and velocity model setup.  \r
   - **ST740203:** Execution of the kinematic inversion using the *SLIPNEAR* inversion code.  \r
   - **ST740204:** Model validation and generation of slip distribution results.  \r
   - **ST740205:** Post-processing and export of kinematically-informed dynamic rupture models.  \r
\r
3. **Workflow Outputs**\r
   - **DT7404 – Kinematic Slip Model**: Slip distribution and model predictions from the inversion process.  \r
   - **DT7405 – Kinematically-informed Dynamic Rupture Model**: Dynamic rupture simulations derived from the kinematic results.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1995" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophysics" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E4] WF7402: Kinematic Inversion Workflow" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1995?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1996?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Alice-Agnes Gabriel",
        "Iris Christadler",
        "Mathilde Marchandon" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:description """## Overview\r
This repository contains the **Common Workflow Language (CWL)** and **RO-Crate metadata** definition for **WF7403**, the **Rupture Forecast** workflow of **DTC-E4**, developed under the [DT-GEO project](https://dtgeo.eu).  \r
\r
The **WF7403** workflow performs **physics-based dynamic rupture simulations** to generate a catalog of rupture scenarios for a given fault system. For each scenario, the workflow computes synthetic data such as ground motions, static displacements, and moment-rate release, along with corresponding shakemaps.  \r
\r
In the event of a moderate to large earthquake, WF7403 supports a **rapid-response mode** that automatically compares real-time seismic and geodetic observations with the precomputed scenario catalog to identify the best-fitting rupture model within minutes to hours after the event.\r
\r
## Workflow Structure\r
\r
The workflow is organized into two interconnected sub-workflows:\r
\r
1. **A. Catalog Creation Workflow**\r
   - Generates a large ensemble of forward dynamic rupture simulations using **SeisSol** or **SPECFEM3D**, producing synthetic observables and storing them in the **Simulation Data Lake (SDL)**.  \r
   - This process typically requires high-performance computing (Tier-0/Tier-1 resources) for up to **100 SeisSol runs**, equivalent to ~500,000 core hours.  \r
\r
2. **B. Rapid Response Workflow**\r
   - Continuously monitors real-time earthquake catalogs.\r
   - When an event is detected, it downloads waveform data and performs a **scenario-matching analysis** against the precomputed catalog in the SDL.\r
   - Outputs the best-matching dynamic rupture scenario for rapid situational awareness.\r
\r
### Workflow Inputs\r
- **DT7406 – Input files for dynamic rupture simulation**: Mesh, velocity model, frictional and medium parameters, and initial stress conditions.  \r
- **DT7408 – Seismic and geodetic data**: Real-time data streamed from EPOS infrastructures.  \r
\r
### Workflow Outputs\r
- **DT7409 – Single Dynamic Rupture Scenario**: The best-fitting scenario output from the catalog, including ground-motion and displacement fields.\r
\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1996" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophysics" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E4] WF7403: Rupture Forecast Workflow" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1996?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1997?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Johannes Kemper",
        "Maren Böse",
        "Savas Ceylan" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:description """## Overview\r
This workflow produces **synthetic shaking simulations** for historical earthquake events.  \r
It integrates **SeisComP**, **FinDer**, and the **Swiss, Italian, and European ShakeMap** implementation to process continuous seismic data, estimate rupture parameters, and generate **evolutionary shake maps**.\r
\r
In its full configuration, WF7601 is also capable of:\r
- Sending **event alerts** when specific trigger conditions are met.\r
- Pushing generated ShakeMaps to an external **web portal** for dissemination.\r
\r
**Note:** In this distribution, both the alerting and ShakeMap publishing features are **disabled**.\r
\r
The main CWL definition is provided in `WF7601.cwl`.  \r
Workflow metadata follows the [workflow-ro-crate-1.0](https://w3id.org/workflowhub/workflow-ro-crate/1.0) profile.\r
\r
## Workflow Structure\r
The workflow consists of the following datasets (DT) and software services (SS):\r
\r
1. **Data Ingestion & Preprocessing**\r
   - **DT7601:** Continuous seismic data (waveforms) from seismic stations. Replaced by a *playback* module to mimic data stream using past earthquakes.\r
   - **SS7602:** SeisComP – for seismic data acquisition and processing.\r
\r
2. **Finite Fault Estimation**\r
   - **SS7603:** FinDer – finite fault earthquake early warning algorithm.\r
\r
3. **ShakeMap Generation**\r
   - **SS7601:** Swiss, Italian and European ShakeMap – generates synthetic ground motion maps.\r
\r
4. **Output Storage**\r
   - **DT7606:** Evolutionary shake maps calculated at multiple update intervals between 0-60 s.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1997" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophysics" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E6] WF7601: Synthetic Shaking Workflow" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1997?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1998?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Johannes Kemper",
        "Maren Böse",
        "Savas Ceylan" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-10-14" ;
    ns1:description """## Overview\r
This workflow generates **evolutionary ShakeMaps** by combining multiple parametric data sources:  \r
event alerts, automatic and manual peak motions, and crowdsourced felt reports.\r
\r
It integrates **pyFinDer**, **FinDer**, **ShakeMap**, **RRSM**, and **EMSC** to continuously update ground motion maps as new information arrives.\r
\r
In its full configuration, WF7602 is also capable of:\r
- Sending **event alerts** for each ShakeMap update.\r
\r
**Note:** In this distribution, the alerting feature is **disabled**.\r
\r
The main CWL definition is provided in `WF7602.cwl`.  \r
Workflow metadata follows the [workflow-ro-crate-1.0](https://w3id.org/workflowhub/workflow-ro-crate/1.0) profile.\r
\r
## Workflow Structure\r
The workflow consists of the following datasets (DT) and software services (SS):\r
\r
1. **Data Ingestion**\r
   - **DT7602:** Event alerts from seismic services.\r
   - **DT7603:** Automated peak motions from RRSM.\r
   - **DT7604:** Not Implemented: Felt reports from EMSC.\r
   - **DT7605:** Manual peak motions from ESM.\r
\r
2. **Processing & ShakeMap Generation**\r
   - **SS7602:** Parametric web services and Python wrapper for FinDer (SS7603) \r
   - **SS7603:** FinDer – finite fault estimation.\r
   - **SS7601:** Swiss, Italian and European ShakeMap – produces ground motion maps.\r
\r
3. **Output Storage**\r
   - **DT7606:** Evolutionary shake maps calculated at every update.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1998" ;
    ns1:keywords "digital twin",
        "dt-geo",
        "geophyics" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "[DTC-E6] WF7602: ShakeMaps from Parametric Data" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1998?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/1999?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.1999.1" ;
    ns1:creator "Diane Duroux" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2025-10-17" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2025-10-17" ;
    ns1:description """\r
# ?? Generalizable machine learning models for rapid antimicrobial resistance prediction in unseen healthcare settings\r
\r
This repository contains the code used for the experiments in the paper:\r
\r
**_Generalizable machine learning models for rapid antimicrobial resistance prediction in unseen healthcare settings_**  \r
by *Diane Duroux, Paul P. Meyer, Giovanni Visonà, and Niko Beerenwinkel*.\r
\r
## ⚙️ Install the dependencies\r
You can set up the project with either pip or uv.\r
\r
### Option A - pip:\r
Install the necessary dependencies listed in the requirements.txt file\r
\r
```bash\r
pip install -r requirements.txt\r
```\r
\r
### Option B - uv:\r
We provide pyproject.toml and uv.lock for macOS, Windows, and Linux.\r
\r
Note: On a Linux or non-apple silicon  please use the pyproject.toml file for Mac and rewrite the uv.lock after installation. \r
\r
```bash\r
# 0) Install uv (one-time)\r
# mac/linux:\r
curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh\r
# windows (PowerShell):\r
iwr https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 -UseBasicParsing | iex\r
 \r
# 1) Ensure the pinned Python is available (adjust if your pyproject pins a version)\r
uv python install 3.11\r
 \r
# 2) Create the exact environment from the lockfile\r
uv sync --frozen\r
 \r
# 3) Run your code within the env\r
uv run python -V\r
uv run python your_script.py\r
```\r
\r
## ?? AMR Classifier Training with ResMLP and inference\r
\r
The following command trains a ResMLP model for AMR classification using the preprocessed DRIAMS data.\r
\r
### ?? Output\r
\r
In `output/<experiment_group>/<experiment_name>_results/`, the script generates:\r
\r
- `test_set_seed0.csv`  \r
  ➤ Contains predictions: `species`, `sample_id`, `drug`, `response`, and `Prediction`.\r
\r
### ?? Required Arguments\r
\r
| Argument                | Description                                                                                     |\r
|-------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\r
| `--driams_long_table`   | Path to the metadata file for the current dataset.                                              |\r
| `--spectra_matrix`      | Path to the input mass spectra (either raw or MAE-encoded).                                     |\r
| `--sample_embedding_dim`| Dimension of the spectra input (6000 for raw, or same as <encoding_dim> for MAE).               |\r
| `--drugs_df`            | Path to the antimicrobial compound encoding file.                                               |\r
| `--fingerprint_class`   | Type of encoding: `'morgan_1024'`, `'molformer_github'`, or `'selfies_flattened_one_hot'`.      |\r
| `--fingerprint_size`    | Size of the encoding: 1024 (Morgan), 768 (Molformer), or 24160 (SELFIES).                       |\r
| `--split_type`          | Set to `specific` if splits are pre-defined, else random.                                       |\r
| `--split_ids`           | Path to the `data_splits.csv` file.                                                             |\r
| `--experiment_group`    | Name of the output folder.                                                                      |\r
| `--experiment_name`     | Name of the output subfolder.                                                                   |\r
| `--seed`                | Random seed for reproducibility.                                                                |\r
| `--n_epochs`            | Number of epochs for classifier training.                                                       |\r
| `--learning_rate`       | Learning rate for the optimizer.                                                                |\r
| `--patience`            | Number of epochs to wait before early stopping.                                                 |\r
| `--batch_size`          | Batch size for classifier training.                                                             |\r
\r
### ?? Example: ResMLP Training on DRIAMS B2018 with Raw Spectra + Morgan Fingerprints\r
\r
```bash\r
ulimit -Sn 10000  # Optional: increase file descriptor limit if needed\r
\r
python3 code/ResAMR_classifier.py \\\r
    --driams_long_table ProcessedData/B2018/combined_long_table.csv \\\r
    --spectra_matrix ProcessedData/B2018/rawSpectra_data.npy \\\r
    --sample_embedding_dim 6000 \\\r
    --drugs_df OriginalData/drug_fingerprints_Mol_selfies.csv \\\r
    --fingerprint_class morgan_1024 \\\r
    --fingerprint_size 1024 \\\r
    --split_type specific \\\r
    --split_ids ProcessedData/B2018/data_splits.csv \\\r
    --experiment_group rawMS_MorganFing \\\r
    --experiment_name ResMLP \\\r
    --seed 0 \\\r
    --n_epochs 2 \\\r
    --learning_rate 0.0003 \\\r
    --patience 10 \\\r
    --batch_size 128\r
```\r
\r
---\r
\r
## ?? Funding\r
\r
This research was primarily supported by the ETH AI Center.\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "1999" ;
