    ns1:description """[![GitHub Actions CI Status](https://github.com/WEHI-SODA-Hub/spatialvpt/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/WEHI-SODA-Hub/spatialvpt/actions/workflows/ci.yml)\r
[![GitHub Actions Linting Status](https://github.com/WEHI-SODA-Hub/spatialvpt/actions/workflows/linting.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/WEHI-SODA-Hub/spatialvpt/actions/workflows/linting.yml)[![Cite with Zenodo](http://img.shields.io/badge/DOI-10.5281/zenodo.16973285-1073c8?labelColor=000000)](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16973285)\r
[![nf-test](https://img.shields.io/badge/unit_tests-nf--test-337ab7.svg)](https://www.nf-test.com)\r
\r
[![Nextflow](https://img.shields.io/badge/version-%E2%89%A524.04.0-green?style=flat&logo=nextflow&logoColor=white&color=%230DC09D&link=https%3A%2F%2Fnextflow.io)](https://www.nextflow.io/)\r
[![nf-core template version](https://img.shields.io/badge/nf--core_template-3.3.2-green?style=flat&logo=nfcore&logoColor=white&color=%2324B064&link=https%3A%2F%2Fnf-co.re)](https://github.com/nf-core/tools/releases/tag/3.3.2)\r
[![run with conda](http://img.shields.io/badge/run%20with-conda-3EB049?labelColor=000000&logo=anaconda)](https://docs.conda.io/en/latest/)\r
[![run with docker](https://img.shields.io/badge/run%20with-docker-0db7ed?labelColor=000000&logo=docker)](https://www.docker.com/)\r
[![run with singularity](https://img.shields.io/badge/run%20with-singularity-1d355c.svg?labelColor=000000)](https://sylabs.io/docs/)\r
[![Launch on Seqera Platform](https://img.shields.io/badge/Launch%20%F0%9F%9A%80-Seqera%20Platform-%234256e7)](https://cloud.seqera.io/launch?pipeline=https://github.com/WEHI-SODA-Hub/spatialvpt)\r
\r
## Introduction\r
\r
**WEHI-SODA-Hub/spatialvpt** is a bioinformatics pipeline that performs cell segmentation and creates a QC report for MERSCOPE data using the vizgen-postprocessing tool.\r
\r
![WEHI-SODA-Hub/spatialvpt workflow](docs/images/nf-core-spatialvpt_workflow.svg)\r
\r
The pipeline runs the following steps:\r
\r
- [tiff_segmentation_vpt](https://nf-co.re/subworkflows/tiff_segmentation_vpt/) -- nf-core subworkflow that performs segmentation that uses the [vizgen post-processing tool](https://github.com/Vizgen/vizgen-postprocessing), which runs the following modules:\r
  - [vizgenpostprocessing/preparesegmentation](https://nf-co.re/modules/vizgenpostprocessing_preparesegmentation/)\r
  - [vizgenpostprocessing/runsegmentationontile](https://nf-co.re/modules/vizgenpostprocessing_runsegmentationontile/)\r
  - [vizgenpostprocessing/compiletilesegmentation](https://nf-co.re/modules/vizgenpostprocessing_compiletilesegmentation/)\r
- [vpt convert-geometry](https://vizgen.github.io/vizgen-postprocessing/command_line_interface/index.html#convert-geometry) (optionally, to convert existing segmentation geometries)\r
- `vptupdatemeta` subworkflow, which runs:\r
  - [vpt derive-entity-metadata](https://vizgen.github.io/vizgen-postprocessing/command_line_interface/index.html#derive-entity-metadata)\r
  - [vpt partition-transcripts](https://vizgen.github.io/vizgen-postprocessing/command_line_interface/index.html#partition-transcripts)\r
  - [vpt update-vzg](https://vizgen.github.io/vizgen-postprocessing/command_line_interface/index.html#update-vzg) (optional)\r
- [vpt generate-segmentation-metrics](https://vizgen.github.io/vizgen-postprocessing/command_line_interface/index.html#generate-segmentation-metrics)\r
- [Pipeline information](#pipeline-information) - Report metrics generated during the workflow execution\r
\r
## Usage\r
\r
> [!NOTE]\r
> If you are new to Nextflow and nf-core, please refer to [this page](https://nf-co.re/docs/usage/installation) on how to set-up Nextflow. Make sure to [test your setup](https://nf-co.re/docs/usage/introduction#how-to-run-a-pipeline) with `-profile test` before running the workflow on actual data.\r
\r
First, prepare a parameters file with your input data that looks as follows:\r
\r
`params.yml`:\r
\r
```yaml\r
sample: "sample_name"\r
outdir: "/path/to/results"\r
algorithm_json: "/path/to/algorithm.json"\r
images_dir: "/path/to/images"\r
images_regex: "mosaic_(?P<stain>[\\\\w|-]+)_z(?P<z>[0-9]+).tif"\r
um_to_mosaic_file: "/path/to/micron_to_mosaic_pixel_transform.csv"\r
detected_transcripts: "/path/to/detected_transcripts.csv"\r
update_vzg: true\r
input_vzg: "/path/to/sample.vzg"\r
tile_size: 4096\r
tile_overlap: 400\r
```\r
\r
Note that the `algorithm_json` file is temperamental. Check the [Vizgen documentation](https://vizgen.github.io/vizgen-postprocessing/segmentation_options/json_file_format.html) on the requirements.\r
\r
To run cell segmentation via VPT, you can run the pipeline using:\r
\r
```bash\r
nextflow run WEHI-SODA-Hub/spatialvpt \\\r
   -profile <docker/singularity/.../institute> \\\r
   -params-file params.yml \\\r
   --outdir <OUTDIR>\r
```\r
\r
If you are running with Apptainer or Singularity, you will have to set the following environmental variable before running the pipeline:\r
\r
Apptainer:\r
\r
```bash\r
export NXF_APPTAINER_HOME_MOUNT=true\r
nextflow run WEHI-SODA-Hub/spatialvpt \\\r
   -profile apptainer \\\r
   -params-file params.yml \\\r
   --outdir <OUTDIR>\r
```\r
\r
Singularity:\r
\r
```bash\r
export NXF_SINGULARITY_HOME_MOUNT=true\r
nextflow run WEHI-SODA-Hub/spatialvpt \\\r
   -profile singularity \\\r
   -params-file params.yml \\\r
   --outdir <OUTDIR>\r
```\r
\r
### Custom weights\r
\r
You can use a custom weights file by specifying the _full path_ in your\r
parameters file:\r
\r
```yaml\r
custom_weights: "/path/to/custom_weights_file"\r
```\r
\r
Now you need to add the _file name only_ to your algorithm JSON file, e.g:\r
\r
```json\r
"segmentation_properties": {\r
  "model": "cyto2",\r
  "model_dimensions": "2D",\r
  "custom_weights": "custom_weights_file",\r
  "channel_map": {\r
    "red": "Cellbound1",\r
    "green": "Cellbound3",\r
    "blue": "DAPI"\r
  }\r
},\r
```\r
\r
### Report options\r
\r
You can also set extra options for naming channels and filtering by volume and\r
transcript thresholds when generating reports. See the [VPT documentation](https://vizgen.github.io/vizgen-postprocessing/command_line_interface/index.html#generate-segmentation-metrics)\r
for more details.\r
\r
For example:\r
\r
```yaml\r
red_stain_name: "Cellbound1"\r
green_stain_name: "Cellbound2"\r
blue_stain_name: "DAPI"\r
transcript_count_threshold: 100\r
volume_filter_threshold: 200\r
z_index: 2\r
```\r
\r
### Report-only mode\r
\r
If you would like to only generate a QC report, and you already have your metadata,\r
cell_by_gene and boundary files, you can specify those files in parameters in\r
your params file:\r
\r
```yaml\r
report_only: true\r
metadata: "/path/to/entity_metadata.csv"\r
entity_by_gene: "/path/to/entity_by_gene.csv"\r
boundaries: "/path/to/segmentation.parquet"\r
```\r
\r
```bash\r
nextflow run WEHI-SODA-Hub/spatialvpt \\\r
   -profile <docker/singularity/.../institute> \\\r
   -params-file params.yml \\\r
   --outdir <OUTDIR>\r
```\r
\r
> [!WARNING]\r
> Please provide pipeline parameters via the CLI or Nextflow `-params-file` option. Custom config files including those provided by the `-c` Nextflow option can be used to provide any configuration _**except for parameters**_;\r
> see [docs](https://nf-co.re/usage/configuration#custom-configuration-files).\r
\r
