    ns1:dateCreated "2026-02-08" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-02-09" ;
    ns1:description """# Hep_Ploidy_protocol\r
\r
This is the ploidy identification workflow from the article "Stereo-cell Deciphers the Spatial and Functional Heterogeneity of Polyploid Hepatocytes". \r
The method employs deep learning techniques: Cellpose & StarDist to accurately identify DAPI fluorescence-stained images and brightfield images, respectively. \r
It acquires detailed information on the morphological characteristics and spatial localization of nuclei and cells, which serves as the core process for hepatocyte ploidy identification.\r
Leveraging Stereo-cell technology, this workflow significantly enhances both the quantity of hepatocytes for which ploidy information can be obtained and the number of genes detected in the new workflow.\r
\r
\r
<img width="1391" height="385" alt="new_fig1" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1715f8f5-ea70-429c-8b39-407827760ce4" />\r
\r
\r
<img width="1194" height="616" alt="fig2_cut" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/03830306-d12c-42c0-bf25-c213b490c998" />\r
\r
\r
\r
<img width="1345" height="485" alt="fig2" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b0d844a2-9db1-4def-8132-75c3d831693f" />\r
\r
\r
\r
# Tutorial\r
The example data is accessible at this location: "Hep_Ploidy_protocol/Tutorial/Demon_data.rar"\r
#  Step1: GEM Generates UMI images\r
To map the fluorescence staining signals captured by microscopy to the actual gene expression profiles, we first generate spatial expression heatmaps for the genes of interest, followed by registering the DAPI and bright-field images to the spatial pattern defined by these UMI image.\r
```bash\r
python ../01.GEM Generate UMI image/gem2mask.py -f nCount -g ../Y00723M1_con.gem.gz  -b 1 -n 0 10 -C variable x y value exon -o Y00723M1_con_umi\r
```\r
\r
#  Step2: Staining image registration and quantification \r
```\r
Since the slide is not moved between DAPI and brightfield imaging using the microscope,\r
 the DAPI and brightfield microscopy images are already registered.。\r
Therefore, FIJI ImageJ (https://imagej.net/software/fiji/) is only required to register\r
the DAPI images with the UMI image.\r
\r
1. Preprocessing in ImageJ:\r
Convert both DAPI and brightfield images from RGB to 8-bit in ImageJ to reduce memory consumption.\r
Adjust the image brightness to make the DAPI and UMI image basically consistent.\r
\r
2. TrackEM2 Project Setup:\r
Create a TrackEM2 project in ImageJ, then import the folder containing brightfield image,\r
 DAPI image, and UMI image.\r
Switch the active image to the UMI image. Unlink the UMI image from other images to ensure\r
 that adjustments to the DAPI image do not affect the UMI image. Maintain the link between\r
 the DAPI and brightfield images.\r
Select the DAPI layer as the active layer. Configure the UMI image as the red channel\r
and DAPI as the green channel in the layer settings.\r
\r
3. Registration and Validation:\r
  Register the DAPI image using the Transform tool, After registration,\r
validate the alignment by zooming into multiple fields of view to ensure\r
that the majority of the DAPI signal (green channel) overlaps with the UMI regions(red channel).\r
\r
4. Export and save both the DAPI and brightfield images in TIFF format.\r
```\r
CAUTION!  Do not alter the UMI image at any stage of registration;only the DAPI image is subject to transformation.\r
This ensures that spatial gene expression data (UMI) remains unmodified, preserving the integrity of expression localization during alignment with nuclear features (DAPI)\r
\r
#  Step3: Cell and nuclear segmentation\r
Perform nuclear segmentation on the DAPI image (registered with the UMI image) and cell segmenttation on brightfield image. For the brightfield image, use Cellpose (https://github.com/MouseLand/cellpose) with the cyto3 model.    For the DAPI image, use StarDist (https://github.com/stardist/stardist) with the 2D_versatile_fluo model to generate DAPI masks in StarDist_dapi.py.\r
```bash\r
#Nuclear segmentation on Demon DAPI image\r
python ../02.Cell Segmentation/StarDist_small.py -i ../DAPI_image.tif -o ../dapi_mask.label.csv\r
#The example data is small, so a different StarDist function was used;for details, please refer to the official StarDist tutorial.\r
\r
#Nuclear segmentation on normal DAPI images\r
python ../02.Cell Segmentation/StarDist_dapi.py -i ../DAPI_image.tif -o ../dapi_mask.label.csv\r
```\r
For cell segmentation using Cellpose, we highly recommend its graphical user interface (GUI). For the code-based workflow, please refer to Cellpose's official documentation (https://cellpose.readthedocs.io/en/latest/). Therefore, we only demonstrate the detailed steps of the GUI here.\r
\r
<img width="338" height="285" alt="cellpose_done" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/d1874063-1bea-4dae-a084-23867e2d82c3" />\r
\r
1. load bright image\r
<img width="365" height="240" alt="cellpose_11" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/57ba3ab8-1b18-4899-b28a-52ae7ea14cfa" />\r
\r
2. cell segmentation\r
<img width="290" height="191" alt="cellpose_s2" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0450f2f1-f566-4171-b327-d044319cdcec" />\r
\r
3. save result image\r
<img width="375" height="235" alt="cellpose_s3" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6c7615bd-97b1-4b55-873b-2326d95d22f2" />\r
\r
After completing cell segmentation, we first segment the GEM-format file based on the result files generated by Cellpose.\r
The Cellpose output files in *.png format must be converted to TIFF format prior to segmentation.\r
```bash\r
python ../02.Cell Segmentation/gem_cut.py -g ../Y00723M1.gem.gz -m ../cellpose_mask.tif  -b 1 -o Y00723M1_cell\r
```\r
\r
# Step4: Hepatocytes polyploidy identification\r
\r
In Python, we integrate the cell segmentation results from Cellpose and nuclear segmentation results from StarDist to quantify the number and area of nuclei within cells.\r
\r
```python\r
import sys\r
sys.path.append("../03.Hepatocyte Ploidy Identification/cell_nuclear_stats.py")\r
from cell_nuclear_stats import calculate_dapi_bright_analysis\r
\r
\r
    bright_path = "../cellpose_masks.tif"  # Cellpose Cell Segmentation Result Images\r
    dapi_path = "../dapi_mask.label.csv"   # StarDist Nuclear Segmentation Result File\r
    dapi_raw_path = "../DAPI_image.tif"    # Registered Original DAPI Image\r
\r
    output_file = "../results/" \r
    sample_name = "Y00723M1" \r
\r
calculate_dapi_bright_analysis(dapi_raw_path = dapi_raw_path,\r
                                   bright_path = bright_path,\r
                                   dapi_path = dapi_path,\r
                                   sample_name = sample_name,\r
                                   output_file = output_file\r
                                  )\r
    )\r
```\r
After running calculate_cell_nuclear_stats, a file with the suffix "dtb.csv" will be generated. This file records the features of each nucleus row by row. Next, it is necessary to identify which nuclei are diploid and which are tetraploid.\r
The classification between single-nucleus diploid and single-nucleus tetraploid uses k-means. In essence, k-means will converge on several groups of points with the largest differences, which is similar to the sudden increase in nuclear area from diploid to tetraploid due to chromosome replication.For multiple distinct samples, we recommend merging all the dtb.csv files first, then performing k-means clustering on the combined dataset in a unified manner.\r
Generate single-cell analysis files using the newly generated segmented GEM file (implemented here with R-based Seurat(V4.2.0)).\r
```r\r
library(data.table)\r
library(Seurat)\r
source("../03.Hepatocyte Ploidy Identification/R_function.r")\r
df_gem<-fread("../Y00723M1_cell_cut.gem.gz")\r
rna_m1<-gem2rds(df_gem)  #Use this function to perform the format conversion from GEM to Seurat object.\r
\r
#Add ploidy information to the Seurat object to initiate the analysis.\r
df_m1_ploidy<-read.csv("../Y00723M1_dtb.csv")\r
k_m1<-kmeans(df_m1_ploidy$area,centers = 3)\r
head(k_m1)  #Examine the clustering results based on nuclear area, where we define Cluster 3 as the group with largest mean nuclear areas,Clustre 1 as smallest nuclear areas.\r
df_m1_ploidy$k_cluster<-k_m1$cluster\r
cut_num1<-max(df_mi_ploidy$area[df_m1_ploidy$k_cluster == 1]) #Determine the segmentation threshold\r
cut_num2<-min(df_mi_ploidy$area[df_m1_ploidy$k_cluster == 3])\r
df_m1_meta<-addPloidy(df_m1_ploidy,cut_num1,cut_num2)\r
rna_m1<-AddMetaData(rna_m1,df_m1_meta)\r
\r
saveRDS(rna_m1,"../Y00723M1_seurat.rds")\r
```\r
\r
\r
\r
\r
\r
\r
\r
\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "2079" ;
    ns1:license "MIT" ;
    ns1:name "SCIPI:a tool for hepatocyte ploidy subpopulation identification" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2079?version=3> ;
    ns1:version "3" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2080?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.2080.1" ;
    ns1:creator "Sam Haynes",
        "Sumeet Tiwari" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-02-05" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """# ARG-Sniper\r
\r
A Nextflow pipeline for antibiotic resistance gene detection from paired-end sequencing reads.\r
\r
## Introduction\r
\r
ARG-Sniper is a Nextflow DSL-2 pipeline designed for metagenomic analysis that processes paired-end FASTQ files to detect antibiotic resistance genes using multiple bioinformatics tools. The pipeline runs five different analysis tools in parallel: GROOT, ARIBA, KMA (adopted from ARGprofiler), KARGA, and SRST2, each requiring their respective databases.\r
Users can selectively skip any of the five tools using command-line flags (--skip_groot, --skip_ariba, etc.), allowing for customized analysis workflows. The pipeline takes FASTQ and processes them through the selected tools.\r
After individual tool execution, the pipeline collects all results and generates a summary report that consolidates findings from each analysis. The workflow outputs separate directories for each tool's results along with a final summary directory containing the integrated analysis.\r
\r
Note: This pipeline focuses on detecting antibiotic resistance genes and does not report SNP-based resistance mechanisms.\r
\r
\r
## How-2-Run\r
Before running the pipeline make sure all the required databases and tool-dependencies were met.\r
\r
### Software Requirements\r
- **Nextflow** (≥22.04.0) with DSL-2 support\r
- **Singularity** container runtime\r
\r
### Bioinformatics Tools (via Singularity containers)\r
- **SRST2** v2.0.0 - Short Read Sequence Typing\r
- **GROOT** v1.1.2 - Graph-based resistance gene detection  \r
- **ARIBA** v2.14.6 - Antimicrobial Resistance Identification\r
- **KARGA** v1.02 - K-mer based resistance gene analysis\r
- **KMA** v1.4.9 - K-mer alignment tool (used by ARGprofiler)\r
\r
### Required Databases\r
All tools require pre-built databases from the panARG v2 collection:\r
- grootdb (indexed database)\r
- aribadb (prepared database)\r
- srst2db (FASTA sequences)\r
- kargadb (FASTA sequences) \r
- argprofilerdb (KMA indexed database)\r
- panARG annotations (TSV metadata file)\r
\r
### System Requirements\r
- **CPU:** 8 cores (default)\r
- **Memory:** 16 GB RAM (default)\r
- **Scheduler:** SLURM (for HPC execution)\r
\r
### Usage\r
\r
Run `--help` to see available options:\r
\r
```bash\r
nextflow run ARG-sniper/main.nf --help\r
```\r
\r
```\r
Usage:\r
    nextflow run ARG-Sniper-pipeline.nf --offline -with-report <ARGUMENTS>\r
\r
Required Arguments:\r
    Input:\r
        --reads           Folder containing reads with file name *_R{1,2}.fastq.gz\r
        --gootdb          Path of indexed GROOT database\r
        --aribadb         Path to ARIBA database\r
        --kargadb         Path to KARG database\r
        --srst2db         Path to SRST2 database\r
        --argprofilerdb   Path to ARGprofiler database\r
        --output          Folder for output files\r
\r
# By default, the pipeline will run all supported tools.\r
Optional Arguments:\r
    Skipping specific tools:\r
        --skip_groot      Skip running GROOT\r
        --skip_kma        Skip running KMA\r
        --skip_ariba      Skip running ARIBA\r
        --skip_karga      Skip running KARGA\r
        --skip_srst2      Skip running SRST2\r
```\r
## Expected Output\r
\r
Upon successful execution with all tools, ARG-Sniper generates the following directory structure with results for each sample:\r
\r
```\r
results/\r
├── argprofiler_results/\r
│   └── ARGprofiler_report_{sample}.txt\r
├── ariba_results/\r
│   ├── ariba_report_{sample}.tsv\r
│   └── ariba_summary_{sample}.csv\r
├── groot_results/\r
│   └── groot_report_{sample}.tsv\r
├── karga_results/\r
│   └── karga_report_{sample}.csv\r
├── srst2_results/\r
│   └── srst2_report_{sample}_fullgenes_sequence_results.txt\r
└── summary/\r
    └── summary_{sample}.tsv\r
```\r
\r
**Example output for multiple samples:**\r
```\r
results/\r
├── argprofiler_results/\r
│   ├── ARGprofiler_report_dataset-100x-depth.txt\r
│   ├── ARGprofiler_report_dataset-90x-depth.txt\r
│   └── ARGprofiler_report_dataset-95x-depth.txt\r
├── ariba_results/\r
│   ├── ariba_report_dataset-100x-depth.tsv\r
│   ├── ariba_report_dataset-90x-depth.tsv\r
│   ├── ariba_report_dataset-95x-depth.tsv\r
│   ├── ariba_summary_dataset-100x-depth.csv\r
│   ├── ariba_summary_dataset-90x-depth.csv\r
│   └── ariba_summary_dataset-95x-depth.csv\r
├── groot_results/\r
│   ├── groot_report_dataset-100x-depth.tsv\r
│   ├── groot_report_dataset-90x-depth.tsv\r
│   └── groot_report_dataset-95x-depth.tsv\r
├── karga_results/\r
│   ├── karga_report_dataset-100x-depth.csv\r
│   ├── karga_report_dataset-90x-depth.csv\r
│   └── karga_report_dataset-95x-depth.csv\r
├── srst2_results/\r
│   ├── srst2_report_dataset-100x-depth_fullgenes_sequence_results.txt\r
│   ├── srst2_report_dataset-90x-depth_fullgenes_sequence_results.txt\r
│   └── srst2_report_dataset-95x-depth_fullgenes_sequence_results.txt\r
└── summary/\r
    ├── summary_dataset-100x-depth.tsv\r
    ├── summary_dataset-90x-depth.tsv\r
    └── summary_dataset-95x-depth.tsv\r
```\r
\r
The `summary/` directory contains consolidated results from all tools for each sample.""" ;
