<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2105?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Kelly Sovacool",
        "Vishal Koparde" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-03-05" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-03-05" ;
    ns1:description """# CHAMPAGNE ??\r
\r
**CH**rom**A**tin i**M**muno **P**recipit**A**tion sequencin**G** a**N**alysis pip**E**line\r
\r
[![build](https://github.com/CCBR/CHAMPAGNE/actions/workflows/build.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/CCBR/CHAMPAGNE/actions/workflows/build.yml)\r
[![docs](https://github.com/CCBR/CHAMPAGNE/actions/workflows/docs-mkdocs.yml/badge.svg)](https://ccbr.github.io/CHAMPAGNE)\r
[![DOI](https://zenodo.org/badge/DOI/10.5281/zenodo.10516078.svg)](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.10516078)\r
[![release](https://img.shields.io/github/v/release/CCBR/CHAMPAGNE?color=blue&label=latest%20release)](https://github.com/CCBR/CHAMPAGNE/releases/latest)\r
\r
View the full documentation website: <https://ccbr.github.io/CHAMPAGNE>\r
\r
<a href="https://ccbr.github.io/CHAMPAGNE/latest/diagram/">\r
<figure>\r
  <img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CCBR/CHAMPAGNE/refs/heads/main/docs/diagram/overview.svg" alt="CHAMPAGNE workflow overview diagram">\r
  <figcaption>\r
    <strong>CHAMPAGNE Workflow Overview.</strong>\r
    View a detailed version of the workflow diagram on the website.\r
  </figcaption>\r
</figure>\r
</a>\r
\r
## Set up\r
\r
Champagne is installed on the [Biowulf HPC](#biowulf).\r
For installation in other execution environments,\r
refer to the [docs](https://ccbr.github.io/CHAMPAGNE/).\r
\r
### Biowulf\r
\r
Champagne is available on [Biowulf](https://hpc.nih.gov/) in the `ccbrpipeliner` module.\r
You'll first need to start an interactive session, then load the module:\r
\r
```sh\r
# start an interactive node\r
sinteractive --mem=2g --cpus-per-task=2 --gres=lscratch:200\r
\r
# load the ccbrpipeliner module\r
module load ccbrpipeliner\r
```\r
\r
## Quickstart\r
\r
Initialize and run champagne with test data:\r
\r
```sh\r
# copy the champagne config files to your project directory.\r
# --output is optional and defaults to your current working directory.\r
champagne init --output /data/$USER/champagne_project\r
\r
# preview the champagne jobs that will run with the test dataset\r
champagne run --output /data/$USER/champagne_project \\\r
  --mode local -profile test -preview\r
\r
# launch a champagne run on slurm with the test dataset\r
champagne run --output /data/$USER/champagne_project \\\r
  --mode slurm -profile test\r
```\r
\r
To run champagne on your own data, you'll need to create a sample sheet.\r
Take a look at these examples:\r
\r
- [assets/samplesheet_test.csv](https://github.com/CCBR/CHAMPAGNE/blob/main/assets/samplesheet_test.csv) - mix of single and paired end reads downloaded from github.\r
- [assets/samplesheet_full_mm10.csv](https://github.com/CCBR/CHAMPAGNE/blob/main/assets/samplesheet_full_mm10.csv) - single end reads on biowulf.\r
\r
Once you've created a samplesheet with paths to your fastq files,\r
run champagne with the `--input` option to specify the path to your sample sheet:\r
\r
```sh\r
champagne run --output /data/$USER/champagne_project \\\r
    --mode slurm \\\r
    --input samplesheet.csv \\\r
    --genome hg38\r
```\r
\r
### Parameters\r
\r
You can specify workflow parameters via the command line or in a YAML file.\r
Here's an example YAML file with some common parameters:\r
\r
`assets/params.yml`\r
\r
```YAML\r
input: './assets/samplesheet_full_mm10.csv'\r
contrasts: './assets/contrasts_full_mm10.csv'\r
genome: mm10\r
run_gem: false\r
run_chipseeker: false\r
run_qc: true\r
```\r
\r
You can then use these parameters with the `-params-file` option:\r
\r
```sh\r
champagne run --output /data/$USER/champagne_project \\\r
    --mode slurm \\\r
    -params-file assets/params.yml\r
```\r
\r
View the [full list of parameters](https://ccbr.github.io/CHAMPAGNE/dev/guide/params)\r
in the documentation.\r
\r
### Reference Genomes\r
\r
View [the list of available reference genomes](https://ccbr.github.io/CHAMPAGNE/dev/guide/genomes)\r
to see which genomes are pre-configured for use with champagne on biowulf.\r
If you'd like to use a genome that's not already available, view the\r
[guide on preparing a custom reference genome](https://ccbr.github.io/CHAMPAGNE/dev/guide/genomes/#custom-reference-genome).\r
\r
### Spike-in Control\r
\r
If your experiment uses a spike-in control, you can specify the spike-in genome\r
with the `--spike_genome` parameter:\r
\r
```sh\r
champagne run --output /data/$USER/champagne_project \\\r
    --mode slurm \\\r
    --input samplesheet.csv \\\r
    --genome hg38 \\\r
    --spike_genome dmelr6.32 \\\r
    --deeptools_normalize_using None\r
```\r
\r
View [the spike-in docs](https://ccbr.github.io/CHAMPAGNE/dev/guide/spike-in/)\r
for more information on how to use & customize spike-in controls.\r
\r
## Help & Contributing\r
\r
Come across a **bug**? Open an [issue](https://github.com/CCBR/CHAMPAGNE/issues)\r
and include a minimal reproducible example.\r
\r
Have a **question**? Ask it in\r
[discussions](https://github.com/CCBR/CHAMPAGNE/discussions).\r
\r
Want to **contribute** to this project? Check out the\r
[contributing guidelines](.github/CONTRIBUTING.md).\r
\r
**General Inquiries and Collaboration:** Please contact the CCBR Pipeliner team\r
at [CCBR_Pipeliner@mail.nih.gov](mailto:CCBR_Pipeliner@mail.nih.gov).\r
\r
## References\r
