    ns1:description """Automated screening monitoring workflow designed to provide real-time feedback to the microscope during data acquisition. The pipeline processes movies on-the-fly, performing motion correction, maxshift analysis, CTF estimation and consensus validation, as well as micrograph quality assessment through AI-based categorization. The workflow also includes particle picking, automatic box size estimation, particle extraction, streaming 2D classification, and 2D class quality assessment. Results are integrated into a feedback system that can guide acquisition decisions and optimize screening efficiency in real time. \r
Scipion required plugins: smartscope, pwem, xmipp3, motioncorr, miffi, cistem, emfacilities, sphire, gautomatch, relion, repic\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "2194" ;
    ns1:license "Apache-2.0" ;
    ns1:name "Workflow 2D Smartscope" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2194?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2195?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:applicationSubCategory <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0622>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0781>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3344> ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.2195.1" ;
    ns1:creator "Ammar Aziz",
        "Tristan Reynolds" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-06-18" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-06-25" ;
    ns1:description """# Viral Amplicon Analysis Pipeline for ONT Data\r
\r
# Description\r
\r
Galaxy workflow for processing viral amplicon datasets, such as MPXV (Mpox), sequenced using the Oxford Nanopore Technologies (ONT) platform. The workflow uses the latest Fieldbioinformatics Artic Minion pipeline that uses the [clair3 variant caller](https://www.nature.com/articles/s43588-022-00387-x). \r
\r
The ARTIC minion is a pipeline for working with viral nanopore sequencing data, generated from tiling amplicon schemes. It is designed to help run the artic bioinformatics protocols; for example the [MPXV protocol](https://artic.network/viruses/mpxv/mpxv-ont-epi2me-sop.html). The ARTIC minion pipeline performs genome mapping, variant calling and consensus assembly.\r
\r
# Inputs\r
\r
- **ONT Reads**: Oxford Nanopore Technologies (ONT) sequenced reads from ARTIC assay with fastqsanger encoding. [`fastqsanger`, `fastqsanger.gz`]\r
- **Claire3 Model**: Basecalling model to be used for processing ONT data. The basecalling model must be uploaded to your Galaxy History. Available models can be downloaded from the Rerio repository: [https://github.com/nanoporetech/rerio](https://github.com/nanoporetech/rerio). Please note: By using these models, you agree to the [Oxford Nanopore Technologies, Ltd. Public License agreement](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nanoporetech/rerio/refs/heads/master/LICENCE.txt) [`tar.gz`]\r
- **Normalise Reads**: Sample at most this number of reads per amplicon and strand. This parameter sets the value for the ARTIC minion input `Coverage normalisation depth` (Default value `400`).  [`integer`]\r
- **Oxford Nanopore Technologies, Ltd. Public License agreement**: Users must agree to the [Oxford Nanopore Technologies, Ltd. Public License agreement](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nanoporetech/rerio/refs/heads/master/LICENCE.txt) in order to run this workflow. [`boolean`]\r
- **Maximum Read Length**: Optional input specifying the maximum permitted read length. Reads longer than this value will be discarded during filtering (Default value `2600`). [`integer`]\r
- **Minimum Read Length**: Optional input specifying the minimum permitted read length. Reads shorter than this value will be discarded during filtering (Default value `200`). [`integer`]\r
- **Amplicon scheme name**: Name of the amplicon scheme on labs.primalscheme.com to fetch primer BED and reference (Default value `artic-inrb-mpox`).\r
- **Amplicon scheme version**: Version of the amplicon scheme on labs.primalscheme.com in format `vX.X.X` (Default value `v1.0.0`).\r
- **Amplicon scheme amplicon length**: Length of the amplicons in the scheme. Only required if the scheme named has multiple lengths (Default value `2500`).\r
\r
**NOTE:** Alternatively, the primer `bed` file (`primer.bed`) and reference genome `fasta` file (`reference.fasta`) can be provided from the user's History if previously imported into Galaxy from [labs.primalscheme.com](labs.primalscheme.com).\r
\r
# Outputs\r
\r
- **Collated QC Results**: `MultiQC` combined QC reports output by `Fastplong`. [`html`,`tabular`]\r
- **Collated BAM Files**: Collection of the `bam` files output by `Artic minion`. [`bam`]\r
- **Collated Alignment Reports**: Collection of the alignment reports output by `Artic minion`. [`tabular`]\r
- **Collated VCF Outputs**: Collection of `vcf` files output by `Artic minion`. [`vcf`]\r
- **Combined FASTA Outputs**: Single `fasta` file of all consensus sequences output by `Artic minion`. [`fasta`]\r
- **Plot Depth**: Plot of the mapped read depth calculated by `Mosdepth`. [`pdf`]\r
\r
# Workflow Steps\r
\r
1. Filter ONT reads by length using `Fastplong`.\r
2. Run the `ARTIC minion` pipeline tool to perform the following processing steps.\r
   - Align reads to reference genome using `minimap2`\r
   - Trim primers\r
   - Call variants (mutations)\r
   - Create consensus genome sequence\r
3. Calculate the mapped read depth using `Mosdepth` and convert `bedgraph` output to `tabular` using `Convert bedgraph to tsv`.\r
4. Rename consensus sequence headers produced by `Artic minion` from extended path/file names to the sample barcode (e.g. `>barcode09/KJ642613.1_masked/ARTIC/clair3 KJ642613.1_masked` -> `>barcode09`) using `rename header` and `clean header`.\r
5. Combine `Fastplong` QC reports using `MultiQC`.\r
6. Plot mapped read depth using `Plotting tool`.\r
7. Create a collection of the `Artic minion` output `bam` files using `Build list`.\r
8. Create a collection of the `Artic minion` output alignment reports using `Build list`.\r
9. Create a collection of the `Artic minion` output `vcf` files using `Build list`.\r
10. Merge all `Artic minion` output consensus sequences into a single `fasta` file using `Concatenate datasets`.""" ;
    ns1:hasPart "multiqc" ;
    ns1:identifier "2195" ;
    ns1:keywords "amplicon",
        "mpox",
        "mpxv",
        "ont",
        "phylogeny",
        "virus" ;
    ns1:license "MIT" ;
    ns1:name "Viral Amplicon Analysis Pipeline for ONT Data" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2195?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2196?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:applicationSubCategory <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0622>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0781>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3344> ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.2196.1" ;
    ns1:creator "Ammar Aziz",
        "Tristan Reynolds" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-06-18" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-06-25" ;
    ns1:description """# MPXV (Mpox) Phylogenetic Analysis with Squirrel\r
\r
# Description\r
\r
Galaxy workflow to perform MPXV phylogenetic reconstruction using the Squirrel [(Some QUIck Reconstruction to Resolve Evolutionary Links)](https://github.com/aineniamh/squirrel) Galaxy tools, `Squirrel QC` and `Squirrel Phylo`. The workflow also performs masking (using the squirrel tool) of SNPs. \r
\r
Galaxy Squirrel tools:\r
- `Squirrel QC`: The quality control (QC) mode of Squirrel that can run QC on the alignment and flag certain sites to the user that may need to be masked. Squirrel can flag potential issues in the MPXV sequences that have been provided for alignment (e.g. SNPS near tracts of N, clusters of unique SNPs, reversions to reference alleles and convergent mutations) and outputs these in a mask file for investigation.\r
- `Squirrel Phylo`: The Squirrel mode that performs alignment and creation of maximum-likelihood phylogenetic trees. Squirrel allows for rapidly producing reliable alignments for MPXV and also enable maximum-likelihood phylogenetics pipeline tree estimation. Squirrel maps each query genome in the input file against a reference genome specific to each clade using `minimap2`. Using `gofasta`, the mapping file is then converted into a multiple sequence alignment. Squirrel can also run an APOBEC3-reconstruction and map these mutations onto the phylogeny.\r
\r
\r
# Inputs\r
\r
- **Consensus Sequences**: Input consensus sequences for phylogenetic reconstruction. Must be a single clade (Clade I or Clade II) as clades cannot be mixed. [`fasta`]\r
- **MPXV Clade**: MPXV Clade selected from `cladei`,`cladeia`,`cladeib`,`cladeii`,`cladeiib`. [`text`]\r
- **Run APOBEC Analysis**: Optionally perform APOBEC3-reconstruction and map mutations onto the phylogeny. [`boolean`]\r
- **Plot Height**: Optional input to set the height of the phylogenetic tree plots in inches. [`integer`]\r
- **Plot Width**: Optional input to set the width of the phylogenetic tree plots in inches. [`integer`]\r
\r
# Outputs\r
\r
- **Tree (Newick)**: The output maximum likelihood tree file from `iqtree2` with Node labels that correspond to the reconstruction Node labels. This tree can be viewed in various tree viewers, for example FigTree. [`newick`] \r
- **MSA**: The alignment file, with alignment scaffolded against a clade-specific reference. By default one of the ITR regions and a curated set of problematic regions is masked as Ns. [`fasta`]\r
- **Tree (PNG)**: Visualisation of reconstructed tree showing whether mutations are consistent with APOBEC3 editing or not. [`png`]\r
- **Tree (SVG)**: Visualisation of reconstructed tree showing whether mutations are consistent with APOBEC3 editing or not. [`svg`]\r
- **Branch AA Reconstruction**: A report of each mutation that occurs across the phylogeny, their location, dinucleotide context, APOBEC3 status, which gene they're present in, codon position, amino acid change and a prediction of how extreme that amino acid change is with Grantham score. [`csv`]\r
- **Branch SNPS Reconstruction**: A report of individual site changes mapped to specific branches and their dinucleotide context. [`csv`]\r
\r
# Workflow Steps\r
\r
1. Run `Squirrel QC` to perform QC on the alignment and flag sites that may need to be masked. `Squirrel QC` produces an output `csv` file of flagged sites that is used to mask these sites by `Squirrel Phylo`.\r
2. Run `Squirrel Phylo` to perform the following steps for phylogenetic analysis.\r
   - Alignment of consensus sequences to clade specific reference genome using `minimap2`.\r
   - Masking (replacement with N) of low-complexity or repetitive regions that have been characterised for Clade I and II and sites flagged by `Squirrel QC`.\r
   - Generate a maximum liklihood phylogenetic tree using `iqtree2`.\r
   - Optionally, run APOBEC3 ancestral state reconstruction and map these mutations onto the phylogeny using `iqtree2`.\r
3. Convert the `nexus` format phylogenetic tree output by `Squirrel Phylo` to `newick` format using `Regex Find And Replace`.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "2196" ;
    ns1:keywords "amplicon",
        "mpox",
        "mpxv",
        "ont",
        "phylogeny",
        "virus" ;
    ns1:license "MIT" ;
    ns1:name "MPXV (Mpox) Phylogenetic Analysis with Squirrel" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2196?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2197?version=2> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Sudeep Sahadevan" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-07-16" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-07-16" ;
    ns1:description """# CLIP-seq Workflow\r
\r
A [Nextflow](https://nextflow.io) workflow for end-to-end processing of CLIP-seq data, supporting multiple CLIP protocols.\r
\r
## Overview\r
\r
Starting from raw FASTQ files (or un-demultiplexed iCLIP data), the workflow processes reads through quality control, adapter trimming, rRNA removal, genome alignment, and UMI deduplication, then runs shoji to extract crosslink sites and produce per-sample and combined count matrices ready for differential binding analysis (see [DEWSeq](https://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/DEWSeq.html)).\r
\r
## Workflow steps\r
\r
- **Demultiplexing** *(optional, iCLIP only)*\r
    - [x] [flexbar](https://github.com/seqan/flexbar) (barcode-based demultiplexing) + umi_tools (UMI extraction)\r
- **Quality Control**\r
    - [x] [FastQC](https://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/projects/fastqc/)\r
    - [x] [MultiQC](https://seqera.io/multiqc/)\r
- **UMI pre-processing** *(optional, R2-CLIP only)*\r
    - [x] [umi_tools](https://umi-tools.readthedocs.io/en/latest/QUICK_START.html)\r
- **Adapter and Quality Trimming**\r
    - [x] [fastp](https://github.com/opengene/fastp) (default; single-step or two-step depending on protocol)\r
    - [ ] [cutadapt](https://cutadapt.readthedocs.io/en/stable/) *(partial implementation - used in eCLIP two-step trim)*\r
- **Fastq data sketching and similarity comparison**\r
    - [x] [sourmash](https://github.com/sourmash-bio/sourmash) - plots to check sample group similarity\r
- **rRNA filtering** *(optional)*\r
    - [x] [bbduk](https://github.com/BioInfoTools/BBMap) - to remove rRNA reads using a reference Fasta\r
- **Alignment**\r
    - [x] [STAR](https://github.com/alexdobin/STAR) (single-pass or two-pass)\r
- **Contamination estimation**\r
    - [x] [Kraken2](https://github.com/DerrickWood/kraken2) - run on unmapped reads to detect unexpected organisms\r
- **UMI deduplication** *(optional)*\r
    - [x] [umi_tools](https://umi-tools.readthedocs.io/en/latest/QUICK_START.html) `dedup`\r
- **Downstream processing**\r
    - [x] [shoji](https://github.com/EMBL-Hentze-group/Shoji)\r
- **Final statistics report**\r
    - [x] Per-sample read counts at each processing stage (raw → trimmed → rRNA-filtered → aligned → deduplicated), plus Kraken2 classification summary\r
\r
## Prerequisites\r
\r
- **Java 11+** (required by Nextflow)\r
- **[Nextflow](https://www.nextflow.io/docs/latest/install.html)** - tested versions:\r
  - [`25.04.6`](https://github.com/nextflow-io/nextflow/releases/tag/v25.04.6)\r
  - [`25.10.1`](https://github.com/nextflow-io/nextflow/releases/tag/v25.10.1)\r
- One of the following for software environments:\r
  - **[Apptainer](https://apptainer.org/)** (formerly Singularity) - recommended for HPC, see [apptainer configs](conf/containers/apptainer.config)\r
  - **[Conda](https://conda.io)** / **[Mamba](https://mamba.readthedocs.io)** - see [conda configs](conf/conda/)\r
\r
> :warning: If using conda/mamba, ensure no active conda environment is loaded before launching Nextflow, as it can interfere with the JRE.\r
\r
\r
## Reference files\r
\r
The genome profiles ([conf/genome/](conf/genome/)) contain organism specific reference configs. \r
\r
> :information_source: See [this note](#genomeinfo) about creating genome specific configs\r
\r
Before running, you will need to prepare and configure paths to:\r
\r
| File | Used by | Description |\r
|------|---------|-------------|\r
| STAR genome index | STAR | Build using [`STAR --runMode genomeGenerate`](https://github.com/alexdobin/STAR/blob/master/doc/STARmanual.pdf) against your genome FASTA or FASTA + GTF |\r
| GFF3 annotation | shoji | Gene annotation file ([GENCODE](https://www.gencodegenes.org/)). See [this section](#gff3warn) about using annotation files from non GENCODE sources.|\r
| Genome FAI | tracks | FASTA index (`.fa.fai`) for the genome, used to set chromosome sizes for track generation (see [`samtools faidx`](https://www.htslib.org/doc/samtools-faidx.html)) |\r
| rRNA FASTA | bbduk | Reference sequences for rRNA filtering |\r
| Kraken2 database | Kraken2 | Pre-built Kraken2 database (e.g. from [https://benlangmead.github.io/aws-indexes/k2](https://benlangmead.github.io/aws-indexes/k2)). See **[kraken2](#kraken2) section**  |\r
\r
> :warning: Edit the relevant genome config (e.g. [conf/genome/hsa.config](conf/genome/hsa.config) or [conf/genome/rDNA.config](conf/genome/rDNA.config)) to point to your local copies.\r
\r
### GFF3\r
\r
<a id="gff3warn"></a>\r
> :warning: When using GFF3 files from sources other than GENCODE, shoji paramaters corresponding to gene id, name, type and optionally feature needs to supplied. See [shoji annotation documentation](https://shoji.readthedocs.io/en/latest/documentation.html#id1) for a description of these parameters. In [hsa](conf/genome/hsa.config) and [rDNA](conf/genome/hsa.config) configs, edit the variable `annotation_params` to fit the attribute names in the GFF3 file being used.\r
\r
<a id="kraken2"></a>\r
### Kraken2\r
> :warning: [Kraken2 config](conf/tools/kraken2.config) parameters `db`, `nodes` and `names` are placeholder pathes. Edit these to point to actual files before running the workflow\r
\r
| Parameter | Required file | Description |\r
|------|---------|-------------|\r
|`db`|Kraken2 index file| See [Kraken 2 index](https://benlangmead.github.io/aws-indexes/k2) for a list of downloadable index files|\r
|`nodes`|NCBI taxonomy db `nodes.dmp` file| See [this readme](https://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pub/taxonomy/taxdump_readme.txt)|\r
|`names`|NCBI taxonomy db `names.dmp` file| See [this readme](https://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pub/taxonomy/taxdump_readme.txt)|\r
\r
> :information_source: See this [shell script](./scripts/soniCLIP_hsa_apptainer_shoji.sh) for an example supplying these files using command line parameters\r
\r
## Built-in profiles\r
\r
### Supported protocols\r
\r
| Profile |Sequencing type| Description |\r
|---------|:---------:|-------------|\r
| [`eCLIP`](conf/protocol/eCLIP.config) | paired-end | :warning:  two-step adapter trimming (cutadapt) and UMI deduplication |\r
| [`iCLIP`](conf/protocol/iCLIP.config) | single-end | barcode demultiplexing + UMI extraction via flexbar |\r
| [`R2CLIP`](conf/protocol/R2CLIP.config) | paired-end | Read 2 is expected to contain only UMIs,and after UMI extraction Read 1 is processed as single-end |\r
| [`soniCLIP`](conf/protocol/soniCLIP.config) | single-end | no demultiplexing or deduplication |\r
\r
> :warning: The current version of [eCLIP](./conf/protocol/eCLIP.config) profile is designed to handle UMI-extracted reads available from [the ENCODE portal](https://www.encodeproject.org/search/?type=Experiment&searchTerm=eCLIP)\r
\r
### Genomes\r
\r
| Profile | Description |\r
|---------|-------------|\r
| `hsa` | Human GRCh38 / GENCODE v42 primary assembly |\r
| `rDNA` | Human hg38 with rDNA-masked genome (for rRNA binding RBPs); rRNA trimming disabled by default. rDNA genomes for human and mouse are available from [this reference](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2023.104766) |\r
\r
<a id="genomeinfo"></a>\r
> :information_source: it is also possible to skip creating/using genome configs altogether and supply these reference files using parameters. See [this soniCLIP shell script template](./scripts/soniCLIP_run.sh) for an example\r
\r
### Run environments\r
\r
| Profile | Description |\r
|---------|-------------|\r
| `apptainer` | Runs processes inside Apptainer containers (paths should be configured separately - see [conf/containers/README.md](conf/containers/README.md)) |\r
| `conda` | Creates and caches conda environments per process (see [conf/conda/](conf/conda/) and [conda config](conf/conda/conda.config)) |\r
| `slurm` | SLURM executor settings (see [conf/run/embl_hd.config](conf/run/embl_hd.config)); adapt queue names and resource limits for your cluster |\r
\r
### Using profiles\r
\r
Profiles are combined with commas. See [nextflow.config](./nextflow.config) for the full list.\r
\r
```\r
nextflow run ... -profile slurm,apptainer,eCLIP,hsa\r
```\r
\r
This runs the workflow on a SLURM cluster using Apptainer containers, the eCLIP protocol, and hg38 genome alignment.\r
\r
> :information_source: The `slurm` profile is pre-configured for the EMBL Heidelberg HPC. For other SLURM clusters, copy [conf/run/embl_hd.config](conf/run/embl_hd.config), adjust queue names and resource parameters, and reference your copy in [nextflow.config](./nextflow.config).\r
\r
## Workflow\r
\r
\r
### Sample sheet format\r
\r
This workflow uses [nf-schema](https://nextflow-io.github.io/nf-schema/latest/) plugin and the supported sample sheet format.\r
\r
For `eCLIP`, `R2-CLIP` and `soniCLIP` protocols, the following columns (in csv) is expected:\r
\r
<a id="default"></a>\r
#### eCLIP\r
\r
`eCLIP`: `fastq_2` column **MUST be** provided.\r
\r
|sample|fastq_1|fastq_2|\r
|------|-------|-------|\r
|sample1|/path/to/sample1_R1.fastq.gz| /path/to/sample1_R2.fastq.gz|\r
\r
#### R2-CLIP\r
\r
`R2-CLIP`: `fastq_1` for acutal reads, and `fastq_2` is expected to contain only UMIs. \r
\r
[umi_tools `extract`](https://umi-tools.readthedocs.io/en/latest/reference/extract.html) is used to extract UMIs from `fastq_2` (based on parameter `bc_pattern` in [config file](conf/protocol/R2CLIP.config)) and add them to `fastq_1` headers and are then processed as regular single-end reads.\r
\r
|sample|fastq_1|fastq_2|\r
|------|-------|-------|\r
|sample1|/path/to/sample1_R1.fastq.gz| /path/to/sample1_R2.fastq.gz|\r
\r
#### soniCLIP\r
`soniCLIP`:  only uses `fastq_1`\r
\r
|sample|fastq_1|\r
|------|-------|\r
|sample1|/path/to/sample1.fastq.gz|\r
\r
\r
#### iCLIP\r
For `iCLIP` protocol, the following columns (in csv) is expected:\r
\r
|fastq|barcode|\r
|------|-------|\r
|/path/to/run1.fastq.gz| /path/to/run1_barcode.fa|\r
\r
- `fastq` column contains the path to the raw, un-demultiplexed fastq files.\r
- `barcode` column contains the path to the fasta file with barcodes for demultiplex\r
\r
`barcode` fasta file format example:\r
```\r
>sample_1\r
NNNNATATATATNN\r
>sample_2\r
NNNNCGCGCGCGNN\r
```\r
:information_source: [flexbar](https://github.com/seqan/flexbar) is  used for demultiplexing iCLIP data based on the provided barcodes with corresponding header as sample name. UMIs (`N`s in the sequences) are extracted from the reads during demultiplexing and added to fastq header.\r
\r
:information_source: iCLIP fastq files that are already processed (demultiplexed and UMI extracted) can also be provided, using the same sample sheet format as for eCLIP/R2-CLIP/soniCLIP (with `sample`, `fastq_1` columns) ([see section eCLIP, R2-CLIP and soniCLIP](#default)).\r
\r
### Running the workflow\r
\r
> :warning: most of the example workflows below assumes that there is a genome assembly config with appropriate paths and parameters in the [genome](./conf/genome/) folder and that this assembly is included in the [nextflow config](./nextflow.config) file\r
\r
> ℹ️ see [this shell script](#cmdparam) for supplying these files as command-line arguments\r
\r
#### eCLIP with human genome (hg38) on SLURM using conda\r
\r
See [this shell script](./scripts/eCLIP_hsa_conda.sh)\r
\r
#### iCLIP with human genome (hg38) on SLURM using apptainer\r
\r
See [this shell script](./scripts/iCLIP_hsa_apptainer.sh)\r
\r
#### soniCLIP with human genome (hg38) on SLURM using apptainer with custom shoji parameters\r
\r
See [this shell script](./scripts/soniCLIP_hsa_apptainer_shoji.sh)\r
\r
<a id="cmdparam"></a>\r
#### soniCLIP without using a genome config  on SLURM and conda\r
\r
See [this shell script](./scripts/soniCLIP_custom_conda.sh)\r
\r
> :information_source: The shell script above shows how to use custom genome files without adding a genome config.\r
\r
## Output\r
Given below is an example output directory structure from this pipeline.\r
\r
> :information_source: the output directory is defined by nextflow `-output-dir` parameter, and the files in this directory will be symbolic links to the files in the work directory, defined by nextflow parameter `-work-dir`\r
\r
|Directory|Sub-directory|File|Description\r
|------|-------|------|-------|\r
|Annotation| | |Shoji annotation files|\r
|Fastq| | |Fastq files after trimming|\r
| | rRNA_trim| |after rRNA read removal|\r
| | trim| |after rRNA read removal|\r
|Genome_align| | |Genome alignments|\r
| | alignment| |bam files, alignment statistics,...|\r
| | mapped_fq| |mapped reads in fq format|\r
| | multimapped_fq| |multimapped reads in fq format|\r
| | unmapped_fq| |un-mapped reads in fq format|\r
|Kraken2| | |Kraken 2 output directory|\r
| | contamination_check| |Kraken2 classification files and contamination reports|\r
|QC| | |QC files: fastqc and multiqc files|\r
| | raw| |raw data QC|\r
| | rRNA_trim| |QC after rRNA read removal|\r
| | trim| |QC after adapter trimming|\r
|Shoji| | |Shoji and related outputs|\r
| | counts| |count files from `shoji count`|\r
| | matrix| |Final output matrices for DEWSeq analysis|\r
| | sites| |bed formatted output files from `shoji extract`|\r
| | tracks| |`.bw` files for visualization|\r
|Sourmash| | |Sourmash files and plots|\r
| | align| |for aligned reads|\r
| | kraken2| |after Kraken2 contamination estimation|\r
| | raw| |for raw reads|\r
| | rRNA_trim| |after rRNA read trimming|\r
| | trim| |after adapter trimming|\r
|Stats| | |Read count statistics|\r
| | | all_samples_combined_stats.csv|read count statistics for all samples from raw reads to alignment, deduplication (optional) and contamination estimation|\r
| | |`<sample>`_all_stats.json|per sample read count statistics in json format|\r
\r
\r
Developed at: [Hentze Group](https://www.embl.org/groups/hentze/), EMBL Heidelberg\r
""" ;
