## Citation\r
\r
If you use this workflow in your research, please cite it. Use the **"Cite this\r
repository"** button on the GitHub repository page (generated from\r
[`CITATION.cff`](CITATION.cff)), or cite the archived release on Zenodo via the DOI\r
badge above — the concept DOI always resolves to the latest version.\r
\r
**Please also cite the individual tools used:**\r
- **Snakemake**: Köster, J. and Rahmann, S. (2012). Snakemake—a scalable bioinformatics workflow engine. Bioinformatics, 28(19), 2520-2522.\r
- **MACS2**: Zhang, Y. et al. (2008). Model-based analysis of ChIP-Seq (MACS). Genome Biology, 9, R137.\r
- **deepTools**: Ramírez, F. et al. (2016). deepTools2: a next generation web server for deep-sequencing data analysis. Nucleic Acids Research, 44(W1), W160-W165.\r
- **Bowtie2**: Langmead, B. and Salzberg, S.L. (2012). Fast gapped-read alignment with Bowtie 2. Nature Methods, 9, 357-359.\r
- **SAMtools**: Li, H. et al. (2009). The Sequence Alignment/Map format and SAMtools. Bioinformatics, 25(16), 2078-2079.\r
- **IDR**: Li, Q. et al. (2011). Measuring reproducibility of high-throughput experiments. Annals of Applied Statistics, 5(3), 1752-1779.\r
- **featureCounts (Subread)**: Liao, Y. et al. (2014). featureCounts: an efficient general purpose program for assigning sequence reads to genomic features. Bioinformatics, 30(7), 923-930.\r
- **Consensus peaks (fixed-width / score-per-million)**: Corces, M.R. et al. (2018). The chromatin accessibility landscape of primary human cancers. Science, 362(6413), eaav1898.\r
- **BEDTools**: Quinlan, A.R. and Hall, I.M. (2010). BEDTools: a flexible suite of utilities for comparing genomic features. Bioinformatics, 26(6), 841-842.\r
- **FastQC**: Andrews, S. (2010). FastQC: a quality control tool for high throughput sequence data.\r
- **fastp**: Chen, S. et al. (2018). fastp: an ultra-fast all-in-one FASTQ preprocessor. Bioinformatics, 34(17), i884-i890.\r
\r
