    ns1:dateCreated "2026-07-31" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-07-31" ;
    ns1:description """OdorSig is a fully automated, open-source Python pipeline for reproducible olfactory receptor-odorant molecular docking analysis. The system integrates AutoDock Vina, Open Babel, PyMOL, and Biopython behind a Streamlit interface to deliver an end-to-end workflow requiring only receptor and ligand names as input.\r
Applied to 40 human olfactory receptors and 12 structurally diverse odorants, OdorSig generated a large-scale 480-pair binding affinity dataset, in which 80.6% of pairs demonstrated high docking reproducibility (sigma <= 0.2 kcal/mol) and 36 high-confidence strong binders (delta-G <= -7.0 kcal/mol) were identified.\r
\r
Pipeline stages:\r
1. Receptor sequence retrieval (NCBI) and ligand retrieval (PubChem)\r
2. Homology model generation and structural validation (SWISS-MODEL, Ramachandran-based quality filtering)\r
3. Ligand preparation and format conversion (Open Babel)\r
4. Docking configuration (grid box, exhaustiveness, seed mode)\r
5. Triplicate AutoDock Vina docking execution\r
6. Reproducibility-aware scoring: mean binding energy and standard deviation computed automatically for every receptor-odorant pair\r
7. Results parsing, PyMOL pose visualization, and CSV/figure export\r
\r
By reporting mean binding energy and standard deviation as first-class outputs across triplicate runs, OdorSig turns stochastic single-run docking scores into consistent, comparable receptor-odorant interaction profiles.\r
\r
Source code: https://github.com/ODOR-SIG/Automated-Molecular-Docking Code archive DOI (concept, latest release): 10.5281/zenodo.17814494 \r
Dataset archive DOI (480-pair dataset, raw docking logs): 10.5281/zenodo.20373888""" ;
    ns1:identifier "2237" ;
    ns1:keywords "autodock vina",
        "bioinformatics",
        "cheminformatics",
        "computational olfaction",
        "digital olfaction",
        "gpcr",
        "molecular docking",
        "olfactory receptors",
        "reproducibility",
        "streamlit" ;
    ns1:license "MIT" ;
    ns1:name "OdorSig: Automated Molecular Docking Pipeline" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2237?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2238?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.2238.1" ;
    ns1:creator "Guillaume Lemaire" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-07-31" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-07-31" ;
    ns1:description """# SIGMA : Single-cell Identifier using Gaussian Mixture Approach\r
\r
SIGMA is a bioinformatics pipeline for automated identification of targeted cell types from multiple heterogeneous single-cell transcriptomic datasets (formats supported: `.h5ad`). It is designed to simplify and accelerate integrative large-scale single-cell analyses.\r
\r
## Features\r
\r
- Quality control, filtering, and doublet removal\r
- Extraction of target cell types based on user-defined gene markers\r
- Harmonization and merging of multiple studies\r
- Fully configurable via a single config file\r
\r
## Installation\r
\r
Clone the repository:\r
\r
```bash\r
git clone git@github.com:Guitout31415/SIGMA.git\r
cd SIGMA\r
conda env create -f environment.yml\r
conda activate SIGMA\r
pip install snakemake\r
```\r
\r
## Configuration\r
\r
Edit `config_template.conf` to specify:\r
\r
- **[Metadata]**: Metadata fields to extract/conserve\r
- **[Candidate]**: Candidate genes for initial filtering\r
- **[Target]**: Positive marker genes for target cell identification\r
- **[Exclude1]**: Negative or exclusion marker genes used to exclude cell types 1\r
- **[Exclude2]**: Negative or exclusion marker genes used to exclude cell types 2\r
- **[Thresholds]**: Filtering and assignment thresholds\r
- **[Folder]**: Input/output folder paths\r
- **[Options]**: Species, QC parameters, plotting options\r
\r
The number of components for the Gaussian Mixture Models (`n_components_target` and\r
`n_components_exclu` in **[Thresholds]**) must be set explicitly to `kde` (KDE peak\r
detection), `bic` (reproducible selection at the elbow of the BIC-vs-n_components\r
curve), or a fixed integer. Both `kde` and `bic` are deterministic. With `bic`, a\r
diagnostic BIC curve is saved to the plots folder.\r
\r
## Usage\r
\r
Run the pipeline with Snakemake:\r
\r
```bash\r
snakemake \\\r
  --config file=ConfigFile.conf \\\r
  --cores 10 \\\r
  --jobs 5 \\\r
  --resources mem_mb=100000\r
```\r
\r
- Adjust `--cores` and `--jobs` as needed.\r
- The main workflow is defined in `Snakefile`.\r
\r
### Main Steps\r
\r
1. **Quality control**: Filtering, outlier removal, doublet detection (`scripts/quality_control.py`)\r
2. **Target extraction**: Select cells expressing candidate/marker genes (`scripts/extract_target.py`)\r
3. **Harmonization**: Standardize metadata interface (`scripts/harmonize_metadata.py`)\r
4. **Merging**: Combine all studies into a single `.h5ad` (`scripts/merge_h5ad.py`)\r
\r
## Output\r
\r
- Filtered and processed `.h5ad` files per study: `output_folder/qc/`, `output_folder/find/`, `output_folder/harmonized/`\r
- Merged dataset: `output_folder/merge.h5ad`\r
- Logs: `output_folder/logs/`\r
- Plots: `output_folder/plots/`\r
\r
## Troubleshooting\r
\r
- Check log files in `output_folder/logs/` for errors.\r
- Ensure all dependencies are installed and input files are correctly formatted.\r
\r
## License\r
\r
GPL-3.0 License. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "2238" ;
    ns1:license "GPL-3.0" ;
    ns1:name "SIGMA : Single-cell Identifier using Gaussian Mixture Approach" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2238?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

