<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2311?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.2311.1" ;
    ns1:creator "Cristina Bernad",
        "Katja Gilly",
        "Sonja Filiposka",
        "This work has been funded by the FUMD-AI project, an EOSC GRAVITY - Inter Project with Grant Number 25-EOSC-GRV-INTER-013." ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-09-15" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-09-16" ;
    ns1:description """Turns raw SUMO (vehicle mobility) and OMNeT++/VEINS (cellular network) co-simulation output into a labeled, AI-ready dataset of cellular handover/migration events, for training and evaluating cellular handover / mobility-prediction models in vehicular (V2X) networks.\r
\r
The workflow chains seven Jupyter notebooks — runnable individually or in one call via a master orchestrator notebook (run_pipeline.ipynb) built on papermill — that take a simulation from raw simulator output to a fully labeled feature matrix: parsing raw SUMO fcd-output XML and OMNeT++ .vec vector files; reconstructing per-vehicle trajectories with lagged position history; aligning SUMO and OMNeT/VEINS vehicle identities (including SUMO's teleportation behavior and OMNeT module-slot reuse, both resolved per-timestep rather than collapsed); merging mobility and radio-network metrics (SINR, CQI, RLC delay/throughput, serving cell) into a single per-vehicle-per-timestep table; and classifying every handover into one of four cases — normal handover, delayed (A-B-C), ping-pong (A-B-A), or no-prior-history — plus a diagnostic-only "unstable" category. An optional seventh notebook produces summary figures and diagnostics.\r
\r
The workflow is dependency-light (Python ≥3.10, standard scientific-Python stack, plus sed for large-file cleaning), ships with a tiny bundled synthetic example so every notebook runs out of the box with no external data.""" ;
    ns1:identifier "2311" ;
    ns1:keywords "dataset",
        "handover",
        "jupyter",
        "machine learning",
        "mobility",
        "prediction",
        "urban" ;
    ns1:license "CC-BY-4.0" ;
    ns1:name "FUMD-AI Preprocessing Workflow" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2311?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2312?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.2312.1" ;
    ns1:creator "Cristina Bernad",
        "Katja Gilly",
        "Sonja Filiposka" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-09-16" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-09-16" ;
    ns1:description """This workflow is the training and postprocessing stage of the FUMD-AI pipeline for simulated vehicular mobility and cellular network data (SUMO + OMNeT++). \r
It consumes the AI-ready labeled dataset produced by the companion [FUMD-AI preprocessing workflow](https://doi.org/10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.2311.1) and trains a 2×BiLSTM encoder with a Bahdanau-attention decoder to forecast a vehicle's serving cellular base station 1–7 seconds into the future. \r
The pipeline is implemented as a chain of parameterized, papermill-runnable Jupyter notebooks: \r
* Step 1 loads and windows a labeled dataset (with an alternate \r
* Step 1b for combining multiple simulation runs into one training set); \r
* Step 2 trains the model; \r
* Step 3 evaluates it on a held-out validation split and, optionally, on other independent runs to check cross-run generalization; \r
* Step 4 aggregates and compares metrics across runs or model variants; and an optional \r
* Step 5 plots handover/migration events spatially by type and destination cell, over vehicle positions and (optionally) real base-station coordinates and a street map. \r
 \r
A master notebook chains Steps 1–5 end-to-end and ships with a small bundled example dataset so the full pipeline can be run out of the box with no external data. \r
Batch-run scripts (local and Slurm) support running the pipeline across an arbitrary set of simulation datasets at once, including a combined-dataset training track validated against held-out runs.""" ;
    ns1:hasPart "tensorflow" ;
    ns1:identifier "2312" ;
    ns1:keywords "handover",
        "java",
        "lstm",
        "machine learning",
        "mobility",
        "prediction" ;
    ns1:license "CC-BY-4.0" ;
    ns1:name "FUMD-AI Training & Postprocessing Workflow" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2312?version=1> ;
    ns1:version "1" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2314?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.2314.1" ;
    ns1:creator "Jae Woo Baek",
        "Jinwook Choi",
        "Nayeon Park",
        "Shin Young Park",
        "Songwon Lim",
        "Sunjae Lee",
        "Youngchang Seo" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-09-19" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-09-19" ;
    ns1:description """# M-SCAN RNA-to-pFBA Tutorial\r
\r
M-SCAN builds Human-GEM reaction-capacity constraints from RNA expression,\r
optional deleterious mutation attenuation, predicted kcat values, and medium\r
constraints, then runs pFBA. The bundled three-sample CCLE example reproduces\r
the Figure 2 mini-reference outputs and is also a template for applying the\r
workflow to new datasets.\r
\r
## Quick Start\r
\r
```bash\r
conda env create -f environment.yml\r
conda activate M-SCAN\r
pip install -e .\r
mscan run-all --config configs/demo_ccle.yaml\r
```\r
\r
The same demo can be run with the shell wrapper:\r
\r
```bash\r
bash run_tutorial.sh\r
```\r
\r
Generated files are written to `outputs/`. The default demo runs the full\r
RNA-to-pFBA workflow and checks the resulting Vmax and pFBA outputs against the\r
stored Figure 2 three-sample reference subset.\r
\r
If the environment already exists:\r
\r
```bash\r
conda env update -n M-SCAN -f environment.yml\r
```\r
\r
## What the Demo Runs\r
\r
```text\r
RNA expression\r
  -> protein-coding and Human-GEM gene filtering\r
  -> target-sum normalization and zero-expression floor\r
  -> optional mutation attenuation\r
  -> GPR aggregation with or=sum and and=min\r
  -> optional kcat scaling\r
  -> medium-constrained Human-GEM pFBA\r
```\r
\r
Default inputs:\r
\r
- `data/demo/ccle_rna_depmap_3samples.csv`\r
- `data/demo/ccle_mutation_with_prediction_final_3samples.csv`\r
- `data/kcat/kcat_predictions.csv`\r
- `data/media/cell_media.csv`\r
- `data/model/Human-GEM.xlsx`\r
- `data/model/Human-GEM.mat`\r
\r
Default outputs:\r
\r
- `outputs/tutorial_rna_preprocessed.csv`\r
- `outputs/tutorial_rna_for_vmax.csv`\r
- `outputs/tutorial_applied_mutations.csv`\r
- `outputs/tutorial_vmax.csv`\r
- `outputs/tutorial_pfba_results.csv`\r
\r
## Apply M-SCAN to Another Dataset\r
\r
Copy the demo config and edit the paths:\r
\r
```bash\r
cp configs/demo_ccle.yaml configs/my_dataset.yaml\r
```\r
\r
For a generic gene-by-sample expression matrix, set:\r
\r
```yaml\r
rna_mode: gene_matrix\r
rna: path/to/gene_by_sample_expression.csv\r
mutation_table: path/to/mutation_factors.csv\r
check_reference: false\r
```\r
\r
Then run:\r
\r
```bash\r
mscan run-all --config configs/my_dataset.yaml\r
```\r
\r
Useful command-line overrides:\r
\r
```bash\r
mscan run-all --config configs/my_dataset.yaml --skip-mutation\r
mscan run-all --config configs/my_dataset.yaml --mutation-factor 0.10\r
mscan run-all --config configs/my_dataset.yaml --no-kcat\r
mscan run-all --config configs/my_dataset.yaml --no-media\r
mscan run-all --config configs/my_dataset.yaml --solver glpk --skip-reference-check\r
```\r
\r
See [docs/input_format.md](docs/input_format.md) for required columns and\r
[docs/step_by_step.md](docs/step_by_step.md) for manual step-by-step commands.\r
\r
`--mutation-factor` overrides both CCLE severe and LOF factors in the config.\r
In generic mode it changes the default, not explicit per-row mutation factors.\r
Disable reference checking when changing demo settings. Reference checks require\r
all reference samples and selected reactions, finite numeric values, and unique\r
identifiers; extra observed samples/reactions may be present.\r
\r
## Regression Tests\r
\r
```bash\r
python -m unittest discover -s tests -v\r
```\r
\r
## Step-Wise CLI\r
\r
M-SCAN can be run one step at a time:\r
\r
```bash\r
mscan prepare-rna --config configs/demo_ccle.yaml\r
mscan build-vmax --config configs/demo_ccle.yaml\r
mscan run-pfba --config configs/demo_ccle.yaml\r
mscan check-reference --config configs/demo_ccle.yaml\r
```\r
\r
For generic gene-matrix mode with a separate sample-gene mutation table:\r
\r
```bash\r
mscan prepare-rna --config configs/my_dataset.yaml\r
mscan apply-mutations --config configs/my_dataset.yaml\r
mscan build-vmax --config configs/my_dataset.yaml\r
mscan run-pfba --config configs/my_dataset.yaml\r
```\r
\r
## Configuration\r
\r
The config file is a simple `key: value` file read without extra YAML\r
dependencies. Important keys include:\r
\r
- `rna_mode`: `ccle` or `gene_matrix`.\r
- `rna`: RNA input table.\r
- `rna_layout`: CCLE-mode RNA layout, either `auto`, `sample_rows`, or\r
  `gene_rows`.\r
- `sample_column`: CCLE-mode sample/cell-line column. Defaults to\r
  `cell_line_display_name`; rowname-style `Unnamed: 0` input is also accepted.\r
- `mutation_table`: optional mutation table.\r
- `mutation_factor`: residual capacity assigned to mutation-affected genes.\r
- `use_kcat`: whether to use `data/kcat/kcat_predictions.csv`.\r
- `use_media`: whether to apply the exchange medium table.\r
- `solver`: `gurobi`, `glpk`, or `auto`.\r
- `check_reference`: should be `true` only for the bundled three-sample demo.\r
\r
M-SCAN does not run LoGoFunc internally. If LoGoFunc missense annotations are\r
available, provide them in the `final_prediction` column of the mutation table.\r
Rows with `final_prediction == LOF` are treated as deleterious when\r
`include_lof: true`.\r
\r
## GPR Rule\r
\r
M-SCAN treats isoenzymes connected by `or` as additive and protein complexes\r
connected by `and` as limited by the minimum subunit value. Mixed rules are\r
parsed recursively.\r
\r
Examples:\r
\r
- `A or B`: `A + B`\r
- `A and B`: `min(A, B)`\r
- `(A and B) or C`: `min(A, B) + C`\r
- `(A or B) and C`: `min(A + B, C)`\r
\r
## Medium\r
\r
The default medium is the Ham's F-12-derived Figure 2 benchmark medium mapped to\r
Human1/Human-GEM exchange reactions. It includes mapped Ham's F-12 components,\r
core exchange reactions (`H2O`, `O2`, `H+`), minimal feasibility\r
add-backs. Replace `data/media/cell_media.csv` or set `use_media:\r
false` to test a different medium definition.\r
\r
## Notes\r
\r
The bundled data are intentionally small so the repository can be tested\r
quickly on another machine. For full-scale runs, increase `max_samples` or set\r
it to `0` to process all sample columns in the Vmax table.\r
\r
## License\r
\r
The tutorial code in this repository is released under the MIT License.\r
\r
Included example data and third-party model/resource files remain subject to\r
their original provider licenses and terms of use.\r
\r
This repository includes copies of the following Human-GEM v1.19.0 model files:\r
\r
- `Human-GEM.xlsx`\r
- `Human-GEM.mat`\r
\r
These files are from SysBioChalmers/Human-GEM v1.19.0 and are licensed under\r
the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0).\r
\r
Source:\r
https://github.com/SysBioChalmers/Human-GEM/tree/v1.19.0\r
\r
License:\r
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\r
\r
No modifications were made to these Human-GEM files.\r
""" ;
    ns1:identifier "2314" ;
    ns1:keywords "cancer metabolism",
        "flux balance analysis",
        "genome-scale metabolic model",
        "human1",
        "kcat",
        "multi-omics",
        "pan-cancer" ;
    ns1:license "MIT" ;
