<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/2344?version=1> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:applicationSubCategory <http://edamontology.org/topic_0196>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0622> ;
    ns1:citation "" ;
    ns1:creator "Diego De Panis" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-10-16" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-10-16" ;
    ns1:description """# GEP2\r
\r
**Genome Evaluation Pipeline (v2)**\r
\r
This repository contains a significantly updated version of [GEP](https://git.imp.fu-berlin.de/begendiv/gep) that builds upon lessons learned from [ERGA](https://www.erga-biodiversity.eu/team-1/sac---sequencing-and-assembly-committee) and [GAME](https://github.com/diegomics/GAME). This pipeline is being used in the [BGE+](https://biodiversitygenomics.eu) task 5.1 “implementation of a comprehensive framework for benchmarking genome workflows, particularly those producing genome assemblies”.\r
\r
Data is entered via a simple table, and configuration is managed through a tidy control panel. GEP2 uses a modern [Snakemake](https://snakemake.readthedocs.io) version with [containers](https://apptainer.org) and can run on a server/cluster (SLURM) or a local computer.\r
\r
**Please cite:** Genome Evaluation Pipeline (GEP): A fully-automated quality control tool for parallel evaluation of genome assemblies. https://doi.org/10.1093/bioadv/vbaf147\r
\r
## GEP2 can:\r
```\r
• download assemblies & reads (or use the ones in your local storage)\r
• trim/filter/qc reads (paired-end, 10x, HiFi, ONT)\r
• classic contiguity metrics and other assembly stats (e.g., N50...)\r
• completeness metrics based on single-copy orthologs\r
• kmer-based analyses\r
• long-reads-based analysis\r
• Hi-C analysis, metrics and curation files production\r
• contamination screening\r
• aggregate results and show EBP-metrics guidance\r
```\r
\r
## How to Get and Set Up GEP2\r
\r
### 1) Get the latest version\r
\r
GEP2 is adding features rapidly, so please download the latest [release](https://github.com/diegomics/GEP2/releases) (or clone the repo for getting hot fixes faster!)\r
\r
### 2) Create the GEP2 Conda Environment\r
\r
The environment contains Snakemake packages and [NomNom](https://github.com/diegomics/NomNom). Enter the GEP folder and:\r
\r
```bash\r
conda env create -f install.yml\r
```\r
\r
### 3) Enter Your Data in a Table\r
\r
You can use Google Drive, Excel, LibreOffice, Numbers, CSV, TSV, etc.\r
\r
The table should contain these columns:\r
\r
| sp_name | asm_id | skip | asm_files | read_type | read_files |\r
|---------|--------|------|-----------|-----------|------------|\r
|         |        |      |           |           |            |\r
\r
**Please see the [example table](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1xmsstJGBo45SEQgCPncE76u51_VN5IDG9sFFafYkGfI/edit?gid=1029606022#gid=1029606022)**. **The easiest is to make a copy of that Google table** (`File`->`Make a copy`) and replace the fields with your data. Remember to change permissions (`Share`-> change `General access` to "Anyone with the link" `viewer`)\r
\r
#### Column Descriptions:\r
\r
- **sp_name**: Species name in binomial nomenclature (e.g., `Vultur gryphus`)\r
- **asm_id**: Assembly identifier (e.g., `hifiasm_l2`, `yahs_test`, or `ASM2260516v1`)\r
- **skip**: Flag assemblies for selective analysis skipping. Leave empty (or `-` or `off`) to run all analyses. Set to `on` to flag this assembly, then control which analyses to skip in `control_panel.yaml` using `SKIP_KMER`, `SKIP_INSP`, `SKIP_HIC`, etc. Useful for running quick QC on draft assemblies while running full analysis on final assemblies.\r
- **asm_files**: Full path to assembly file, URL, or accession number (e.g., `GCA_022605165.1`). If it's a link or accession, the pipeline will download the data automatically. If Pri/Alt or (Hap1/Hap2) assemblies available, add as comma-separated, like: `GCA_963854735.1, GCA_963694935.1`\r
- **read_type**: Can be `illumina`, `10x`, `hifi`, or `ont` (variations like `PacBio`, `paired-end`, `linked-read`, `arima`, `promethion` and others should also work fine)\r
- **read_files**: Comma-separated list of full paths to read files. Can also be accession numbers (e.g., `ERR12205285,ERR12205286`). For paired-end reads, list as: `forward1,reverse1,forward2,reverse2`. Also can use pattern expansion in full paths, like `/readsA/*.fq.gz, /readsB/*.fq.gz` \r
\r
\r
### 4) Configure the Control Panel\r
\r
Add the table path/address and select different options in:\r
\r
```\r
config/control_panel.yaml\r
```\r
\r
### 5) Configure Cluster or Computer Parameters\r
\r
Depending on which mode you will run, configure the respective parameters in the config file. **Important:** Don't forget to bind the folders in the _apptainer-args_ field.\r
\r
```\r
GEP2/execution/\r
├── local/\r
│   └── config.yaml\r
└── slurm/\r
    └── config.yaml\r
```\r
\r
**Note:** You can tweak per-tool resources boundaries in `GEP2/config/resources.yaml`\r
\r
### 6) Run!\r
\r
**First run takes longer** as containers need to be built.\r
\r
Load the conda environment like `conda activate GEP2_env` and in the GEP2 folder run:\r
\r
#### On HPC/Server/Cluster using [Slurm](https://slurm.schedmd.com/):\r
\r
```bash\r
nohup snakemake --profile execution/slurm &\r
```\r
> A convenient way to check which particular rules are running on the jobs is:<br>\r
> `squeue --me -S -t,i -o "%.10i %.9P %.12j %.2t %.10M %.11l %.4C %.7m %R | %k"` (or use -u $USER instead of --me).\r
\r
#### On Local Computer:\r
\r
```bash\r
nohup snakemake --profile execution/local &\r
```\r
\r
#### About the Command:\r
- `nohup` runs Snakemake in a way that won't be interrupted if you lose connection to the server/cluster\r
- The trailing `&` runs the command in the background, allowing you to continue using the terminal\r
\r
#### Dry Run (Recommended):\r
Before running the full pipeline, perform a dry run to check what will execute and catch any errors:\r
\r
```bash\r
snakemake --profile execution/slurm --dry-run\r
```\r
\r
You can also inspect:\r
- `GEP2_results/data_config.yaml`\r
- `GEP2_results/download_manifest.json`\r
\r
#### Common Troubleshooting:\r
- If your process was killed or stopped abruptly, Snakemake might complain about incomplete files when you try to run it again. We can tell Snakemake to identify and rerun those incomplete parts (and always a good idea to try a dry-run first, -n):\r
\r
```bash\r
snakemake --profile execution/local --rerun-incomplete -n\r
```\r
\r
- If Snakemake was suddenly killed, it might leave a hidden lock on your working directory to prevent other processes from overwriting files, and will tell you the directory is locked. You need to unlock it first before proceed with the run command: `snakemake --unlock`\r
- If correct output files were already created by a previously killed job, but Snakemake isn't recognising them, you can tell the pipeline to accept them without re-running by updating their timestamps using `snakemake --touch`. **Use this with caution:** double-check that those files are complete and not corrupted or empty, otherwise invalid data will propagate downstream!\r
- Some HPC systems do not allow users to keep processes running on the login node, even if those processes consume virtually no resources. This is the case for our Snakemake workflow in Slurm mode, which only submits jobs to the queue. In such cases, the pipeline can be executed from a Slurm job script, for example:\r
\r
```bash\r
#!/bin/bash\r
\r
#SBATCH -J gep2_controller\r
### Add your Slurm parameters here (partition, std out, etc.)\r
#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=1\r
#SBATCH --mem=9G\r
#SBATCH --time=5-00:00:00\r
\r
conda activate GEP2_env # or the way you use to load the conda env in a job script\r
module load apptainer   # or the way apptainer is made available on your cluster\r
\r
# Prevent common issues with Slurm environment variables and Snakemake\r
for var in $(env | grep -i "^SLURM_" | cut -d= -f1); do\r
    unset "$var"\r
done\r
\r
cd GEP2\r
snakemake --profile execution/slurm\r
```\r
\r
---\r
\r
## Results Structure\r
\r
Open the report with a markdown renderer, like [Zed](https://zed.dev), [VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com) or [MarkText](https://marktext.me) (also notice `{asm_id}_portable_md.tar.gz`, which contains the md file and all the plots to easily share the report!):\r
\r
```\r
GEP2_results/{sp_name}/{asm_id}/{asm_id}_report.md\r
```\r
\r
Also, check the _super_ report with all the assemblies in `GEP2_results/super_report.html`\r
\r
### Directory Structure:\r
\r
```\r
GEP2_results/\r
├── data/\r
│   └── {sp_name}/\r
│       └── reads/\r
│           ├── {read_type}/\r
│           │   ├── {read_symlink}\r
│           │   ├── kmer_db_k{k-mer_length}/\r
│           │   │   └── {read_name}.meryl\r
│           │   ├── logs/\r
│           │   └── processed/\r
│           │       ├── {read_type}_Path{number}_{read_name}_{process}.fq.gz\r
│           │       └── reports/\r
│           │           └── multiqc_report.html\r
│           └── ...\r
├── data_config.yaml\r
├── data_table_{hash}.csv\r
├── downloaded_data/\r
│   └── {sp_name}/\r
│       ├── assemblies/\r
│       │   └── {asm_file}\r
│       └── reads/\r
│           └── {read_type}/\r
│               └── {read_file}\r
├── download_manifest.json\r
├── super_report.html\r
└── {sp_name}/\r
    └── {asm_id}/\r
        ├── {asm_id}_report.md\r
        ├── compleasm/\r
        │   └── {asm_file_name}/\r
        │       ├── {asm_file_name}_results.tar.gz\r
        │       └── {asm_file_name}_summary.txt\r
        ├── decontamination/\r
        │   ├── fcs-gx/\r
        │   │   └── {asm_file_name}/\r
        │   │       ├── {asm_file_name}.fcs_gx_report.txt\r
        │   │       └── {asm_file_name}.taxonomy.rpt\r
        │   └── blobtools/\r
        │       └── {asm_file_name}/\r
        │           ├── Blobdir/\r
        │           ├── ..\r
        │           ├── ..blob.circle.png\r
        │           ├── ..cumulative_plot.png\r
        │           └── ..snail_plot.png\r
        ├── gfastats/\r
        │   └── {asm_file_name}_stats.txt\r
        ├── hic/\r
        │   └── {asm_file_name}/\r
        │       ├── {asm_file_name}.cool\r
        │       ├── {asm_file_name}.mcool\r
        │       ├── {asm_file_name}.pairs.gz\r
        │       ├── {asm_file_name}.pairtools_stats.txt\r
        │       ├── {asm_file_name}.pretext\r
        │       ├── {asm_file_name}_tracks.pretext\r
        │       ├── {asm_file_name}_snapshots\r
        │       │   └── {asm_file_name}_FullMap.png\r
        │       └── tracks\r
        │           └── ...bedgraph\r
        ├── inspector/\r
        │   └── {asm_file_name}/\r
        │       ├── ..\r
        │       └── summary_statistics\r
        ├── k{k-mer_length}/\r
        │   ├── {asm_id}.hist\r
        │   ├── {asm_id}.meryl\r
        │   └── genomescope2/\r
        │       └── {asm_id}_linear_plot.png\r
        ├── logs/\r
        └── merqury/\r
            ├── ..\r
            ├── {asm_file_name}.completeness.stats\r
            ├── {asm_file_name}.qv\r
            └── ...png\r
```\r
\r
### Main tools:\r
\r
| tool                                                              | doi                            | version | container                                                    |\r
|:----------------------------------------------------------------- |:------------------------------ |:------- |:------------------------------------------------------------ |\r
| [bedtools](https://github.com/arq5x/bedtools2)                    | 10.1093/bioinformatics/btq033  | 2.31.1  | docker://diegomics/hic_analysis:0.2.1                        |\r
| [blobtools](https://github.com/genomehubs/blobtoolkit)            | -                              | 4.5.5   | docker://genomehubs/blobtoolkit:4.5.5                        |\r
| [bbmap](https://sourceforge.net/projects/bbmap)                   | 10.1371/journal.pone.0185056   | 40.02   | docker://diegomics/gep2_base:0.4.2                           |\r
| [busco](https://gitlab.com/ezlab/busco)                           | 10.1093/molbev/msab199         | 6.1.0   | docker://ezlabgva/busco:v6.1.0_cv1                           |\r
| [bwa-mem2](https://github.com/bwa-mem2/bwa-mem2)                  | 10.1109/IPDPS.2019.00041       | 2.3     | docker://diegomics/hic_analysis:0.2.1                        |\r
| [chromap](https://github.com/haowenz/chromap)                     | 10.1038/s41467-021-26865-w     | 0.3.2   | docker://diegomics/hic_analysis:0.2.1                        |\r
