<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/402?version=8> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:applicationSubCategory <http://edamontology.org/topic_3172>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3173> ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.402.8" ;
    ns1:creator "Anna Niehues",
        "Casper de Visser" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2023-11-24" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description "This workflow is designed to analyze to a multi-omics data set that comprises genome-wide DNA methylation profiles, targeted metabolomics, and behavioral data of two cohorts that participated in the ACTION Biomarker Study (ACTION, Aggression in Children: Unraveling gene-environment interplay to inform Treatment and InterventiON strategies. (Boomsma 2015, Bartels 2018, Hagenbeek 2020, van Dongen 2021, Hagenbeek 2022). The ACTION-NTR cohort consists of twins that are either longitudinally concordant or discordant for childhood aggression. The ACTION-Curium-LUMC cohort consists of children referred to the Dutch LUMC Curium academic center for child and youth psychiatry. With the joint analysis of multi-omics data and behavioral data, we aim to identify substructures in the ACTION-NTR cohort and link them to aggressive behavior. First, the individuals are clustered using Similarity Network Fusion (SNF, Wang 2014), and latent feature dimensions are uncovered using different unsupervised methods including Multi-Omics Factor Analysis (MOFA) (Argelaguet 2018) and Multiple Correspondence Analysis (MCA, Lê 2008, Husson 2017). In a second step, we determine correlations between -omics and phenotype dimensions, and use them to explain the subgroups of individuals from the ACTION-NTR cohort. In order to validate the results, we project data of the ACTION-Curium-LUMC cohort onto the latent dimensions and determine if correlations between omics and phenotype data can be reproduced." ;
    ns1:featureList <http://edamontology.org/operation_3432>,
        <http://edamontology.org/operation_3935> ;
    ns1:identifier "402" ;
    ns1:keywords "behavioral data",
        "epigenomics",
        "fair",
        "metabolomics",
        "multi-omics",
        "nextflow" ;
    ns1:license "CC-BY-4.0" ;
    ns1:name "X-omics ACTIONdemonstrator analysis workflow" ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/402?version=8> ;
    ns1:version "8" .

<https://workflowhub.eu/workflows/403?version=11> a ns1:ComputationalWorkflow ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://bioschemas.org/profiles/ComputationalWorkflow/1.0-RELEASE" ;
    ns1:citation "10.48546/workflowhub.workflow.403.11" ;
    ns1:creator "Anna Syme",
        "Gareth Price",
        "Kate Farquharson",
        "Simon Tang" ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2026-04-21" ;
    ns1:dateModified "2026-05-13" ;
    ns1:description """Post-genome assembly quality control workflow using Quast, BUSCO, Meryl, Merqury and Fasta Statistics, with updates November 2024.\r
\r
Workflow inputs: reads as fastqsanger.gz (not fastq.gz), and primary assembly.fasta. (To change reads format: click on the pencil icon next to the file in the Galaxy history, then "Datatypes", then set "New type" as fastqsanger.gz). Note: the reads should be those that were used for the assembly (i.e., the filtered/cleaned reads), not the raw reads. \r
\r
What it does:\r
Computes read coverage. \r
Runs Quast. \r
Runs Fasta Statistics.\r
Runs Meryl and Merqury. \r
Runs Busco. (New default settings for BUSCO: lineage = eukaryota; for Quast: lineage = eukaryotes, genome = large.)\r
\r
Workflow outputs:\r
Reports assembly stats into a table called metrics.tsv, including selected metrics from Fasta Stats, and read coverage; reports BUSCO versions and dependencies; and displays these tables in the workflow report.\r
\r
Note: a known bug is that sometimes the workflow report text resets to default text. \r
\r
To check and restore: open the workflow in Galaxy for editing.\r
\r
Click on the "Edit Report" icon (top right, pencil icon). \r
\r
Copy and paste the following text into the workflow report, then exit this report page, then save the workflow.\r
\r
# Workflow Execution Report\r
\r
Workflow name: Genome assessment post assembly\r
\r
## Genome assembly metrics\r
\r
Selected statistics from the workflow outputs. Additional metrics are available in other outputs in the history.\r
\r
```galaxy\r
history_dataset_display(output="Genome assembly metrics")\r
```\r
## Software\r
\r
Busco version and dependencies:\r
\r
```galaxy\r
history_dataset_display(output="Busco and dependencies version")\r
```\r
## Galaxy Australia\r
\r
Thanks for using Galaxy! When you use Galaxy Australia to support your publication or project, please acknowledge its use with the following statement: "This work is supported by Galaxy Australia, a service provided by the Australian Biocommons and its partners. The service receives NCRIS funding through Bioplatforms Australia and the Australian Research Data Commons (https://doi.org/10.47486/PL105), as well as The University of Melbourne and Queensland Government RICF funding."\r
""" ;
    ns1:hasPart "busco",
